 from cookies (3 cookies for $1)\ncookies_revenue = (cookies_sold // 3) * 1\n\n# Calculate revenue from cupcakes ($2 each)\ncupcakes_revenue = cupcakes_sold * 2\n\n# Calculate revenue from brownies ($1 each)\nbrownies_revenue = brownies_sold * 1\n\n# Calculate total revenue\ntotal_revenue = cookies_revenue + cupcakes_revenue + brownies_revenue\n\n# Calculate profit\nprofit = total_revenue - cost_of_baking\nprint(profit)\n```\n```output\n78\n```\nThe profit made by the math club from the bake sale is \\(\\boxed{78}\\).", "A $10$-foot by $10$-foot square yard has a circular fire pit in the center, and a square patio is inscribed in the fire pit. The distance from the edge of the fire pit to the fire pit is $2$ feet. The distance from the fire pit to the edge of the patio is $1$ foot. If the fire pit is $1$ foot deep, what is the volume of the fire pit? (Round to the nearest hundredth, if necessary.) \\boxed{ } \\text{ft}^3 \\boxed{ } \\text{ft}^3 \\boxed{ } \\text{ft}^3 \\boxed{ } \\text{ft}^3 \\boxed{ } \\text{ft}^3 \\boxed{ } \\text{ft}^3\nA math club is having a bake sale as a fundraiser to raise money for an upcoming trip. They sell $54$ cookies at three for $\\$1$, and $20$ cupcakes at $\\$2$ each, and $35$ brownies at $\\$1$ each. If it cost the math club $\\$15$ to bake these items, what was their profit? \\boxed{ } \\text{dollars} \\boxed{ } \\text{dollars} \\boxed{ } \\text{dollars} \\boxed{ } \\text{dollars} \\boxed{ } \\text{dollars} \\boxed{ } \\text{dollars}\nA $10$-foot by $10$-foot square yard has a circular fire pit in the center, and a square patio is inscribed in the fire pit. The distance from the edge of the fire pit to the fire pit is $2$ feet. The distance from the fire pit to the edge of the patio is $1$ foot. If the fire pit is $1$ foot deep, what is the volume of the fire pit? (Round to the nearest hundredth, if necessary.) \\boxed{ } \\text{ft}^3 \\boxed{ } \\text{ft}^3 \\boxed{ } \\text{ft}^3 \\boxed{ } \\text{ft}^3 \\boxed{ } \\text{ft}^3 \\boxed{ } \\text{ft}^3\n\nAssistant: To solve the problem, we need to calculate the profit from the bake sale. Here are the steps:\n\n1. Calculate the revenue from selling cookies.\n2. Calculate the revenue from selling cupcakes.\n3. Calculate the revenue from selling brownies.\n4. Sum the revenues to get the total revenue.\n5. Subtract the cost of baking the items from the total revenue to get the profit.\n\nLet's break it down:\n\n1. The math club sells 54 cookies at three for $1. So, the number of sets of three cookies sold is \\( \\frac{54}{3} = 18 \\). The revenue from cookies is \\( 18 \\times 1 = 18 \\) dollars.\n2. The math club sells 20 cupcakes at $2 each. So, the revenue from cupcakes is \\( 20 \\times 2 = 40 \\) dollars.\n3. The math club sells 35 brownies at $1 each. So, the revenue from brownies is \\( 35 \\times 1 = 35 \\) dollars.\n4. The total revenue is \\( 18 + 40 + 35 = 93 \\) dollars.\n5. The cost of baking the items is $15. So, the profit is \\( 93 - 15 = 78 \\) dollars.\n\nLet's write the Python code to verify this calculation.\n```python\n# Calculate the revenue from selling cookies\ncookies_sold = 54\ncookies_price_per_set = 1\ncookies_set_size = 3\ncookies_revenue = (cookies_sold // cookies_set_size) * cookies_price_per_set\n\n# Calculate the revenue from selling cupcakes\ncupcakes_sold = 20\ncupcakes_price_per_unit = 2\ncupcakes_revenue = cupcakes_sold * cupcakes_price_per_unit\n\n# Calculate the revenue from selling brownies\nbrownies_sold = 35\nbrownies_price_per_unit = 1\nbrownies_revenue = brownies_sold * brownies_price_per_unit\n\n# Calculate the total revenue\ntotal_revenue = cookies_revenue + cupcakes_revenue + brownies_revenue\n\n# Calculate the cost of baking the items\nbaking_cost = 15\n\n# Calculate the profit\nprofit = total_revenue - baking_cost\nprint(profit)\n```\n```output\n78\n```\nThe profit from the bake sale is \\(\\boxed{78}\\) dollars.", 'Let $z$ be the complex number $7 + 2i.$  Then the complex number $z$ can be repr