\arctan(3) \\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[ -\\theta = \\arctan(3) + \\arctan\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\]\n\nSince $\\arctan(3) + \\arctan\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, we get:\n\n\\[ -\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\]\n\nSo, $\\theta = -\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Since we are looking for a positive angle in the range $0 < \\theta < 90$, we take the positive equivalent, which is $\\theta = 90^\\circ - \\frac{\\pi}{2} = 90^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 45^\\circ$.\n\n### Problem 3: Finding $\\theta$ for the Rotation of $3 - 4i$\n\nSimilarly, we can find the angle $\\theta$ for the rotation of $3 - 4i$ to $4 + 3i$. The argument of $3 - 4i$ is $\\arctan\\left(\\frac{-4}{3}\\right) + 2\\pi = 2\\pi - \\arctan\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)$. The argument of $4 + 3i$ is $\\arctan\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)$. The difference in arguments is $\\theta$.\n\nSo, we need to solve:\n\n\\[ 2\\pi - \\arctan\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) - \\theta = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[ -\\theta = \\arctan\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) + \\arctan\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) \\]\n\nSince $\\arctan\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) + \\arctan\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right) = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, we get:\n\n\\[ -\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\]\n\nSo, $\\theta = -\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. Since we are looking for a positive angle in the range $0 < \\theta < 90$, we take the positive equivalent, which is $\\theta = 90^\\circ - \\frac{\\pi}{2} = 90^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 45^\\circ$.\n\n### Final Answer\n\nLet's summarize the results:\n\n1. The resulting complex number after a $90^\\circ$ rotation of", 'Find the complex number corresponding to the point one-third of the way from $2 + 3i$ to $-4 - i$ on the line segment connecting these points. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \