\nFrom this, we can set up the following equations:\n\\[\n\\frac{5 + x}{2} = \\frac{13}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{3 + y}{2} = 6\n\\]\n\nSolving these equations will give us the values of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).\n\nLet's solve the equations step-by-step using Python.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\nx, y = symbols('x y')\r\n\r\n# Define the equations based on the midpoint formula\r\neq1 = Eq((5 + x) / 2, 13 / 2)\r\neq2 = Eq((3 + y) / 2, 6)\r\n\r\n# Solve the equations\r\nsolution = solve((eq1, eq2), (x, y))\r\nprint(solution)\n```\n```output\n{x: 8.00000000000000, y: 9.00000000000000}\n```\nThe solution to the equations is \\(x = 8\\) and \\(y = 9\\). Therefore, the coordinates of the fourth vertex are \\((8, 9)\\).\n\nThe value of \\(x + y\\) is \\(8 + 9 = 17\\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{17}\n\\]", "The given coordinates of the parallelogram are \\((5, 3)\\), \\((6, 8)\\), \\((7, 4)\\), and \\((x, y)\\) with \\(x > 7\\). To find the coordinates \\((x, y)\\), we can use the property that the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other. This means the midpoint of one diagonal is equal to the midpoint of the other diagonal.\n\nLet's denote the coordinates of the parallelogram as \\(A(5, 3)\\), \\(B(6, 8)\\), \\(C(7, 4)\\), and \\(D(x, y)\\). The midpoint of diagonal \\(AC\\) should be equal to the midpoint of diagonal \\(BD\\).\n\nThe midpoint of \\(AC\\) is:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{5 + 7}{2}, \\frac{3 + 4}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{12}{2}, \\frac{7}{2} \\right) = \\left( 6, \\frac{7}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nThe midpoint of \\(BD\\) is:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 + x}{2}, \\frac{8 + y}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSince these midpoints are equal, we have:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 + x}{2}, \\frac{8 + y}{2} \\right) = \\left( 6, \\frac{7}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two equations:\n\\[\n\\frac{6 + x}{2} = 6 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{8 + y}{2} = \\frac{7}{2}\n\\]\n\nSolving the first equation:\n\\[\n6 + x = 12 \\impli