qrt{3}}{2} \\) or \\( y = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\).\n\n   Since \\( y = z^2 \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   z^2 = \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\quad \\text{or} \\quad z^2 = \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n   \\]\n   The solutions to these equations are the square roots of \\( \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) and \\( \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\).\n\n2. **Express the roots in polar form:**\n\n   The complex numbers \\( \\frac{-1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) and \\( \\frac{-1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\) can be written in polar form. Both have a magnitude of 1 and arguments of \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\) and \\( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\), respectively. The square roots of these complex numbers will have arguments of \\( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\), \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\), \\( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\), and \\( \\frac{5\\pi}{3} \\).\n\n3. **Determine the smallest \\( n \\) such that all roots are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity:**\n\n   The \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity are the solutions to the equation \\( z^n = 1 \\). The arguments of the roots of unity are \\( \\frac{2k\\pi}{n} \\) for \\( k = 0, 1, 2, \\ldots, n-1 \\). The arguments of our roots are \\( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\), \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\), \\( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\), and \\( \\frac{5\\pi}{3} \\). The smallest \\( n \\) that can accommodate all these arguments is 12, because \\( \\frac{12\\pi}{12} = \\pi \\), and the multiples of \\( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\) are \\( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\), \\( \\frac{2\\pi}{3} \\), \\( \\pi \\), \\( \\frac{4\\pi}{3} \\), \\( \\frac{5\\pi}{3} \\), and \\( 2\\pi \\).\n\nSo, the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that all the roots of \\( z^4 + z^2 + 1 = 0 \\) are \\( n^{\\text{th}} \\) roots of unity is \\( \\boxed{12} \\).\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport cmath\r\nimport numpy as np\r\n\r\n# Define the polynomial coefficients\r\ncoefficients = [1, 0, 1, 0, 1]\r\n\r\n# Find the roots of the polynomial\r\nroots = np.roots(coefficients)\r\n\r\n# Check the argumen