ed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed{} \\boxed', "To find the vertical asymptotes of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{2x}{x^2 - 5x - 14} \\), we need to determine where the denominator is equal to zero. The vertical asymptotes occur at the values of \\( x \\) that make the denominator zero, provided they do not also make the numerator zero (which would indicate a removable discontinuity rather than a vertical asymptote).\n\nThe denominator of the function is \\( x^2 - 5x - 14 \\). We can factor this quadratic expression as follows:\n\\[ x^2 - 5x - 14 = (x - 7)(x + 2). \\]\nSetting each factor to zero, we find the vertical asymptotes at \\( x = 7 \\) and \\( x = -2 \\). So, \\( a = 7 \\) and \\( b = -2 \\).\n\nNext, we need to find the horizontal asymptote. The horizontal asymptote of a rational function can be determined by comparing the degrees of the numerator and the denominator. Since the degree of the numerator (1) is less than the degree of the denominator (2), the horizontal asymptote is \\( y = 0 \\). So, \\( c = 0 \\).\n\nNow, we can find \\( a + b + c \\):\n\\[ a + b + c = 7 + (-2) + 0 = 5. \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the function to find the roots of the denominator\r\nfrom sympy import symbols, solve\r\n\r\n# Define the variable\r\nx = symbols('x')\r\n\r\n# Define the denominator\r\ndenominator = x**2 - 5*x - 14\r\n\r\n# Solve for the roots of the denominator\r\nvertical_asymptotes = solve(denominator, x)\r\n\r\n# The horizontal asymptote is y = 0 since the degree of the numerator is less than the degree of the denominator\r\nhorizontal_asymptote = 0\r\n\r\n# Calculate a + b + c\r\na, b = vertical_asymptotes\r\nc = horizontal_asymptote\r\nresult = a + b + c\r\nprint(result.evalf())\n```\n```output\n5.00000000000000\n```\nThe value of \\( a + b + c \\) is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).", 'The graph of $f(x)=\\frac{3x}{x^2-4x+4}$ has vertical asymptotes $x=p$ and $x=q$, and horizontal asymptote $y=r$.  Find $p+q+r$.  \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\