 the denominator (2), the horizontal asymptote is \\(y = 0\\).\n\n3. **Sum of Asymptotes:**\n   Once we find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) (the roots of the quadratic equation), and \\(c\\) (which is 0), we can find \\(a + b + c\\).\n\nLet's proceed with the calculations in Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and the function\nx = sp.symbols('x')\nnumerator = 2*x\ndenominator = x**2 - 5*x - 14\n\n# Solve the denominator for vertical asymptotes\nvertical_asymptotes = sp.solve(denominator, x)\na, b = vertical_asymptotes\n\n# Horizontal asymptote\nhorizontal_asymptote = 0  # Since the degree of numerator < degree of denominator\n\n# Sum of a, b, and c\nsum_asymptotes = a + b + horizontal_asymptote\nprint(sum_asymptotes.evalf())\n```\n```output\n5.00000000000000\n```\nThe sum of the vertical asymptotes \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) and the horizontal asymptote \\(c\\) is:\n\n\\[\na + b + c = 5\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{5}\n\\]", 'The graph of $f(x)=\\frac{2x}{x^2-5x-14}$ has vertical asymptotes $x=a$ and $x=b$, and horizontal asymptote $y=c$.  Find $a+b+c$.  (This problem is identical to the previous one.  The "ansomuser" and "ansomassistant" account was created by a user who was trying to get a head start on the MathCounts Trainer problems and needed a username.  The user was also using multiple accounts to get a head start on the problems and was trying to get a head start on the problems.  The user was also using multiple accounts to get a head start on the problems and was trying to get a head start on the problems.  The user was also using multiple accounts to get a head start on the problems and was trying to get a head start on the problems.  The user was also using multiple accounts to get a head start on the problems and was trying to get a head start on the problems.  The user was also using multiple accounts to get a head start on the problems and was trying to get a head start on the problems.