heck \\(38\\):\n\n\\[\n38^2 = 1444\n\\]\n\nThe digits of 1444 cannot be rearranged to form 38.\n\nNext, we check \\(39\\):\n\n\\[\n39^2 = 1521\n\\]\n\nThe digits of 1521 cannot be rearranged to form 39.\n\nNext, we check \\(40\\):\n\n\\[\n40^2 = 1600\n\\]\n\nThe digits of 1600 cannot be rearranged to form 40.\n\nNext, we check \\(41\\):\n\n\\[\n41^2 = 1681\n\\]\n\nThe digits of 1681 cannot be rearranged to form 41.\n\nNext, we check \\(42\\):\n\n\\[\n42^2 = 1764\n\\]\n\nThe digits of 1764 cannot be rearranged to form 42.\n\nNext, we check \\(43\\):\n\n\\[\n43^2 = 1849\n\\]\n\nThe digits of 1849 cannot be rearranged to form 43.\n\nNext, we check \\(44\\):\n\n\\[\n44^2 = 1936\n\\]\n\nThe digits of 1936 can be rearranged to form 44 (1936 \\(\\rightarrow\\) 1936 \\(\\rightarrow\\) 44).\n\nThus, the next larger integer \\(N\\) with this peculiarity is \\(\\boxed{44}\\).\n\n$$\\text{Problem 3}$$\n\nWe need to determine the number of different ways the digits 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 can be arranged to form a nine-digit number where the number formed by the first \\(n\\) digits is divisible by \\(n\\) for \\(n = 1, 2, 3, \\ldots, 9\\).\n\nThis is a well-known problem in combinatorial number theory, and the number of such arrangements is 0. This is because it is impossible to arrange the digits 1 through 9 in such a way that the number formed by the first \\(n\\) digits is divisible by \\(n\\) for all \\(n\\) from 1 to 9.\n\nThus, the number of different ways to arrange the digits is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).', "The sum of $3x+12$ and $9x+11$ is $105$. What is the value of $x$? Shay's age is $x$. In $2$ years, she will be $14$ years old. What is the value of $x$? Shay's age is $x$. In $2$ years, she will be $14$ years old. What is the value of $x$? Shay's age is $x$. In $2$ years, she will be $14$ years old. What is the value of $x$? Shay's age is $x$. In $2$ years, she will be $14$ years old. What is the value of $x$? Shay's age is $x$. In $2$ years, she will be $14$ years old. W