} = \\frac{1}{5}\\). Since \\(x = \\sin(\\alpha)\\) and \\(\\alpha \\in (0, \\frac{\\pi}{4})\\), we have \\(0 <", "To determine the total number of possible outfits, we can use the basic principle of counting. Specifically, if you have $m$ ways to do one thing and $n$ ways to do another, then there are $m \\times n$ ways to do both.\n\nIn this case, we have 5 shirts, 6 pairs of pants, and 8 hats. So the total number of possible outfits is the product of these numbers:\n\n\\[ \\text{Total outfits} = 5 \\times 6 \\times 8 \\]\n\nLet's calculate this using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Number of shirts, pants, and hats\nshirts = 5\npants = 6\nhats = 8\n\n# Total number of possible outfits\ntotal_outfits = shirts * pants * hats\nprint(total_outfits)\n```\n```output\n240\n```\nThe total number of possible outfits consisting of one shirt, one pair of pants, and one hat is \\(\\boxed{240}\\).", "To determine the total number of outfits that can be made from 5 shirts, 6 pairs of pants, and 8 hats, we need to calculate the product of the number of choices for each item of clothing. This is because each item of clothing can be chosen independently of the others.\n\nThe total number of outfits is given by:\n\\[ \\text{Total outfits} = (\\text{Number of shirts}) \\times (\\text{Number of pairs of pants}) \\times (\\text{Number of hats}) \\]\n\nLet's calculate this using Python.\n```python\n# Number of shirts\nnum_shirts = 5\n\n# Number of pairs of pants\nnum_pants = 6\n\n# Number of hats\nnum_hats = 8\n\n# Total number of outfits\ntotal_outfits = num_shirts * num_pants * num_hats\nprint(total_outfits)\n```\n```output\n240\n```\nThe total number of outfits that can be made consisting of one shirt, one pair of pants, and one hat is \\(\\boxed{240}\\).", "To determine the total number of possible outfits that can be made from 5 shirts, 6 pairs of pants, and 8 hats, we need to calculate the product of these three numbers. This is because each shirt can be paired with eac