lution.\n\n2. For \\(x = -\\frac{1}{3}\\):\n\\[\n|5\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right) - 1| = -\\frac{1}{3} + 3 \\implies \\left|-\\frac{5}{3} - 1\\right| = \\frac{8}{3} \\implies \\left|-\\frac{8}{3}\\right| = \\frac{8}{3} \\implies \\frac{8}{3} = \\frac{8}{3}\n\\]\nThis is true, so \\(x = -\\frac{1}{3}\\) is also a valid solution.\n\nSince we are asked to find the largest value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation, the answer is \\(x = 1\\).\n\nLet's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\n# Define the function to check the solutions\r\ndef check_solution(x):\r\n    return abs(5 * x - 1) == x + 3\r\n\r\n# Solutions from the two cases\r\nsolutions = [1, -1/3]\r\n\r\n# Check which solutions satisfy the original equation\r\nvalid_solutions = [x for x in solutions if check_solution(x)]\r\n\r\n# Find the largest valid solution\r\nlargest_solution = max(valid_solutions)\r\nprint(largest_solution)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nThe largest value of \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation \\(|5x - 1| = x + 3\\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).", "To determine the largest value of \\(a\\) such that the graph of \\(y = f(x)\\) intersects every horizontal line at least once, we need to analyze the behavior of the function \\(f(x)\\). The function \\(f(x)\\) is defined piecewise as follows:\n\n\\[\nf(x) =\n\\begin{cases}\nax^2 & \\text{if } x \\geq a,\\\\\nax + 2a & \\text{if } x < a.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nTo ensure that the graph of \\(y = f(x)\\) intersects every horizontal line at least once, the function \\(f(x)\\) must be surjective, meaning it must take on all real values. This requires that the function \\(f(x)\\) covers the entire range of real numbers.\n\n1. **Analyze the first piece \\(f(x) = ax^2\\) for \\(x \\geq a\\):**\n\n   - If \\(a > 0\\), the function \\(ax^2\\) is a parabola opening upwards, and for \\(x \\geq a\\), the minimum value of \\(ax^2\\) is \\(a \\cdot a^2 = a^3\\). So, the range of \\(ax^2\\) for \\(x \\geq a\\) is \\([a^3, \\infty)\\).\n   - If \\(a < 0\\), the function \\(ax^2\\) is a parabola opening downwards, and for \\(x \\geq a\\), the maximum value of \\(ax^2\\) is \\(a \\cdot a^2 = a^3\\). So, the range of \\(ax^2\\) for \\(x \\geq a\\) is \\((-\\infty, a^3]\\).\n   - If \\(a = 0\\), the function \\(ax^2\\) is identically zero, and the range is \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\n2. **Analyze the second piece \\(f(x) = ax + 2a\\) for \\(x < a\\):**\n\n   - If \\(a > 0\\), the function \\(ax + 2a\\) is a line with positive slope. As \\(x\\) ranges from \\(-\\infty\\) to \\(a\\), the value of \\(ax + 2a\\) ranges from \\(-\\infty\\) to \\(a^2 + 2a\\).\n   - If \\(a < 0\\), the function \\(ax + 2a\\) is a line with negative slope. As \\(x\\) ranges from \\(-\\infty\\) to \\(a\\), the value of \\(ax + 2a\\) ranges from \\(\\infty\\) to \\(a^2 + 2a\\).\n   - If \\(a = 0\\), the function \\(ax + 2a\\) is identically zero, and the range is \\(\\{0\\}\\).\n\nTo ensure that \\(f(x)\\) covers all real numbers, we need the ranges of the two pieces to overlap in such a way that there are no gaps. This means that the minimum value of the second piece must be less than or equal to the maximum value of the first piece, and vice versa.\n\nLet's consider the case where \\(a > 0\\):\n\n- The range of \\(ax^2\\) for \\(x \\geq a\\) is \\([a^3, \\infty)\\).\n- The range of \\(ax + 2a\\) for \\(x < a\\) is \\((-\\infty, a^2 + 2a]\\).\n\nFor the function to cover all real numbers, we need \\(a^3 \\leq a^2 + 2a\\). Simplifying this inequality, we get:\n\n\\[a^3 \\leq a^2 + 2a\\]\n\\[a^3 - a^2 - 2a \\leq 0\\]\n\\[a(a^2 - a - 2) \\leq 0\\]\n\\[a(a - 2)(a + 1) \\leq 0\\]\n\nSince \\(a > 0\\), we only consider the positive roots. The inequality \\(a(a - 2)(a + 1) \\leq 0\\) is satisfied when \\(0 < a \\leq 2\\).\n\nTherefore, the largest value of \\(a\\) such that the graph of \\(y = f(x)\\) intersects every horizontal line at least once is \\(a = 2\\).\n\nLet's verify this using Python code to ensure the accuracy of the result.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable and the inequality\na = sp.symbols('a', real=True, positive=True)\ninequality = a * (a - 2) * (a + 1) <= 0\n\n# Solve the inequality\nsolution = sp.solve(inequality, a)\nprint(solution)\n``", "To determine the largest value of \\(a\\) such that the graph of \\(y = f(x)\\) intersects every horizontal line at least once, we need to analyze the function \\(f(x)\\) and its behavior.\n\nThe function \\(f(x)\\) is defined piecewise:\n\\[ f(x) =\n\\begin{cases}\nax^2 & \\text{if } x \\geq a,\\\\\nax + 2a & \\text{if } x < a.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFor the function \\(f(x)\\) to intersect every horizontal line at least once, it must cover all possible \\(y\\)-values. This means that the range of \\(f(x)\\) must be all real numbers.\n\nLet's analyze the two pieces of the function separately:\n\n1. For \\(x \\geq a\\), the function is \\(f(x) = ax^2\\). This is a parabola that opens upwards if \\(a > 0\\) and downwards if \\(a < 0\\).\n    - If \\(a > 0\\), the minimum value of \\(ax^2\\) for \\(x \\geq a\\) is \\(a \\cdot a^2 = a^3\\).\n    - If \\(a < 0\\), the function \\(ax^2\\) will not cover all negative \\(y\\)-values because it will only cover values from \\(a^3\\) upwards.\n\n2. For \\(x < a\\), the function is \\(f(x) = ax + 2a\\). This is a line with slope \\(a\\).\n    - If \\(a > 0\\), the function \\(ax + 2a\\) will cover all \\(y\\)-values from \\(-\\infty\\) up to \\(a^2 + 2a\\) (which is the value of the function at \\(x = a\\)).\n    - If \\(a < 0\\), the function \\(ax + 2a\\) will cover all \\(y\\)-values from \\(a^2 + 2a\\) down to \\(-\\infty\\).\n\nTo ensure that \\(f(x)\\) covers all \\(y\\)-values, we need to check the continuity at \\(x = a\\). The value of the function at \\(x = a\\) from the left side (using the linear part) is \\(a \\cdot a + 2a = a^2 + 2a\\), and from the right side (using the quadratic part) is \\(a \\cdot a^2 = a^3\\). For the function to be continuous and cover all \\(y\\)-values, we need \\(a^3 \\leq a^2 + 