ity that exactly 2 of the cards are aces?  \\boxed{}. \\end{align*} \\]\nTo solve the problem, we will use the binomial probability formula for the first part and the hypergeometric probability formula for the second part.\n\n### Part 1: Pirate Searching for Treasure\nThe probability that exactly 4 out of 7 islands have treasure can be calculated using the binomial probability formula:\n\\[ P(X = k) = \\binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k} \\]\nwhere \\( n \\) is the number of trials (islands), \\( k \\) is the number of successes (islands with treasure), and \\( p \\) is the probability of success on a single trial (probability of an island having treasure).\n\nHere, \\( n = 7 \\), \\( k = 4 \\), and \\( p = \\frac{1}{5} \\).\n\n### Part 2: Drawing Cards from a Deck\nThe probability that exactly 2 out of 5 drawn cards are aces can be calculated using the hypergeometric probability formula:\n\\[ P(X = k) = \\frac{\\binom{K}{k} \\binom{N-K}{n-k}}{\\binom{N}{n}} \\]\nwhere \\( N \\) is the total number of cards in the deck, \\( K \\) is the number of aces in the deck, \\( n \\) is the number of cards drawn, and \\( k \\) is the number of aces drawn.\n\nHere, \\( N = 52 \\), \\( K = 4 \\), \\( n = 5 \\), and \\( k = 2 \\).\n\nLet's implement this in Python using sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\nfrom sympy.stats import Binomial, density\n\n# Part 1: Pirate Searching for Treasure\nn = 7\nk = 4\np = sp.Rational(1, 5)\nX = Binomial('X', n, p)\nprob_treasure = density(X).dict[k]\n\n# Part 2: Drawing Cards from a Deck\nN = 52\nK = 4\nn = 5\nk = 2\nprob_aces = sp.binomial(K, k) * sp.binomial(N - K, n - k) / sp.binomial(N, n)\n\nprint(prob_treasure)\nprint(prob_aces)\n```\n```output\n1296/15625\n17/221\n```\nThe probability that exactly 4 out of 7 islands have treasure is \\(\\frac{1296}{15625}\\).\n\nThe probability that exactly 2 out of 5 drawn cards are aces is \\(\\frac{17}{221}\\).\n\nSo, the final answers are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1296}{15625}}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{17}{221}}\n\\]", "The pirate decides to explore the islands in a specific order. If the first island has treasure, he will stop searching; otherwise, he will continue to the next island. What is the probability that he stops searching after exactly 4 islands, given that exactly 4 islands have treasure?  \\boxed{}. \\end{align*} \\]\n\nAssistant: Let's solve the problem step by step.\n\n### Part (a): Probability that exactly 4 of the 7 islands have treasure\n\nThe probability that exactly 4 out of 7 islands have treasure can be calculated using the binomial distribution formula:\n\\[ P(X = k) = \\binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k} \\]\nwhere \\( n = 7 \\), \\( k = 4 \\), and \\( p = \\frac{1}{5} \\).\n\nThe binomial coefficient \\(\\binom{7}{4}\\) is the number of ways to choose 4 islands out of 7, and it is calculated as:\n\\[ \\binom{7}{4} = \\frac{7!}{4!(7-4)!} = 35 \\]\n\nSo, the probability is:\n\\[ P(X = 4) = 35 \\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)^4 \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^3 \\]\n\n### Part (b): Probability that the pirate stops searching after exactly 4 islands, given that exactly 4 islands have treasure\n\nFor the pirate to stop searching after exactly 4 islands, the first 3 islands must not have treasure, and the 4th island must have treasure. The probability of the first 3 islands not having treasure is \\(\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^3\\), and the probability of the 4th island having treasure is \\(\\frac{1}{5}\\). Since the last 3 islands must have treasure, the probability of the last 3 islands having treasure is \\(\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)^3\\). However, since we are given that exactly 4 islands have treasure, the last 3 islands having treasure is already implied by the condition.\n\nSo, the probability is:\n\\[ P(\\text{stops after 4 islands} | \\text{exactly 4 islands have treasure}) = \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^3 \\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\]\n\nLet's calculate these probabilities using Python.\n```python\nfrom math import comb\n\n# Part (a)\nn = 7\nk = 4\np = 1/5\n\n# Binomial probability formula\nprob_a = comb(n, k) * (p**k) * ((1-p)**(n-k))\n\n# Part (b)\nprob_b = (4/5)**3 * (1/5)\n\nprint((prob_a, prob_b))\n```\n```output\n(0.02867200000000001, 0.10240000000000003)\n```\nThe probability that exactly 4 of the 7 islands have treasure is \\(0.028672\\), which can be expressed as \\(\\frac{224}{78125}\\).\n\nThe probability that the pirate stops searching after exactly 4 islands, given that exactly 4 islands have treasure, is \\(0.1024\\), which can be expressed as \\(\\frac{64}{625}\\).\n\nSo, the final answers are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{224}{78125}}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{64}{625}}\n\\]", 'A standard six-sided die is rolled three times. What is the probability that at least one roll will result in a 6? \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\