 labeled as \\(x\\), \\(y\\), and \\(z\\).\n\nWe need to find the measure of \\(\\angle AFD\\).\n\nLet's start by examining the trisected angles:\n- \\(\\angle BAD\\) is trisected into three equal angles, so each angle is \\(x\\).\n- \\(\\angle CDA\\) is trisected into three equal angles, so each angle is \\(y\\).\n\nSince \\(ABCD\\) is a quadrilateral, the sum of its interior angles is \\(360^\\circ\\):\n\\[\n\\angle BAD + \\angle ABC + \\angle BCD + \\angle CDA = 360^\\circ\n\\]\n\nGiven that:\n\\[\n\\angle BAD = 3x \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\angle CDA = 3y\n\\]\n\nWe can write the equation for the sum of the angles:\n\\[\n3x + \\angle ABC + \\angle BCD + 3y = 360^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\angle ABC\\) and \\(\\angle BCD\\) are not given directly, we need to use the given angles:\n\\[\n\\angle ABC = 110^\\circ \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\angle BCD = 100^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these values into the equation:\n\\[\n3x + 110^\\circ + 100^\\circ + 3y = 360^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSimplifying:\n\\[\n3x + 3y + 210^\\circ = 360^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\\[\n3x + 3y = 150^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\nx + y = 50^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(\\angle AFD\\). Since \\(F\\) is a point on the trisected lines, we can use the fact that the sum of the angles around point \\(F\\) should be \\(360^\\circ\\).\n\nLet's denote the angles around \\(F\\) as follows:\n- \\(\\angle AFD = \\alpha\\)\n- \\(\\angle BFC = \\beta\\)\n- \\(\\angle CFD = \\gamma\\)\n- \\(\\angle AFE = \\delta\\)\n\nWe know that:\n\\[\n\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma + \\delta = 360^\\circ\n\\]\n\nGiven the symmetry and the trisected angles, we can deduce that:\n\\[\n\\alpha = \\beta = \\gamma = \\delta\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\n4\\alpha = 360^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha = 90^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of \\(\\angle AFD\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{90^\\circ}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given angles and trisected angles\r\nangle_BAD = 110  # in degrees\r\n