l rotation (360 degrees) is 90 degrees. So, 0.25 of a rotation is:\n   \\[\n   0.25 \\times 360 = 90 \\text{ degrees}\n   \\]\n   Spinning 90 degrees to the right from north will bring the skater to face east.\n\nTherefore, after spinning 2250 degrees to her right, the figure skater will be facing \\boxed{\\text{east}}.', 'To determine the direction the figure skater is facing after spinning 2250 degrees to her right, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the initial position:**\n   - The skater starts facing north.\n\n2. **Convert the total degrees of rotation into full circles:**\n   - A full circle is 360 degrees.\n   - We need to find out how many full circles are in 2250 degrees.\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2250 \\text{ degrees}}{360 \\text{ degrees/circle}} = 6.25 \\text{ circles}\n   \\]\n   This means the skater completes 6 full circles and has an additional 0.25 of a circle.\n\n3. **Calculate the remaining degrees after completing full circles:**\n   - To find the remaining degrees, multiply the decimal part by 360 degrees.\n   \\[\n   0.25 \\times 360 \\text{ degrees} = 90 \\text{ degrees}\n   \\]\n   So, after spinning 6 full circles, the skater has an additional 90 degrees to spin.\n\n4. **Determine the direction after the remaining degrees:**\n   - Starting from north, spinning 90 degrees to the right means the skater will face east.\n\nTherefore, the direction the figure skater is facing when she finishes her spin is \\boxed{\\text{east}}.', 'To determine the direction the figure skater is facing after spinning 2250 degrees to her right, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the problem**: The skater starts facing north and spins 2250 degrees to the right. We need to find out which direction she is facing after the spin.\n\n2. **Normalize the angle**: Since a full circle is 360 degrees, we can reduce the angle by finding the equivalent angle within one full rotation. We do this by dividing 2250 by 360 and finding the remainder.\n\n   \\[\n   2250 \\div 360 = 6.25\n   \\]\n\n   The quotient is 6, which means the skater completes 6 full rotations. The remainder is:\n\n   \\[\n   2250 - (6 \\times 360) = 2250 - 2160 = 90\n   \\]\n\n   So, 2250 degrees is equivalent to 90 degrees.\n\n3. **Determine the direction**: Now, we need to figure out which direction the skater is facing after spinning 90 degrees to the right from north. Spinning 90 degrees to the right from north will point her to the east.\n\nTherefore, the skater is facing \\boxed{\\text{east}} when she finishes her spin.', "To solve this problem, we need to determine the direction the figure skater is facing after spinning 2250 degrees. We know that a full spin is 360 degrees. \n\nFirst, let's find out how many full spins the skater completes and how many degrees are left over after the full spins.\n\n1. Divide the total degrees by 360 to find the number of full spins:\n   $$ \\frac{2250}{360} = 6.25 $$\n\n   This means the skater completes 6 full spins and has 2250 - (6 × 360) = 2250 - 2160 = 90 degrees left.\n\n2. Determine the direction after 90 degrees:\n   - 0 to 90 degrees: North to East\n   - 90 degrees: East\n   - 90 to 180 degrees: East to South\n   - 180 degrees: South\n   - 180 to 270 degrees: South to West\n   - 270 degrees: West\n   - 270 to 360 degrees: West to North\n\n   Since 90 degrees is directly to the right of the starting point (north), the skater is facing East after spinning 2250 degrees.\n\nTherefore, the figure skater is facing East when she finishes her spin.", 'To determine the direction the figure skater is facing after spinning 2250 degrees to her right, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the problem**: The skater starts facing north and spins 2250 degrees to her right. We need to find out which cardinal direction she is facing after this spin.\n\n2. **Normalize the angle**: Since a full rotation is 360 degrees, we can reduce the angle by finding the equivalent angle within one full rotation. We do this by dividing 2250 by 360 and finding the remainder.\n\n   \\[\n   2250 \\div 360 = 6.25\n   \\]\n\n   The quotient is 6, which means the skater completes 6 full rotations. The remainder is:\n\n   \\[\n   2250 - (6 \\times 360) = 2250 - 2160 = 90\n   \\]\n\n   So, 2250 degrees is equivalent to 90 degrees.\n\n3. **Determine the final direction**: Starting from north and spinning 90 degrees to the right, the skater will face east.\n\nTherefore, the skater is facing \\boxed{\\text{east}}.', 'To determine the direction the figure skater is facing after spinning 2250 degrees to her right, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the initial direction:**\n   The skater starts facing north.\n\n2. **Convert the total degrees into full rotations and remaining degrees:**\n   Since one full rotation is 360 degrees, we can find out how many full rotations and how many degrees are left after 2250 degrees by dividing 2250 by 360.\n   \\[\n   2250 \\div 360 = 6.25\n   \\]\n   This means the skater completes 6 full rotations and has an additional 0.25 of a rotation left. To find the remaining degrees:\n   \\[\n   0.25 \\times 360 = 90 \\text{ degrees}\n   \\]\n   So, the skater has spun 6 full rotations and an additional 90 degrees to the right.\n\n3. **Determine the direction after the remaining degrees:**\n   Since the skater started facing north and spun 90 degrees to the right, she will now be facing east.\n\nTherefore, the direction the skater is facing when she finishes her spin is \\boxed{\\text{east}}.', 'To determine the direction the figure skater is facing after spinning 2250 degrees to her right, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the initial direction**: The skater starts facing north.\n\n2. **Convert the total degrees into full rotations and remaining degrees**:\n   - A full rotation is 360 degrees.\n   - We divide 2250 degrees by 360 degrees to find out how many full rotations and the remaining degrees she has spun.\n\n   \\[\n   2250 \\div 360 = 6 \\text{ full rotations and } 90 \\text{ degrees remaining}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the effect of the full rotations**:\n   - Each full rotation (360 degrees) brings the skater back to her starting direction (north).\n   - Therefore, 6 full rotations will also bring her back to north.\n\n4. **Consider the remaining degrees**:\n   - After 6 full rotations, she has 90 degrees left to spin.\n   - Spinning 90 degrees to the right from north will bring her to east.\n\n5. **Final direction**:\n   - Since the remaining 90 degrees is the only part of the spin that affects her final direction, she will be facing east.\n\nThus, the skater is facing \\boxed{east} when she finishes her spin.', 'To determine the direction the figure skater is facing after spinning 2250 degrees to her right, we need to break down the problem step by step.\n\n1. **Understand the initial direction:**\n   The skater starts facing north.\n\n2. **Convert the total spin into full rotations:**\n   A full rotation is 360 degrees. We need to find out how many full rotations are in 2250 degrees.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of full rotations} = \\frac{2250 \\text{ degrees}}{360 \\text{ degrees/rotation}} = 6.25 \\text{ rotations}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the remaining degrees after full rotations:**\n   Since 6.25 rotations include 6 full rotations and a fraction of a rotation, we calculate the remaining degrees:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Remaining degrees} = 2250 \\text{ degrees} - (6 \\times 360 \\text{ degrees}) = 2250 \\text{ degrees} - 2160 \\text{ degrees} = 90 \\text{ degrees}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Interpret the remaining degrees:**\n   The remaining 90 degrees is the additional rotation beyond the full rotations. Since the skater spins to her right, we need to determine the direction she faces after spinning 90 degrees to the right from north.\n\n5. **Determine the final direction:**\n   - Starting from north and spinning 90 degrees to the right, the skater will face east.\n\nTherefore, the skater is facing \\boxed{\\text{east}} when she finishes her spin.', 'To determine the direction the figure skater is facing after spinning 2250 degrees to the right, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the starting direction:**\n   - The skater starts facing north.\n\n2. **Convert the total degrees of spin into complete rotations:**\n   - A full rotation is 360 degrees.\n   - We need to find out how many complete 360-degree rotations are in 2250 degrees.\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2250}{360} = 6.25\n   \\]\n   - This means the skater completes 6 full rotations and has an additional 0.25 of a rotation.\n\n3. **Calculate the degrees in the additional rotation:**\n   - To find the degrees in the additional 0.25 rotation:\n   \\[\n   0.25 \\times 360 = 90 \\text{ degrees}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Determine the direction after the additional rotation:**\n   - Starting from north, a 90-degree rotation to the right will face the skater east.\n\nTherefore, after spinning 2250 degrees to the right, the figure skater will be facing \\boxed{east}.\n\nFind the difference between $x$ and $y$, given that $x=-\\frac{41}{5}+\\frac{31 i}{5}$ and $y=\\frac{44}{5}-\\frac{11 i}{5}$.\nTo find the difference between \\( x \\) and \\( y \\), we need to compute \\( x - y \\). Given:\n\n\\[ x = -\\frac{41}{5} + \\frac{31 i}{5} \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{44}{5} - \\frac{11 i}{5} \\]\n\nWe subtract \\( y \\) from \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ x - y = \\left( -\\frac{41}{5} + \\frac{31 i}{5} \\right) - \\left( \\frac{44}{5} - \\frac{11 i}{5} \\right) \\]\n\nFirst, distribute the negative sign through the parentheses:\n\n\\[ x - y = -\\frac{41}{5} + \\frac{31 i}{5} - \\frac{44}{5} + \\frac{11 i}{5} \\]\n\nNext, combine the real parts and the imaginary parts separately:\n\n\\[ x - y = \\left( -\\frac{41}{5} - \\frac{44}{5} \\right) + \\left( \\frac{31 i}{5} + \\frac{11 i}{5} \\right) \\]\n\nAdd the real parts:\n\n\\[ -\\frac{41}{5} - \\frac{44}{5} = -\\frac{41 + 44}{5} = -\\frac{85}{5} = -17 \\]\n\nAdd the imaginary parts:\n\n\\[ \\frac{31 i}{5} + \\frac{11 i}{5} = \\frac{31 + 11}{5} i = \\frac{42}{5} i \\]\n\nCombine these results:\n\n\\[ x - y = -17 + \\frac{42 i}{5} \\]\n\nThus, the difference between \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{-17 + \\frac{42 i}{5}} \\]\n\nFind the roots of the polynomial: $-25x^2/2 + 17x/2 + 13/2$.\nTo find the roots of the polynomial \\(-\\frac{25x^2}{2} + \\frac{17x}{2} + \\frac{13}{2}\\), we will use the quadratic formula. The quadratic formula is given by:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we need to identify the coefficients \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) from the polynomial. The given polynomial is:\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{25x^2}{2} + \\frac{17x}{2} + \\frac{13}{2}\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite it in the standard form \\(ax^2 + bx + c\\):\n\n\\[\na = -\\frac{25}{2}, \\quad b = \\frac{17}{2}, \\quad c = \\frac{13}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{-\\left(\\frac{17}{2}\\right) \\pm \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{17}{2', 'To determine the direction the figure skater is facing after spinning 2250 degrees to the right, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the initial direction:**\n   The skater starts facing north.\n\n2. **Calculate the equivalent angle within one full rotation:**\n   A full rotation is 360 degrees. We need to find the equivalent angle within one full rotation by subtracting multiples of 360 degrees from 2250 degrees until we get an angle between 0 and 360 degrees.\n   \\[\n   2250 \\div 360 = 6.25\n   \\]\n   This means 2250 degrees is equivalent to 6 full rotations plus an additional 0.25 of a rotation. To find the additional angle:\n   \\[\n   2250 - (6 \\times 360) = 2250 - 2160 = 90\n   \\]\n   So, 2250 degrees is equivalent to 90 degrees.\n\n3. **Determine the direction after spinning 90 degrees to the right:**\n   Starting from north and spinning 90 degrees to the right means she will face east.\n\nTherefore, the skater is facing \\boxed{\\text{east}} when she finishes her spin.', "To solve this problem, we need to understand how degrees relate to directions on a compass. A full rotation around a circle is 360 degrees. When the skater spins, we need to determine how many full rotations she completes and what the remainder is.\n\nFirst, let's find out how many full rotations (360 degrees each) are in 2250 degrees:\n\n\\[ 2250 \\div 360 = 6 \\text{ remainder } 90 \\]\n\nThis means the skater completes 6 full rotations and has an additional 90 degrees of rotation.\n\nNext, we need to determine the direction for the remaining 90 degrees. We can use the following rules:\n\n- 0 degrees or 360 degrees: North\n- 90 degrees: East\n- 180 degrees: South\n- 270 degrees: West\n\nSince 90 degrees falls between 0 and 360 degrees, and it is exactly 90 degrees, the skater will be facing East when she finishes her spin.\n\nTherefore, the final direction the skater is facing is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{East}}\n\\]\n\n### Detailed Explanation\n\n1. Divide the total degrees by 360: \n   \\[ 2250 \\div 360 = 6.25 \\]\n   This means the skater completes 6 full rotations and has an additional \\( 2250 - 6 \\times 360 = 2250 - 2160 = 90 \\) degrees.\n\n2. Identify the direction for 90 degrees:\n   - 0 degrees (or 360 degrees) corresponds to North.\n   - 90 degrees corresponds to East.\n   - 180 degrees corresponds to South.\n   - 270 degrees corresponds to West.\n\nSince 90 degrees is exactly East, the skater will be facing East when she finishes her spin.\n\nBy breaking down the problem into manageable parts and using simple division and remainder calculations, we can determine the direction the skater will be facing after her spin. This approach teaches the students how to handle large numbers and apply modular arithmetic to solve real-world problems.", 'To determine the direction the figure skater is facing after spinning 2250 degrees to her right, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the initial direction:**\n   - The skater starts facing north.\n\n2. **Convert the total degrees of rotation into full circles:**\n   - A full circle is 360 degrees.\n   - We need to find out how many full circles are in 2250 degrees.\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2250 \\text{ degrees}}{360 \\text{ degrees/circle}} = 6.25 \\text{ circles}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the remaining degrees after completing full circles:**\n   - Since 6.25 circles means 6 full circles plus a fraction of a circle, we calculate the remaining degrees:\n   \\[\n   2250 \\text{ degrees} - (6 \\times 360 \\text{ degrees}) = 2250 \\text{ degrees} - 2160 \\text{ degrees} = 90 \\text{ degrees}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Interpret the remaining degrees:**\n   - The skater has rotated an additional 90 degrees to the right from her initial position.\n\n5. **Determine the final direction:**\n   - Starting from north and rotating 90 degrees to the right:\n     - North to East (90 degrees right)\n\nTherefore, after spinning 2250 degrees to her right, the figure skater is facing \\boxed{east}.', 'To determine the direction the figure skater is facing after spinning 2250 degrees to her right, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the initial direction:**\n   - The skater starts facing north.\n\n2. **Convert the total degrees into full rotations:**\n   - A full rotation is 360 degrees.\n   - We need to find out how many full rotations are in 2250 degrees.\n\n3. **Calculate the number of full rotations:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of full rotations} = \\frac{2250 \\text{ degrees}}{360 \\text{ degrees/rotation}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of full rotations} = 6.25\n   \\]\n\n4. **Determine the remaining degrees after full rotations:**\n   - Since 6.25 rotations include 6 full rotations and a fraction of a rotation, we calculate the remaining degrees:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Remaining degrees} = 2250 \\text{ degrees} - (6 \\times 360 \\text{ degrees})\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Remaining degrees} = 2250 \\text{ degrees} - 2160 \\text{ degrees}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Remaining degrees} = 90 \\text{ degrees}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Determine the direction after the remaining degrees:**\n   - The skater starts facing north.\n   - Spinning 90 degrees to the right from north will face her east.\n\nTherefore, after spinning 2250 degrees to her right, the skater is facing \\boxed{\\text{east}}.', 'To determine the direction the figure skater is facing after spinning 2250 degrees to her right, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the problem**: The skater starts facing north and spins 2250 degrees to her right. We need to find out which direction she is facing after this spin.\n\n2. **Normalize the angle**: Since a full rotation is 360 degrees, we can reduce the angle by finding the equivalent angle within one full rotation. This is done by dividing the total degrees by 360 and taking the remainder.\n\n   \\[\n   2250 \\div 360 = 6.25\n   \\]\n\n   The quotient is 6, which means the skater completes 6 full rotations. The remainder is:\n\n   \\[\n   2250 - (6 \\times 360) = 2250 - 2160 = 90\n   \\]\n\n   So, 2250 degrees is equivalent to 90 degrees.\n\n3. **Determine the final direction**: Starting from north and spinning 90 degrees to the right means she will face east.\n\nTherefore, the skater is facing \\boxed{\\text{east}} when she finishes her spin.', 'To determine the direction the figure skater is facing after spinning 2250 degrees to her right, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the initial direction:**\n   The skater starts facing north.\n\n2. **Calculate the effective rotation:**\n   Since a full rotation is 360 degrees, we need to find the equivalent rotation within one full circle by subtracting multiples of 360 degrees from 2250 degrees.\n\n   \\[\n   2250 \\div 360 = 6.25\n   \\]\n\n   This means the skater completes 6 full rotations and an additional 0.25 of a rotation. To find the degrees in the additional rotation:\n\n   \\[\n   0.25 \\times 360 = 90 \\text{ degrees}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the final direction:**\n   The skater starts facing north and spins an additional 90 degrees to her right. We can visualize this as follows:\n   - Starting at North\n   - After 90 degrees to the right: East\n\nTherefore, the skater is facing \\boxed{\\text{East}} when she finishes her spin.', 'To determine the direction the figure skater is facing after spinning 2250 degrees to her right, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Understand the initial direction:**\n   The skater starts facing north.\n\n2. **Convert the total degrees into full rotations:**\n   A full rotation is 360 degrees. We need to find out how many full rotations are in 2250 degrees.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of full rotations} = \\frac{2250 \\text{ degrees}}{360 \\text{ degrees/rotation}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of full rotations} = 6.25\n   \\]\n   This means the skater completes 6 full rotations and an additional 0.25 of a rotation.\n\n3. **Calculate the remaining degrees after full rotations:**\n   To find the remaining degrees, we multiply the fractional part of the rotation by 360 degrees.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Remaining degrees} = 0.25 \\times 360 \\text{ degrees}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Remaining degrees} = 90 \\text{ degrees}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Determine the direction after the remaining degrees:**\n   Starting from north, we spin 90 degrees to the right. The directions in a clockwise manner are:\n   - North to East (90 degrees)\n   - East to South (180 degrees)\n   - South to West (270 degrees)\n   - West to North (360 degrees)\n\n   Since we are spinning 90 degrees to the right from north, we end up facing east.\n\nTherefore, the direction the skater is facing when she finishes her spin is \\boxed{east}.', 'Simplify $(-3k + 5) + (-2 + 7k)$. البنك\nSimplify $(-5k + 3) + (-4 - 3k)$. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\b