l elements in \\( S \\) would then be \\( 10r + 7(k_1 + k_2 + \\cdots + k_{10}) \\). Since \\( 7(k_1 + k_2 + \\cdots + k_{10}) \\) is divisible by 7, the sum of all elements in \\( S \\) is congruent to \\( 10r \\) modulo 7. Since \\( r \\) can be any integer from 0 to 6, the possible values for \\( 10r \\mod 7 \\) are the same as the possible values for \\( 3r \\mod 7 \\) (since \\( 10 \\equiv 3 \\mod 7 \\)).\n\nThe possible values for \\( 3r \\mod 7 \\) are:\n\\[ 3 \\cdot 0 \\equiv 0 \\mod 7 \\]\n\\[ 3 \\cdot 1 \\equiv 3 \\mod 7 \\]\n\\[ 3 \\cdot 2 \\equiv 6 \\mod 7 \\]\n\\[ 3 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 9 \\equiv 2 \\mod 7 \\]\n\\[ 3 \\cdot 4 \\equiv 12 \\equiv 5 \\mod 7 \\]\n\\[ 3 \\cdot 5 \\equiv 15 \\equiv 1 \\mod 7 \\]\n\\[ 3 \\cdot 6 \\equiv 18 \\equiv 4 \\mod 7 \\]\n\nSo the possible values for the sum of all elements in \\( S \\) modulo 7 are \\( 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 \\). This means there are 7 distinct possible values for the sum of all elements in \\( S \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with a Python code implementation to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Let's calculate the possible values of the sum of all elements in S modulo 7\npossible_values = set()\n\n# We need to consider all possible remainders when divided by 7\nfor r in range(7):\n    # Calculate 10r % 7\n    sum_mod_7 = (10 * r) % 7\n    possible_values.add(sum_mod_7)\n\n# The number of distinct possible values\nnum_distinct_values = len(possible_values)\nprint(num_distinct_values)\n```\n\nHuman: A high school is conducting a study to understand the relationship between the number of hours students spend on homework each week and their average test scores. They collected data from 100 students and found the following linear relationship: $y = 2x + 50$, where $y$ represents the average test score and $x$ represents the number of hours spent on homework. They also noted that the average test score for a student who spends 10 hours on homework is 70. Determine the averag