 sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is \\(720^\\circ\\), we have:\n\\[\n105^\\circ + 110^\\circ + \\alpha + a + b + c = 720^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n215^\\circ + \\alpha + a + b + c = 720^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha + a + b + c = 505^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSince the hexagon is inscribed in a circle, opposite angles sum to \\(180^\\circ\\). Therefore, we can pair the angles as follows:\n\\[\n105^\\circ + a = 180^\\circ \\implies a = 75^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n110^\\circ + b = 180^\\circ \\implies b = 70^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha + c = 180^\\circ \\implies c = 180^\\circ - \\alpha\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) into the equation:\n\\[\n\\alpha + 75^\\circ + 70^\\circ + (180^\\circ - \\alpha) = 505^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha + 75^\\circ + 70^\\circ + 180^\\circ - \\alpha = 505^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n325^\\circ = 505^\\circ - \\alpha\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha = 505^\\circ - 325^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nHowever, this seems incorrect because it doesn't fit the context of the problem. Let's recheck the pairing and the sum. The correct pairing should be:\n\\[\n105^\\circ + c = 180^\\circ \\implies c = 75^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n110^\\circ + a = 180^\\circ \\implies a = 70^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha + b = 180^\\circ \\implies b = 180^\\circ - \\alpha\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) into the equation:\n\\[\n\\alpha + 70^\\circ + (180^\\circ - \\alpha) + 75^\\circ = 505^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha + 70^\\circ + 180^\\circ - \\alpha + 75^\\circ = 505^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n325^\\circ = 505^\\circ - \\alpha\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha = 505^\\circ - 325^\\circ\n\\]\n\\[\n\\alpha = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThis still seems incorrect. Let's simplify the problem. Since the sum of the angles in the hexagon is \\(720^\\circ\\) and we have three known angles and three unknown angles, we can directly find \\(\\alpha\\) by subtracting the known angles from \\(360^\\circ\\) (since opposite angles sum to \\(180^\\circ\\)).\n\nThe correct approach is to directly find \\(\\alpha\