c\\]\n\\[105^\\circ + 110^\\circ + \\alpha + 75^\\circ + 70^\\circ = 360^\\circ\\]\n\\[360^\\circ + \\alpha = 360^\\circ\\]\n\\[\\alpha = 180^\\circ - 105^\\circ - 110^\\circ + 180^\\circ\\]\n\\[\\alpha = 180^\\circ - 215^\\circ + 180^\\circ\\]\n\\[\\alpha = 145^\\circ\\]\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\alpha$ is $\\boxed{145^\\circ}$.', "To solve for the measure of angle $\\alpha$ in the given hexagon inscribed in a circle, we need to use the properties of cyclic hexagons and the fact that the sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is 720 degrees.\n\nGiven:\n- The hexagon is inscribed in a circle.\n- The measure of one of the angles is $105^\\circ$.\n- The measure of another angle is $110^\\circ$.\n- We need to find the measure of angle $\\alpha$.\n\nSince the hexagon is inscribed in a circle, the opposite angles of the hexagon will sum to 180 degrees. Let's denote the angles of the hexagon as $A, B, C, D, E,$ and $F$ in order. We know:\n- $A = 105^\\circ$\n- $B = 110^\\circ$\n- $D = \\alpha$\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is $720^\\circ$, we can write the equation:\n\\[ A + B + C + D + E + F = 720^\\circ \\]\n\\[ 105^\\circ + 110^\\circ + C + \\alpha + E + F = 720^\\circ \\]\n\\[ 215^\\circ + C + \\alpha + E + F = 720^\\circ \\]\n\\[ C + \\alpha + E + F = 505^\\circ \\]\n\nSince $A$ and $C$ are opposite angles, and $B$ and $E$ are opposite angles, we know:\n\\[ A + C = 180^\\circ \\]\n\\[ B + E = 180^\\circ \\]\n\\[ 105^\\circ + C = 180^\\circ \\]\n\\[ C = 75^\\circ \\]\n\\[ 110^\\circ + E = 180^\\circ \\]\n\\[ E = 70^\\circ \\]\n\nNow we can substitute $C$ and $E$ back into the equation:\n\\[ 75^\\circ + \\alpha + 70^\\circ + F = 505^\\circ \\]\n\\[ 145^\\circ + \\alpha + F = 505^\\circ \\]\n\\[ \\alpha + F = 360^\\circ \\]\n\nSince $D$ and $F$ are opposite angles, we know:\n\\[ D + F = 180^\\circ \\]\n\\[ \\alpha + F = 180^\\circ \\]\n\nTherefore, $\\alpha = 180^\\circ - F$. But since $\\alpha + F = 360^\\circ$, we can see that $F$ must be