rc\\), and \\(D = \\alpha\\). Since the hexagon is cyclic, the sum of the angles \\(A\\) and \\(D\\) (opposite angles) is \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\nSo, we can write:\n\\[A + D = 180^\\circ\\]\n\\[105^\\circ + \\alpha = 180^\\circ\\]\n\\[\\alpha = 180^\\circ - 105^\\circ\\]\n\\[\\alpha = 75^\\circ\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the calculation is accurate.\n```python\n# Given angles\nangle_A = 105\nangle_B = 110\n\n# Since the hexagon is cyclic, the sum of opposite angles is 180 degrees\n# So, angle_A + angle_D = 180 degrees\nangle_D = 180 - angle_A\n\nprint(angle_D)\n```\n```output\n75\n```\nThe measure of \\(\\alpha\\) is \\(\\boxed{75}\\) degrees.", "To solve for the measure of angle \\(\\alpha\\) in the inscribed hexagon, we can use the properties of cyclic polygons and the fact that the sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is \\(720^\\circ\\). Since the hexagon is inscribed in a circle, the opposite angles will sum to \\(180^\\circ\\).\n\nGiven:\n- One angle is \\(105^\\circ\\).\n- Another angle is \\(110^\\circ\\).\n- The angle \\(\\alpha\\) is opposite to another angle which we will denote as \\(\\beta\\).\n\nSince the sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is \\(720^\\circ\\), we can write the equation for the sum of the angles as:\n\\[105^\\circ + 110^\\circ + \\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma + \\delta = 720^\\circ\\]\nwhere \\(\\gamma\\) and \\(\\delta\\) are the other two angles of the hexagon.\n\nBecause the hexagon is inscribed in a circle, opposite angles sum to \\(180^\\circ\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[105^\\circ + \\delta = 180^\\circ \\implies \\delta = 75^\\circ\\]\n\\[110^\\circ + \\gamma = 180^\\circ \\implies \\gamma = 70^\\circ\\]\n\nNow we can substitute \\(\\delta\\) and \\(\\gamma\\) back into the sum of the interior angles equation:\n\\[105^\\circ + 110^\\circ + \\alpha + \\beta + 70^\\circ + 75^\\circ = 720^\\circ\\]\n\\[360^\\circ + \\alpha + \\beta = 720^\\circ\\]\n\\[\\alpha + \\beta = 360^\\circ\\]\n\nSince \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are opposite angles, they also sum to \\(180^\\circ\\):\n\\[\\alpha + \\beta = 180^\\circ\\]\n\nFrom the two equations \\(\\alpha + \\beta = 360^\\circ\\) and \\(\\alpha + \\beta = 180^\\circ\\), we can see that the only consistent solution is:\n\\[\\alpha = 180^\\circ - \\beta\\]\n\nGiven that the sum of the angles around a point is \\(360^\\circ\\), and knowing that the opposite angles of the inscribed hexagon sum to \\(180^\\circ\\), we can directly find \\(\\alpha\\):\n\\[\\alpha = 180^\\circ - \\beta\\]\nSince \\(\\beta\\) is the angle opposite to \\(105^\\circ\\), we have:\n\\[\\beta = 75^\\circ\\]\nThus:\n\\[\\alpha = 180^\\circ - 75^\\circ = 105^\\circ\\]\n\nHowever, we need to check the consistency with the given angles. The correct approach is to use the fact that the sum of the angles around a point is \\(360^\\circ\\), and knowing that the opposite angles of the inscribed hexagon sum to \\(180^\\circ\\), we can directly find \\(\\alpha\\):\n\\[\\alpha = 180^\\circ - 70^\\circ = 110^\\circ\\]\n\nThus, the measure of \\(\\alpha\\) is:\n\\[\\boxed{125^\\circ}\\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Given angles in the hexagon\r\nangle1 = 105\r\nangle2 = 110\r\n\r\n# Since the hexagon is inscribed in a circle, opposite angles sum to 180 degrees\r\nopposite_angle1 = 180 - angle1\r\nopposite_angle2 = 180 - angle2\r\n\r\n# The remaining angles sum to 360 degrees minus the sum of the given angles and their opposite angles\r\nremaining_sum = 720 - (angle1 + angle2 + opposite_angle1 + opposite_angle2)\r\n\r\n# Since the remaining angles are opposite to each other, they sum to 180 degrees\r\nalpha = remaining_sum // 2\r\n\r\nprint(alpha)\n```", "To solve for the measure of \\(\\alpha\\) in the given hexagon inscribed in a circle, we need to use the properties of cyclic hexagons and the fact that the sum of the interior angles of a hexagon is \\(720^\\circ\\).\n\nGiven:\n- One angle is \\(105^\\circ\\)