}{k}}\\). So, the foci are at \\((\\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{k}}, 0)\\).\n\n2. **Circle Passing Through Foci and Tangent at Two Points on the x-axis:**\n   The circle passes through the foci \\((\\pm \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{k}}, 0)\\) and is tangent to the ellipse at two points on the x-axis. The points of tangency on the x-axis are \\((\\pm a, 0)\\) where \\(a = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k}}\\). The radius of the circle is the distance from the center of the circle to the foci, which is also the distance from the center of the circle to the points of tangency on the x-axis. Since the circle is tangent to the ellipse at \\((\\pm a, 0)\\), the radius of the circle is \\(a\\).\n\n3. **Center of the Circle:**\n   The center of the circle is at \\((0, r)\\) because the circle is symmetric about the y-axis and tangent to the x-axis at \\((\\pm a, 0)\\). The distance from the center of the circle to the foci is the radius of the circle, which is \\(a\\). So, the distance from \\((0, r)\\) to \\((\\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{k}}, 0)\\) is \\(a\\). This gives us:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{\\left(\\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{k}}\\right)^2 + r^2} = a\n   \\]\n   Since \\(a = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k}}\\), we substitute \\(a\\) and simplify:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{k} + r^2} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k}}\n   \\]\n   Squaring both sides, we get:\n   \\[\n   1 - \\frac{1}{k} + r^2 = \\frac{1}{k}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying further, we get:\n   \\[\n   r^2 = \\frac{2}{k} - 1\n   \\]\n   Since the circle is tangent to the ellipse at \\((\\pm a, 0)\\), the radius \\(r\\) is equal to \\(a\\):\n   \\[\n   r = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k}}\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(r\\) in the equation \\(r^2 = \\frac{2}{k} - 1\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{k}}\\right)^2 = \\frac{2}{k} - 1\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{k} = \\frac{2}{k} - 1\n   \\]\n   Multiplying through by \\(k\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   1 = 2 - k\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(k\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   k = 1\n   \\]\n   However, since \\(k > 1\\)