l number of ways to choose 3 boys and 2 girls is the product of these two combinations.\n\n\\[\n\\binom{7}{3} = \\frac{7!}{3!(7-3)!} = \\frac{7 \\times 6 \\times 5}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 35\n\\]\n\\[\n\\binom{4}{2} = \\frac{4!}{2!(4-2)!} = \\frac{4 \\times 3}{2 \\times 1} = 6\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Total number of ways} = \\binom{7}{3} \\times \\binom{4}{2} = 35 \\times 6 = 210\n\\]\n\n### Part 2: Probability that the First and Last Member Get the Same Flavor\n\nThere are 5 flavors of chocolate, and each member picks a chocolate at random. The probability that the first member picks a particular flavor is \\( \\frac{1}{5} \\). Since the last member picks after all other members have picked, the probability that the last member picks the same flavor as the first member is also \\( \\frac{1}{5} \\), assuming the flavors are uniformly distributed and the first member's choice does not affect the last member's choice.\n\nSo, the probability that the first and last member get the same flavor is \\( \\frac{1}{5} \\).\n\nLet's summarize the results in Python code:\n\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Part 1: Calculate the number of ways to choose 3 boys and 2 girls\nways_to_choose_boys = math.comb(7, 3)\nways_to_choose_girls = math.comb(4, 2)\ntotal_ways = ways_to_choose_boys * ways_to_choose_girls\n\n# Part 2: Probability that the first and last member get the same flavor\nprobability_same_flavor = 1 / 5\n\nprint(total_ways, probability_same_flavor)\n```\n```output\n210 0.2\n```\nThe number of ways to choose 3 boys and 2 girls from a class of 7 boys and 4 girls is \\( \\boxed{210} \\).\n\nThe probability that the first and last member to pick a chocolate get the same flavor, given that there are 5 flavors of chocolate in the box, is \\( \\boxed{\\frac{1}{5}} \\).", 'There are 10 people in a club. How many ways can the club choose a president, vice president, secretary, and treasurer? \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}