 \\]\n   Simplifying both sides, we get:\n   \\[\n   3x - 5 = 4\n   \\]\n\n3. Solve for \\(x\\) by isolating the variable. First, add 5 to both sides:\n   \\[\n   3x - 5 + 5 = 4 + 5\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   3x = 9\n   \\]\n\n4. Divide both sides by 3:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3x}{3} = \\frac{9}{3}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   x = 3\n   \\]\n\n5. Verify the solution by substituting \\(x = 3\\) back into the original equation:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{3(3) - 5} = \\sqrt{9 - 5} = \\sqrt{4} = 2\n   \\]\n   Since the left side equals the right side, \\(x = 3\\) is indeed a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]\n\n3. To solve the equation \\(\\sqrt{2x+3}=5\\), we need to eliminate the square root by squaring both sides of the equation. Here are the steps:\n\n1. Start with the given equation:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{2x+3} = 5\n   \\]\n\n2. Square both sides to remove the square root:\n   \\[\n   (\\sqrt{2x+3})^2 = 5^2\n   \\]\n   Simplifying both sides, we get:\n   \\[\n   2x + 3 = 25\n   \\]\n\n3. Solve for \\(x\\) by isolating the variable. First, subtract 3 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   2x + 3 - 3 = 25 - 3\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   2x = 22\n   \\]\n\n4. Divide both sides by 2:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2x}{2} = \\frac{22}{2}\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   x = 11\n   \\]\n\n5. Verify the solution by substituting \\(x = 11\\) back into the original equation:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{2(11) + 3} = \\sqrt{22 + 3} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n   \\]\n   Since the left side equals the right side, \\(x = 11\\) is indeed a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{11}\n\\]', "To solve the equation \\(\\sqrt{3x-5} = 2\\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Square both sides of the equation to eliminate the square root.\n2. Solve the resulting linear equation for \\(x\\).\n\nLet's implement this in Python using sympy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the equatio