lculate this for \\(42!\\):\n\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{42}{5} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{42}{25} \\right\\rfloor = 8 + 1 = 9\n\\]\n\nWe don't need to go further since \\(5^3 = 125\\) is greater than 42.\n\nSo, the number of trailing zeroes in \\(42!\\) is 9. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\ndef trailing_zeroes(n):\n    count = 0\n    power_of_5 = 5\n    while n >= power_of_5:\n        count += n // power_of_5\n        power_of_5 *= 5\n    return count\n\n# Calculate the number of trailing zeroes in 42!\ntrailing_zeroes_42 = trailing_zeroes(42)\nprint(trailing_zeroes_42)\n```\n```output\n9\n```\nThe number of trailing zeroes in \\(42!\\) is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).", "To determine how many zeroes are at the end of \\(42!\\) (42 factorial), we need to count the number of times 10 is a factor in the product of integers from 1 to 42. \n\nSince \\(10 = 2 \\times 5\\), each pair of factors 2 and 5 will contribute to a trailing zero. However, there are usually more factors of 2 than factors of 5 in factorials. Therefore, the number of trailing zeroes is determined by the number of times 5 is a factor in the numbers from 1 to 42.\n\nTo count the number of factors of 5 in \\(42!\\):\n\n1. Count the multiples of 5.\n2. Count the multiples of \\(5^2 = 25\\) (since each contributes an extra factor of 5).\n\nWe can use the formula:\n\\[\n\\text{Number of trailing zeroes} = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{5} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{25} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{125} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\left\\lfloor x \\right\\rfloor\\) denotes the floor function, the greatest integer less than or equal to \\(x\\).\n\nLet's compute this using Python.\n```python\ndef count_trailing_zeroes(n):\n    count = 0\n    power_of_5 = 5\n    while n >= power_of_5:\n        count += n // power_of_5\n        power_of_5 *= 5\n    return count\n\n# Calculate the number of trailing zeroes in 42!\ntrailing_zeroes_42_factorial = count_trai