s \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is the same as the GCD of \\(b\\) and \\(a \\mod b\\). So, we can find:\n   \\[\n   \\gcd(n + 7, 2n + 1) = \\gcd(n + 7, (2n + 1) - 2(n + 7)) = \\gcd(n + 7, 2n + 1 - 2n - 14) = \\gcd(n + 7, -13) = \\gcd(n + 7, 13)\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the problem reduces to finding the greatest common divisor of \\(n + 7\\) and 13. The greatest possible value of the GCD of \\(n + 7\\) and 13 is 13, which occurs when \\(n + 7\\) is a multiple of 13. This means \\(n = 13k - 7\\) for some integer \\(k\\).\n\n3. **Conclusion:**\n   The greatest possible value of \\(\\gcd(n + 7, 2n + 1)\\) is 13. Let's verify this with Python and SymPy.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nn = sp.symbols('n', integer=True)\n\n# Define the expressions\nexpr1 = n + 7\nexpr2 = 2*n + 1\n\n# Compute the gcd\ngcd_expr = sp.gcd(expr1, expr2)\n\n# The gcd should be a constant value, so we can find the maximum possible value\nmax_gcd = sp.Max(gcd_expr)\nprint(max_gcd)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nIt appears there was an error in the reasoning or the code. The correct approach should consider the simplified form of the gcd as derived earlier. We need to ensure that we correctly identify the maximum possible value of the gcd of \\(n + 7\\) and 13.\n\nLet's correct the approach and verify the result again. The gcd of \\(n + 7\\) and 13 is maximized when \\(n + 7\\) is a multiple of 13. This means \\(n = 13k - 7\\) for some integer \\(k\\). In this case, the gcd is 13.\n\nLet's verify this with a different approach in Python:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nn = sp.symbols('n', integer=True)\n\n# Define the expressions\nexpr1 = n + 7\nexpr2 = 2*n + 1\n\n# Compute the gcd of n + 7 and 13\ngcd_expr = sp.gcd(expr1, 13)\n\n# The gcd should be a constant value, so we can find the maximum possible value\nmax_gcd = sp.Max(gcd_expr)\nprint(max_gcd)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nIt seems there is still an issue with the approach. Let's manually verify the steps aga