\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf{w}\\| = \\sqrt{w_1^2 + w_2^2 + w_3^2}\n\\]\n\nLet's calculate the cross product of \\(\\overrightarrow{OA}\\) and \\(\\overrightarrow{OB}\\), and then find the magnitude of the resulting vector. Finally, we will take half of this magnitude to get the area of the triangle.\n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\overrightarrow{OB} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe cross product \\(\\overrightarrow{OA} \\times \\overrightarrow{OB}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{OA} \\times \\overrightarrow{OB} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\cdot 1 - 3 \\cdot (-2) \\\\ 3 \\cdot (-3) - 1 \\cdot 1 \\\\ 1 \\cdot (-2) - 2 \\cdot (-3) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 + 6 \\\\ -9 - 1 \\\\ -2 + 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 8 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nThe magnitude of this vector is:\n\\[\n\\|\\overrightarrow{OA} \\times \\overrightarrow{OB}\\| = \\sqrt{8^2 + (-10)^2 + 4^2} = \\sqrt{64 + 100 + 16} = \\sqrt{180} = 6\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\n\nThe area of the triangle \\(OAB\\) is half of this magnitude:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6\\sqrt{5} = 3\\sqrt{5}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this calculation using Python.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the vectors OA and OB\nOA = np.array([1, 2, 3])\nOB = np.array([-3, -2, 1])\n\n# Calculate the cross product of OA and OB\ncross_product = np.cross(OA, OB)\n\n# Calculate the magnitude of the cross product\nmagnitude = np.linalg.norm(cross_product)\n\n# Calculate the area of the triangle\narea = 0.5 * magnitude\nprint(area)\n```\n```output\n6.708203932499369\n```\nThe area of triangle \\(OAB\\) is \\(3\\sqrt{5}\\), which is approximately \\(6.708\\).\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3\\sqrt{5}}\n\\]", "To find the point on the curve at \\( t = 2 \\), we need to substitute \\( t = 2 \\) into the parameterized equations for \\( x \\) and \\( y \\).\n\nThe parameterized equations are:\n\\[ x = t^3 + 7 \\]\n\\[ y = -3t^2 - 6t - 5 \\]\n\nLet's substitute \\( t = 2 \\) into these equations.\n\n\\[ x = 2^3 + 7 = 8 + 7 = 15 \\]\n\\[ y = -3(2^2) - 6(2) - 5 = -3(4) - 12 - 5 = -12 - 12 - 5 = -29 \\]\n\nSo the point the curve passes through at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\( (15, -29) \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the parameterized equations\ndef x(t):\n    return t**3 + 7\n\ndef y(t):\n    return -3*t**2 - 6*t - 5\n\n# Substitute t = 2 into the equations\nt = 2\npoint_x = x(t)\npoint_y = y(t)\n\nprint(((point_x, point_y)))\n```\n```output\n(15, -29)\n```\nThe point the curve passes through at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{(15, -29)}\\).", "To find the point the curve passes through at \\( t = 2 \\), we need to substitute \\( t = 2 \\) into the parameterized equations:\n\n\\[\nx = t^3 + 7\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -3t^2 - 6t - 5\n\\]\n\nLet's compute \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) when \\( t = 2 \\):\n\n\\[\nx = 2^3 + 7 = 8 + 7 = 15\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -3(2^2) - 6(2) - 5 = -3(4) - 12 - 5 = -12 - 12 - 5 = -29\n\\]\n\nSo, the point the curve passes through at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\((15, -29)\\).\n\nLet's confirm this by using Python code.\n```python\n# Define the parameter t\nt = 2\n\n# Calculate x and y using the given parameterized equations\nx = t**3 + 7\ny = -3*t**2 - 6*t - 5\n\nprint(((x, y)))\n```\n```output\n(15, -29)\n```\nThe point the curve passes through at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{(15, -29)}\\).", "To find the point the curve passes through at \\( t = 2 \\), we need to substitute \\( t = 2 \\) into the parameterized equations \\( x = t^3 + 7 \\) and \\( y = -3t^2 - 6t - 5 \\).\n\nLet's break it down step-by-step:\n\n1. Substitute \\( t = 2 \\) into the equation for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = 2^3 + 7 = 8 + 7 = 15. \\]\n\n2. Substitute \\( t = 2 \\) into the equation for \\( y \\):\n\\[ y = -3(2^2) - 6(2) - 5 = -3(4) - 12 - 5 = -12 - 12 - 5 = -29. \\]\n\nSo the point the curve passes through at \\( t = 2 \\) is \\( (15, -29) \\).\n\nLet's confirm this by implementing it in Python.\n```p