 both sides by 2 to solve for $x$:\n\\[ x = \\frac{22}{2} = 11 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation $\\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{4}{x-5}$ is $x = 11$.\n\n\\boxed{11}\n\nA group of students is collecting data on the number of books read by each student in their class over the summer. They notice that the number of books read by each student is not independent of each other. For example, if one student reads a lot of books, it is likely that their friends will also read a lot of books. To simplify the analysis, they decide to use a method similar to the one described in the math content excerpt.\n\nLet's assume that there are 30 students in the class, and each student has read a certain number of books. The students want to estimate the average number of books read by each student using a simplified version of the method described in the math content. They decide to group the students into 5 groups of 6 students each and calculate the average number of books read by each group. Then, they will calculate the overall average by averaging the group averages.\n\nSuppose the number of books read by the students in each group is as follows:\n\n- Group 1: 10, 12, 14, 15, 16, 18\n- Group 2: 12, 14, 16, 18, 20, 22\n- Group 3: 14, 16, 18, 20, 22, 24\n- Group 4: 16, 18, 20, 22, 24, 26\n- Group 5: 18, 20, 22, 24, 26, 28\n\nCalculate the average number of books read by each group and then find the overall average number of books read by the students in the class using the simplified method.\nTo find the average number of books read by each group, we need to add up the number of books read by each student in the group and then divide by the number of students in the group, which is 6.\n\nFor Group 1, the average number of books read is:\n\\[\n\\frac{10 + 12 + 14 + 15 + 16 + 18}{6} = \\frac{85}{6} = 14.1667\n\\]\n\nFor Group 2, the average number of books read is:\n\\[\n\\frac{12 + 14 + 16 + 18 + 20 + 22}{6} = \\frac{102}{6} = 17\n\\]\n\nFor Group 3, the average 