\boxed{60}\\).\n\n3. The number of ways to choose 2 students out of 5 is given by the combination formula \\(\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\\), where \\(n = 5\\) and \\(k = 2\\):\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4 \\times 3!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{10}\\).\n\n4. Let the two-digit number be represented as \\(10a + b\\), where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are its digits. We are given two conditions:\n1. The sum of the digits is 11: \\(a + b = 11\\)\n2. If the digits are reversed, the new number is 9 more than the original number: \\(10b + a = 10a + b + 9\\)\n\nSimplifying the second equation:\n\\[\n10b + a - 10a - b = 9 \\implies 9b - 9a = 9 \\implies b - a = 1\n\\]\nWe now have a system of linear equations:\n\\[\na + b = 11\n\\]\n\\[\nb - a = 1\n\\]\nAdding these two equations:\n\\[\n(a + b) + (b - a) = 11 + 1 \\implies 2b = 12 \\implies b = 6\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(b = 6\\) into the first equation:\n\\[\na + 6 = 11 \\implies a = 5\n\\]\nTherefore, the original number is \\(10a + b = 1', 'The sum of the first 50 terms of a specific arithmetic sequence is 610. What is the sum of the first 100 terms of the same arithmetic sequence? \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \