05\n```\nThe decimal value of the fraction $\\frac{21}{2^2 \\cdot 5^7}$ is $0.0000672$. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.0000672}\n\\]\n\nLet \\( m \\) be a positive integer, and define \\( S(m) \\) to be the sum of the digits of \\( m \\). For a given integer \\( n \\), define \\( T(n) \\) to be the sum of \\( S(m) \\) for all positive integers \\( m \\) such that \\( 1 \\leq m \\leq n \\). \n\nDetermine the value of \\( T(1000) \\).\nTo determine \\( T(1000) \\), we need to find the sum of the digits of all numbers from 1 to 1000. We can break this problem into several steps:\n\n1. Understanding the Sum of Digits Function \\( S(m) \\):\n   - For any number \\( m \\), \\( S(m) \\) is the sum of its digits.\n   - For example, \\( S(123) = 1 + 2 + 3 = 6 \\).\n\n2. Calculating \\( T(n) \\) for Small Values:\n   - First, calculate \\( T(10) \\):\n     \\[\n     T(10) = S(1) + S(2) + \\ldots + S(10)\n     \\]\n     - \\( S(1) = 1 \\), \\( S(2) = 2 \\), \\( \\ldots \\), \\( S(10) = 1 \\).\n     - Thus, \\( T(10) = 1 + 2 + 3 + \\ldots + 1 = 55 \\).\n\n3. Generalizing for Larger Values:\n   - To find \\( T(1000) \\), we need to consider the sum of digits for each decade from 1 to 1000.\n   - We can use the fact that the sum of the digits from 1 to 9 is \\( 1 + 2 + 3 + \\ldots + 9 = 45 \\).\n\n4. Counting Each Digit's Contribution:\n   - Each digit from 0 to 9 appears equally often in the units place from 1 to 1000. Therefore, each digit appears 100 times.\n   - The sum of the digits in the units place for each number from 1 to 1000 is:\n     \\[\n     100 \\times (0 + 1 + 2 + \\ldots + 9) = 100 \\times 45 = 4500\n     \\]\n\n5. Considering the Tens Place:\n   - In the tens place, each digit from 0 to 9 appears 100 times in each decade (10 to 19, 20 to 29, ..., 90 to 99).\n   - For each decade, the sum of the digits in the tens place is \\( 10 \\times (0 + 1 + 2 + \\ldots + 9) = 10 \\times 45 = 450 \\).\n   - Since there are 10 such decades from 1 to 1000, 