a radius of 1 and they are tangent to each other).\n\nThe quadrilateral $PQRS$ is a square with side length 2. The triangle $PQS$ is formed by three of the vertices of this square. To find the smallest angle in $\\triangle PQS$, we need to recognize that $\\triangle PQS$ is an isosceles right triangle because $PQ = QS = 2$ and $PS = 2\\sqrt{2}$ (the diagonal of the square).\n\nIn an isosceles right triangle, the two equal angles are each $45^\\circ$. Therefore, the smallest angle in $\\triangle PQS$ is $45^\\circ$.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the accuracy of our reasoning.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the vertices of the square PQRS\nP = sp.Point(1, 1)\nQ = sp.Point(-1, 1)\nR = sp.Point(-1, -1)\nS = sp.Point(1, -1)\n\n# Define the triangle PQS\ntriangle_PQS = sp.Triangle(P, Q, S)\n\n# Get the angles of the triangle\nangles = triangle_PQS.angles\n\n# Find the smallest angle\nsmallest_angle = min(angles.values())\n\n# Convert the angle from radians to degrees\nsmallest_angle_degrees = sp.deg(smallest_angle)\n\nprint(smallest_angle_degrees)\n```\n```output\n45\n```\nThe degree measure of the smallest angle in triangle $PQS$ is \\(\\boxed{45}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to find the degree measure of the smallest angle in triangle \\(PQS\\). Given that the circles are tangent to each other and to the sides of the triangle, we can use geometric properties to determine the coordinates of the centers of the circles and then calculate the angles of triangle \\(PQS\\).\n\n1. **Understand the Geometry:**\n   - The centers of the circles \\(P\\), \\(Q\\), \\(R\\), and \\(S\\) form a quadrilateral. Since the circles are tangent to each other and to the sides of the triangle, the centers \\(P\\), \\(Q\\), \\(R\\), and \\(S\\) are equidistant from each other and the sides of the triangle.\n   - The distance between the centers of two tangent circles is equal to the sum of their radii. Since all circles have a radius of 1, the distance 