ts of unity and the fact that the product of the distances from any point on the unit circle to the other three points is maximized when the points are optimally placed.\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of \\( |(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)| \\) is \\(\\boxed{16}\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport cmath\r\n\r\n# Define the complex numbers a, b, c, d as the vertices of a regular tetrahedron inscribed in the unit sphere", "To solve this problem, we need to maximize the expression \\(|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)|\\) given the conditions \\(|a| = |b| = |c| = |d| = 1\\) and \\(a + b + c + d = 0\\).\n\nHere's a step-by-step approach to solving the problem:\n\n1. **Understand the Constraints:**\n   - The magnitudes of \\(a, b, c,\\) and \\(d\\) are all 1, meaning they lie on the unit circle in the complex plane.\n   - The sum of \\(a, b, c,\\) and \\(d\\) is 0.\n\n2. **Symmetry and Geometric Interpretation:**\n   - Since \\(a, b, c,\\) and \\(d\\) lie on the unit circle and their sum is 0, they form a rectangle in the complex plane. This is because the only way four points on the unit circle can sum to 0 is if they are the vertices of a rectangle.\n\n3. **Expressing the Points:**\n   - Without loss of generality, we can express \\(a, b, c,\\) and \\(d\\) as \\(a = e^{i\\theta}, b = e^{i(\\theta + \\pi/2)}, c = e^{i(\\theta + \\pi)}, d = e^{i(\\theta + 3\\pi/2)}\\). This is because these points form a rectangle on the unit circle.\n\n4. **Calculate the Expression:**\n   - We need to calculate \\(|(a + b)(a + c)(a + d)(b + c)(b + d)(c + d)|\\).\n   - Using the symmetry of the rectangle, we can simplify the expression. For example, \\(a + b = e^{i\\theta} + e^{i(\\theta + \\pi/2)} = e^{i\\theta}(1 + e^{i\\pi/2}) = e^{i\\theta}(1 + i)\\), and similarly for the other terms.\n\n5. **Simplify the Expression:**\n   - The expression simplifies to \\(|(1 + i)(1 - 1)(1 - i)(-1 + i)(-1 - 1)(-1 - i)| = |(1 + i)(0)(1 - i)(-1 + i)(-2