on, and subtraction. We perform the operations inside the parentheses first, then multiplication and division from left to right, and finally addition and subtraction from left to right:\n   \\[\n   5 \\times (3 + 2) - 4 \\div 2 = 5 \\times 5 - 4 \\div 2 = 25 - 2 = 23\n   \\]\n   So the answer is \\(\\boxed{23}\\).\n\n3. To solve $2^3 + 3^2 - 4 \\times 2$, we follow the order of operations, which means we perform exponents first, then multiplication and division from left to right, and finally addition and subtraction from left to right:\n   \\[\n   2^3 + 3^2 - 4 \\times 2 = 8 + 9 - 8 = 9\n   \\]\n   So the answer is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).\n\n4. To solve $100 \\div 10 + 10 \\times 10$, we follow the order of operations, which means we perform division and multiplication from left to right, and then addition:\n   \\[\n   100 \\div 10 + 10 \\times 10 = 10 + 100 = 110\n   \\]\n   So the answer is \\(\\boxed{110}\\).\n\n5. To solve $12 \\times (10 + 5) \\div 3 - 2^3$, we follow the order of operations, which means we perform the operations inside the parentheses first, then exponents, then multiplication and division from left to right, and finally subtraction:\n   \\[\n   12 \\times (10 + 5) \\div 3 - 2^3 = 12 \\times 15 \\div 3 - 8 = 180 \\div 3 - 8 = 60 - 8 = 52\n   \\]\n   So the answer is \\(\\boxed{52}\\).\n\n6. To solve $2^4 + 3^3 - 4^2 \\div 2$, we follow the order of operations, which means we perform exponents first, then division, and finally addition and subtraction from left to right:\n   \\[\n   2^4 + 3^3 - 4^2 \\div 2 = 16 + 27 - 16 \\div 2 = 16 + 27 - 8 = 35\n   \\]\n   So the answer is \\(\\boxed{35}\\).\n\n7. To solve $100 \\div (10 \\div 2) + 10^2$, we follow the order of operations, which means we perform the operations inside the parentheses first, then division, and finally addition and exponentiation:\n   \\[\n   100 \\div (10 \\div 2) + 10^2 = 100 \\div 5 + 100 = 20 + 100 = 120\n   \\]\n   So the answer is \\(\\boxed{120}\\).\n\n8. To solve $2^3 \\times 3^2 - 4^2 \\div 2$, we follow the order of operations, which means we perform exponents first, then multiplication and division from left to right, and finally subtraction:\n   \\[\n   2^3 \\times 3^2 - 4^2 \\div 2 = 8 \\times 9 - 16 \\div 2 = 7', 'Find the sum of the first 10 odd numbers. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \