:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ\\right) \\cdot \\frac{3}{4}.\n   \\]\n   Therefore, we need to find the value of $\\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ$.\n\n2. **Use Product-to-Sum Identities:**\n   We can use the identity $\\sin A \\sin B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (A - B) - \\cos (A + B)]$. However, a more straightforward approach is to use the fact that the product of sines of angles in arithmetic progression can be simplified using known trigonometric identities.\n\n3. **Simplify Using Known Identities:**\n   The product of sines of angles in arithmetic progression can be simplified using the identity:\n   \\[\n   \\sin x \\sin (60^\\circ - x) \\sin (60^\\circ + x) = \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 3x.\n   \\]\n   Using this identity, we can pair the angles:\n   \\[\n   \\sin 20^\\circ \\sin 40^\\circ \\sin 80^\\circ = \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\n   \\]\n   Similarly,\n   \\[\n   \\sin 100^\\circ \\sin 140^\\circ \\sin 160^\\circ = \\frac{1}{4} \\sin 300^\\circ = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}.\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the product is:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}\\right) \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{8}\\right) = -\\frac{3}{64}.\n   \\]\n   So, the original expression becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\left(-\\frac{3}{64}\\right) \\cdot \\frac{3}{4} = -\\frac{9}{256}.\n   \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python and SymPy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the angles in degrees\nangles = [20, 40, 60, 80, 100, 120, 140, 160]\n\n# Convert degrees to radians\nangles_rad = [sp.rad(angle) for angle in angles]\n\n# Calculate the product of sines\nproduct = 