 \\mathbf{b}\\| = \\|\\mathbf{a}\\| \\|\\mathbf{b}\\| \\sin(\\theta)\n\\]\nwhere $\\theta$ is the angle between $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$. Given that $\\|\\mathbf{a}\\| = 2$, $\\|\\mathbf{b}\\| = 7$, and $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix}$, we can calculate the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}$ as follows:\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\| = \\sqrt{3^2 + 2^2 + 6^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 4 + 36} = \\sqrt{49} = 7\n\\]\nNow we can substitute the values into the formula:\n\\[\n7 = 2 \\cdot 7 \\cdot \\sin(\\theta)\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n7 = 14 \\sin(\\theta) \\implies \\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\nThe smallest possible angle $\\theta$ for which $\\sin(\\theta) = \\frac{1}{2}$ is $\\theta = 30^\\circ$. Therefore, the smallest possible angle between $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is $30$ degrees.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport math\r\n\r\n# Given values\r\nnorm_a = 2\r\nnorm_b = 7\r\ncross_product = [3, 2, 6]\r\n\r\n# Calculate the magnitude of the cross product\r\nmagnitude_cross_product = math.sqrt(cross_product[0]**2 + cross_product[1]**2 + cross_product[2]**2)\r\n\r\n# Calculate sin(theta)\r\nsin_theta = magnitude_cross_product / (norm_a * norm_b)\r\n\r\n# Calculate the smallest possible angle in degrees\r\ntheta_radians = math.asin(sin_theta)\r\ntheta_degrees = math.degrees(theta_radians)\r\n\r\nprint(theta_degrees)\n```\n```output\n30.000000000000004\n```\nThe smallest possible angle between vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is indeed $30$ degrees. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{30}\n\\]\n\nThe solution set of the inequality $x(9-x) > 0$ is ( )\nA: $(0,9)$\nB: $(9,+\\infty)$\nC: $(-\\infty,9)$\nD: $(-\\infty,0) \\cup (9,+\\infty)$\nTo solve the inequality \\(x(9-x) > 0\\), we need to determine the values of \\(x\\) for which the product \\(x(9-x)\\) is positive. This involves finding the roots of the equation \\(x(9-