nator is \\(|x^2 + 3x - 4| + |x^2 + 9x + 20|\\). For the function to be undefined, both \\(|x^2 + 3x - 4|\\) and \\(|x^2 + 9x + 20|\\) must be zero simultaneously, because the sum of two non-negative numbers can only be zero if both numbers are zero.\n\nFirst, we solve for \\(x^2 + 3x - 4 = 0\\) and \\(x^2 + 9x + 20 = 0\\).\n\n1. Solve \\(x^2 + 3x - 4 = 0\\):\n\\[x^2 + 3x - 4 = (x + 4)(x - 1) = 0\\]\nSo, \\(x = -4\\) or \\(x = 1\\).\n\n2. Solve \\(x^2 + 9x + 20 = 0\\):\n\\[x^2 + 9x + 20 = (x + 4)(x + 5) = 0\\]\nSo, \\(x = -4\\) or \\(x = -5\\).\n\nThe only value that satisfies both equations is \\(x = -4\\). Therefore, the function \\(f(x)\\) is undefined at \\(x = -4\\).\n\nThe value \\(x = 1\\) and \\(x = -5\\) do not make the denominator zero because \\(|x^2 + 9x + 20| \\neq 0\\) when \\(x = 1\\) and \\(|x^2 + 3x - 4| \\neq 0\\) when \\(x = -5\\), respectively.\n\nThus, the only value of \\(x\\) that is not in the domain of \\(f(x)\\) is \\(x = -4\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-4}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the variable and the expressions\r\nx = sp.symbols('x')\r\nexpr1 = x**2 + 3*x - 4\r\nexpr2 = x**2 + 9*x + 20\r\n\r\n# Solve for the roots of the expressions\r\nroots_expr1 = sp.solve(expr1, x)\r\nroots_expr2 = sp.solve(expr2, x)\r\n\r\n# Find the common root\r\ncommon_roots = set(roots_expr1).intersection(set(roots_expr2))\r\nprint(common_roots)\n```", "What is the domain of the function\n$f(x)=\\frac{1}{|x^2+3x-4|+|x^2+9x+20|}$? Shay\n (Express your answer as an interval or as a union of intervals, e.g., $(-\\infty, 2]\\cup(3, 6)\\cup[10,\\infty)$ or $(-5, -1)$.)\n\nAssistant: To determine the domain of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{1}{|x^2 + 3x - 4| + |x^2 + 9x + 20|} \\), we need to identify the values of \\( x \\) for which the denominator is non-zero. The denominator is the sum of two absolute value expressions, and it will be zero if and only if