ed when the denominator is zero.\n\nLet's start by factoring the quadratic expressions inside the absolute values:\n\n1. \\(x^2 + 3x - 4 = (x + 4)(x - 1)\\)\n2. \\(x^2 + 9x + 20 = (x + 4)(x + 5)\\)\n\nSo the function becomes:\n\\[ f(x) = \\frac{1}{|(x + 4)(x - 1)| + |(x + 4)(x + 5)|} \\]\n\nThe denominator \\(|(x + 4)(x - 1)| + |(x + 4)(x + 5)|\\) is zero if and only if both \\(|(x + 4)(x - 1)| = 0\\) and \\(|(x + 4)(x + 5)| = 0\\). This happens when \\(x + 4 = 0\\) or \\(x - 1 = 0\\) or \\(x + 5 = 0\\). However, since \\(|(x + 4)(x - 1)|\\) and \\(|(x + 4)(x + 5)|\\) are both non-negative, they cannot both be zero at the same time unless \\(x = -4\\).\n\nSo, we need to check if \\(x = -4\\) makes the denominator zero:\n\\[ |(x + 4)(x - 1)| + |(x + 4)(x + 5)| = |(-4 + 4)(-4 - 1)| + |(-4 + 4)(-4 + 5)| = |0 \\cdot (-5)| + |0 \\cdot 1| = 0 + 0 = 0 \\]\n\nThus, the only value of \\(x\\) that makes the denominator zero is \\(x = -4\\).\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(x\\) that is not in the domain of \\(f(x)\\) is \\(\\boxed{-4}\\).", ' 2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-01\n 1.2023-04-0