ac^2) - 6abc.\n\\]\nThis can be factored as:\n\\[\nab(a + b) + bc(b + c) + ca(c + a) - 6abc.\n\\]\nUsing \\(a + b + c = 0\\), we can substitute \\(a + b = -c\\), \\(b + c = -a\\), and \\(c + a = -b\\):\n\\[\nab(-c) + bc(-a) + ca(-b) - 6abc = -abc - abc - abc - 6abc = -9abc.\n\\]\nSince \\(abc = 1\\), we get:\n\\[\n-9abc = -9 \\cdot 1 = -9.\n\\]\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-9}.\n\\]\n\nLet's verify the calculations with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the polynomial p(x) = x^2 - 8x + 5\ndef p(x):\n    return x**2 - 8*x + 5\n\n# Compute p(3) and p(4)\np_3 = p", 'Let $a$ and $b$ be the roots of the quadratic equation $x^2-mx+2=0.$ Suppose that $a+(1/b)$ and $b+(1/a)$ are the roots of the quadratic equation $x^2-rx+s=0.$ What is $s?$\n2019 CCA Math Bonanza Individual Round #9\nLet $a$ and $b$ be the roots of the quadratic equation $x^2-mx+2=0.$ Suppose that $a+(1/b)$ and $b+(1/a)$ are the roots of the quadratic equation $x^2-rx+s=0.$ What is $s?$\n2019 CCA Math Bonanza Individual Round #9\nLet $a$ and $b$ be the roots of the quadratic equation $x^2-mx+2=0.$ Suppose that $a+(1/b)$ and $b+(1/a)$ are the roots of the quadratic equation $x^2-rx+s=0.$ What is $s?$\n2019 CCA Math Bonanza Individual Round #9\nLet $a$ and $b$ be the roots of the quadratic equation $x^2-mx+2=0.$ Suppose that $a+(1/b)$ and $b+(1/a)$ are the roots of the quadratic equation $x^2-rx+s=0.$ What is $s?$\n2019 CCA Math Bonanza Individual Round #9\nLet $a$ and $b$ be the roots of the quadratic equation $x^2-mx+2=0.$ Suppose that $a+(1/b)$ and $b+(1/a)$ are the roots of the quadratic equation $x^2-rx+s=0.$ What is $s?$\n2019 CCA Math Bonanza Individual Round #9\nLet $a$ and $b$ be the roots of the quadratic equation $x^2-mx+2=0.$ Suppose that $a+(1/b)$ and $b+(1/a)$ are the roots of the quadratic equation $x^2-rx+s=0.$ What is $s?$\n2019 CCA Math Bonanza Individual Round #9\nLet $a$ and $b$ be the roots of the quadratic equation $x^2-mx+2=0.$ Suppose that $a+(1/b)$ and $b+(1/a)$ are the roo