te \\( x = 2 \\) into the polynomial:\n   \\[\n   p(2) = 2^3 + 2 \\cdot 2^2 + 3 \\cdot 2 + 4\n   \\]\n   Calculate each term step by step:\n   \\[\n   2^3 = 8\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot 2^2 = 2 \\cdot 4 = 8\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3 \\cdot 2 = 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   4 = 4\n   \\]\n   Add these values together:\n   \\[\n   p(2) = 8 + 8 + 6 + 4 = 26\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the value of \\( p(2) \\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{26}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Finding the value of \\( \\frac{p(3)}{p(4)} \\) for the polynomial \\( p(x) = x^2 - 5x + 6 \\):**\n\n   First, find \\( p(3) \\):\n   \\[\n   p(3) = 3^2 - 5 \\cdot 3 + 6\n   \\]\n   Calculate each term:\n   \\[\n   3^2 = 9\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   5 \\cdot 3 = 15\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   6 = 6\n   \\]\n   Add these values together:\n   \\[\n   p(3) = 9 - 15 + 6 = 0\n   \\]\n\n   Next, find \\( p(4) \\):\n   \\[\n   p(4) = 4^2 - 5 \\cdot 4 + 6\n   \\]\n   Calculate each term:\n   \\[\n   4^2 = 16\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   5 \\cdot 4 = 20\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   6 = 6\n   \\]\n   Add these values together:\n   \\[\n   p(4) = 16 - 20 + 6 = 2\n   \\]\n\n   Now, compute \\( \\frac{p(3)}{p(4)} \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{p(3)}{p(4)} = \\frac{0}{2} = 0\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the value of \\( \\frac{p(3)}{p(4)} \\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{0}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Finding the value of \\( \\frac{p(3)}{p(4)} \\) for the polynomial \\( p(x) = x^3 - 3x^2 + 2x \\):**\n\n   First, find \\( p(3) \\):\n   \\[\n   p(3) = 3^3 - 3 \\cdot 3^2 + 2 \\cdot 3\n   \\]\n   Calculate each term:\n   \\[\n   3^3 = 27\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3 \\cdot 3^2 = 3 \\cdot 9 = 27\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot 3 = 6\n   \\]\n   Add these values together:\n   \\[\n   p(3) = 27 - 27 + 6 = 6\n   \\]\n\n   Next, find \\( p(4) \\):\n   \\[\n   p(4) = 4^3 - 3 \\cdot 4^2 + 2 \\cdot 4\n   \\]\n   Calculate each term:\n   \\[\n   4^3 = 64\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   3 \\cdot 4^2 = 3 \\cdot 16 = 48\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2 \\', 'Let $p(x)$ be a polynomial with integer coefficients such that $p(2007) = 36.$  If $p(2011) = 36,$ c