ac{-5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nFrom the first row, we get:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)x + 7y = 0 \\implies x = \\frac{-14}{5 + i\\sqrt{3}} y = \\frac{-14(5 - i\\sqrt{3})}{28} y = \\frac{-5 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2} y', 'To find \\(\\mathbf{A}^{27} + \\mathbf{A}^{31} + \\mathbf{A}^{40}\\), we will first need to determine the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}\\). This will help us to diagonalize the matrix, which will make it easier to compute high powers of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\).\n\nThe matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{A} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\n### Step 1: Find the eigenvalues of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\)\nThe eigenvalues \\(\\lambda\\) of \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) are the roots of the characteristic polynomial, which is given by:\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I}) = 0\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathbf{I}\\) is the identity matrix. So we need to solve:\n\\[\n\\det \\begin{pmatrix} 3 - \\lambda & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 - \\lambda \\end{pmatrix} = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n(3 - \\lambda)(-2 - \\lambda) + 7 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n-6 - 3\\lambda + 2\\lambda + \\lambda^2 + 7 = 0\n\\]\n\\[\n\\lambda^2 - \\lambda + 1 = 0\n\\]\nSolving this quadratic equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\lambda = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{1 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2} = \\frac{1 \\pm i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\nSo the eigenvalues are \\(\\lambda_1 = \\frac{1 + i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\) and \\(\\lambda_2 = \\frac{1 - i\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\).\n\n### Step 2: Diagonalize the matrix \\(\\mathbf{A}\\)\nWe can diagonalize \\(\\mathbf{A}\\) as \\(\\mathbf{A} = \\mathbf{P} \\mathbf{D} \\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\), where \\(\\mathbf{D}\\) is the diagonal matrix of eigenvalues and \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is the matrix of eigenvectors.\n\nThe eigenvectors corresponding to \\(\\lambda_1\\) and \\(\\lambda_2\\) can be found by solving \\((\\mathbf{A} - \\lambda \\mathbf{I}) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\\).\n\nF