es y = 12\n\\]\n\nSo, \\(b = 12\\).\n\nNow we can calculate \\(a + b + m\\):\n\n\\[\na + b + m = 4 + 12 - 3 = 13\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the values of a, b, and m\na = 4  # x-intercept\nb = 12  # y-intercept\nm = -3  # slope\n\n# Calculate a + b + m\nresult = a + b + m\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n13\n```\nThe value of \\(a + b + m\\) is \\(\\boxed{13}\\).", 'Given that $x$ and $y$ are positive real numbers such that $x^2 + y^2 = 5xy$, what is the largest possible value of $\\frac{x+y}{x-y}$?  \\boxed{}. \\rangle\n \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}} \\rangle\n\\boxed{\\frac{1