six cookies that can be selected is \\(\\boxed{28}\\).\n\nGiven that $a$ and $b$ are two propositions, if $a$ is a sufficient condition for $b$, then $\\lnot a$ is a \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ condition for $\\lnot b$ (Fill in the blank with "sufficient condition", "necessary condition", or "necessary and sufficient condition").\nTo solve this problem, we need to understand the logical relationship between the propositions \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). If \\(a\\) is a sufficient condition for \\(b\\), then the logical implication \\(a \\Rightarrow b\\) is true. This means that whenever \\(a\\) is true, \\(b\\) must also be true.\n\nThe contrapositive of \\(a \\Rightarrow b\\) is \\(\\lnot b \\Rightarrow \\lnot a\\). This means that if \\(b\\) is false, then \\(a\\) must also be false. Therefore, \\(\\lnot a\\) is a necessary condition for \\(\\lnot b\\).\n\nLet\'s use Python and sympy to confirm this reasoning:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the propositions\na, b = sp.symbols(\'a b\')\n\n# Define the implication a -> b\nimplication = sp.Implies(a, b)\n\n# Get the contrapositive of the implication\ncontrapositive = sp.Implies(~b, ~a)\n\n# Check if the contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original implication\nequivalence = sp.simplify(sp.Equivalent(implication, contrapositive))\n\n# Print the result of the equivalence check\nprint(equivalence)\n```\n```output\nTrue\n```\nThe output `True` confirms that the contrapositive of the implication \\(a \\Rightarrow b\\) is logically equivalent to the original implication. This means that if \\(a\\) is a sufficient condition for \\(b\\), then \\(\\lnot a\\) is a necessary condition for \\(\\lnot b\\).\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{necessary condition}}\\).\n\nIf the complex number $z$ satisfies $|z-1-i|=1$, then the maximum value of $|z-4-5i|$ is ( )\n\nA: $4$\n\nB: $5$\n\nC: $6$\n\nD: $\\sqrt{41}$\nTo solve this problem, we need to understand the geometric interpretation of the given conditions.\n\n1. 