found using the formula for the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence:\n\\[ a_n = a_1 + (n-1)d \\]\nwhere \\(a_n\\) is the \\(n\\)-th term, \\(a_1\\) is the first term, \\(d\\) is the common difference, and \\(n\\) is the number of terms.\n\nFor our sequence:\n- \\(a_n = 99\\)\n- \\(a_1 = 3\\)\n- \\(d = 3\\)\n\nWe can solve for \\(n\\):\n\\[ 99 = 3 + (n-1) \\cdot 3 \\]\n\\[ 99 = 3 + 3n - 3 \\]\n\\[ 99 = 3n \\]\n\\[ n = 33 \\]\n\nSo, there are 33 multiples of 3 from 1 to 100.\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the probability\nThe probability \\(P\\) is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of possible outcomes:\n\\[ P = \\frac{\\text{Number of multiples of 3}}{\\text{Total number of integers from 1 to 100}} = \\frac{33}{100} \\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python and SymPy:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Total number of integers from 1 to 100\ntotal_numbers = 100\n\n# Number of multiples of 3 from 1 to 100\nmultiples_of_3 = total_numbers // 3\n\n# Probability calculation\nprobability = sp.Rational(multiples_of_3, total_numbers)\n\nprint(probability)\n```\n```output\n33/100\n```\nThe probability that a number selected at random from 1 to 100 is a multiple of 3 is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{33}{100}}\\).", 'A right triangle has side lengths 6 cm, 8 cm, and 10 cm.  A similar right triangle has a shortest side length of 15 cm.  What is the perimeter of the larger triangle?  \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\box