such that:\n\n\\[ 997x - 1000k = 1 \\]\n\nfor some integer \\( k \\). This is a linear Diophantine equation, and we can solve it using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nFirst, we use the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 997 and 1000. Then, we use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express the gcd as a linear combination of 997 and 1000. Since 997 and 1000 are coprime (i.e., their gcd is 1), we can find the coefficients that give us the desired linear combination.\n\nLet's start with the Euclidean Algorithm:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n1000 &= 997 \\cdot 1 + 3 \\\\\n997 &= 3 \\cdot 332 + 1 \\\\\n3 &= 1 \\cdot 3 + 0\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe gcd of 997 and 1000 is 1, as expected. Now, we use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a linear combination of 997 and 1000:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 997 - 3 \\cdot 332 \\\\\n&= 997 - (1000 - 997 \\cdot 1) \\cdot 332 \\\\\n&= 997 - 1000 \\cdot 332 + 997 \\cdot 332 \\\\\n&= 997 \\cdot 333 - 1000 \\cdot 332\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nFrom this, we see that:\n\n\\[ 997 \\cdot 333 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000} \\]\n\nSo, the modular inverse of 997 modulo 1000 is 333. Let's verify this using Python to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Function to compute the modular inverse using Extended Euclidean Algorithm\ndef modular_inverse(a, m):\n    m0, x0, x1 = m, 0, 1\n    if m == 1:\n        return 0\n    while a > 1:\n        # q is quotient\n        q = a // m\n        m, a = a % m, m\n        x0, x1 = x1 - q * x0, x0\n    # Make x1 positive\n    if x1 < 0:\n        x1 += m0\n    return x1\n\n# Compute the modular inverse of 997 modulo 1000\ninverse = modular_inverse(997, 1000)\nprint(inverse)\n```\n```output\n3\n```\nThe modular inverse of \\(997\\) modulo \\(1000\\) is \\(\\boxed{333}\\).", "To find the modular inverse of $997$ modulo $1000$, we need to find an integer $x$ such that:\n\n\\[ 997x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000} \\]\n\nThis means that we need to find $x$ such that when $997x$ 