ength of the larger nonagon (original + 2 units on each side)\nside_length_larger = side_length_original + 2\n\n# Perimeter of the larger nonagon\nperimeter_larger = num_sides * side_length_larger\n\n# Additional perimeter contributed by the band around the nonagon\nadditional_perimeter = perimeter_larger - perimeter_original\n\n# Total perimeter of the set S\ntotal_perimeter_S = perimeter_original + additional_perimeter\n\nprint(total_perimeter_S)\n```\n```output\n36\n```\nThe total perimeter of the set \\( S \\) is \\(\\boxed{36}\\) units.', 'To find the perimeter of the set \\( S \\), we need to understand the geometry of the problem. The set \\( S \\) consists of all points inside a regular nonagon with side length 2 units and all points that are less than 1 unit away from a point on the perimeter of the nonagon. Essentially, this means that the set \\( S \\) is the region enclosed by the original nonagon and an additional region that extends 1 unit outward from each side of the nonagon.\n\nThe perimeter of \\( S \\) will be the sum of the perimeters of the original nonagon and the additional regions that extend outward. Since the additional regions are essentially a "buffer" of width 1 unit around the nonagon, the perimeter of \\( S \\) will be the perimeter of the original nonagon plus the perimeter of a nonagon with side length 4 units (since each side of the original nonagon has a buffer of 1 unit on each side).\n\nThe perimeter of a regular nonagon with side length \\( a \\) is \\( 9a \\). So, the perimeter of the original nonagon with side length 2 units is \\( 9 \\times 2 = 18 \\) units. The perimeter of the nonagon with side length 4 units is \\( 9 \\times 4 = 36 \\) units. Therefore, the total perimeter of \\( S \\) is \\( 18 + 36 = 54 \\) units.\n\nLet\'s confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the side lengths of the original and extended nonagons\nside_length_original = 2\nside_length_extended = 4\n\n# Calculate the perimeter of the original nonagon\nperimeter_original = 9 * side_length_original\n\n# Calculate the perimeter of the extended nonagon\nperimeter_extended = 9 * side_length_extended\n\n# Calculate the total perimeter of the set S\nperimeter_S = perimeter_original + perimeter_extended\nprint(perimeter_S)\n```\n```output\n54\n```\nThe perimeter of the set \\( S \\) is \\(\\boxed{54}\\) units.', 'To solve this problem, we need to understand the geometric shape formed by the union of the set of all points inside a regular nonagon with side length 2 units and the set of all points less than 1 unit away from a point on the perimeter of the nonagon. This union forms a region that includes the nonagon itself and a 1-unit wide "band" around the nonagon.\n\nThe perimeter of this region will be the perimeter of the original nonagon plus the additional perimeter contributed by the 1-unit wide band. The nonagon has 9 sides, each of length 2 units, so the perimeter of the nonagon is \\(9 \\times 2 = 18\\) units.\n\nThe 1-unit wide band around the nonagon adds an additional perimeter equal to the perimeter of a nonagon with side length 2 + 2 \\times 1 = 4 units (since the band extends 1 unit on each side of the original nonagon). The perimeter of this larger nonagon is \\(9 \\times 4 = 36\\) units.\n\nHowever, we must subtract the perimeter of the original nonagon from this total because it is counted twice (once in the original nonagon and once in the band). So, the additional perimeter contributed by the band is \\(36 - 18 = 18\\) units.\n\nTherefore, the total perimeter of the region \\(S\\) is the perimeter of the original nonagon plus the additional perimeter contributed by the band, which is \\(18 + 18 = 36\\) units.\n\nLet\'s confirm this with a Python code implementation to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the side length of the original nonagon\nside_length_original = 2\n# Define the number of sides of the nonagon\nnum_sides = 9\n\n# Calculate the perimeter of the original nonagon\nperimeter_original = side_length_original * num_sides\n\n# Define the side length of the larger nonagon formed by the 1-unit wide band\nside_length_larger = side_length_original + 2 * 1\n\n# Calculate the perimeter of the larger nonagon\nperimeter_larger = side_length_larger * num_sides\n\n# Calculate the additional perimeter contributed by the band\nadditional_perimeter_band = perimeter_larger - perimeter_original\n\n# Calculate the total perimeter of the region S\ntotal_perimeter_S = perimeter_original + additional_perimeter_band\n\nprint(total_perimeter_S)\n```\n```output\n36\n```\nThe perimeter of the region \\(S\\) is \\(\\boxed{36}\\) units.', "To solve the problem, we need to understand the geometry of the set $S$. The set $S$ consists of two parts:\n\n1. The interior of a regular nonagon with side length 2 units.\n2. The set of all points that are less than 1 unit away from a point on the perimeter of the nonagon. This forms a region that extends 1 unit outward from the perimeter of the nonagon.\n\nThe perimeter of $S$ will be the sum of the following:\n1. The perimeter of the outer boundary formed by extending the sides of the nonagon outward by 1 unit.\n2. The perimeter of the nonagon itself (since the points less than 1 unit away from the perimeter include the perimeter itself).\n\nSince the nonagon has 9 sides, each of length 2 units, the perimeter of the nonagon is $9 \\times 2 = 18$ units. When we extend each side outward by 1 unit, we form a parallel side that is also 2 units long, but we need to account for the curvature at the corners. Each corner of the nonagon will contribute an additional arc length of 1 unit (since the radius of the arc is 1 unit and the angle at each corner of the nonagon is $\\frac{2\\pi}{9}$ radians, the arc length is $1 \\times \\frac{2\\pi}{9} = \\frac{2\\pi}{9}$ units).\n\nSince there are 9 corners, the total additional arc length contributed by all the corners is $9 \\times \\frac{2\\pi}{9} = 2\\pi$ units. Therefore, the perimeter of the outer boundary is $9 \\times 2 + 2\\pi = 18 + 2\\pi$ units.\n\nAdding the perimeter of the nonagon itself, the total perimeter of $S$ is $18 + 18 + 2\\pi = 36 + 2\\pi$ units.\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Side length of the nonagon\nside_length = 2\n# Number of sides of the nonagon\nnum_sides = 9\n# Radius of the arcs at each corner\nradius = 1\n\n# Perimeter of the nonagon\nperimeter_nonagon = num_sides * side_length\n\n# Arc length at each corner\narc_length_per_corner = radius * (2 * math.pi / num_sides)\n# Total arc length for all corners\ntotal_arc_length = num_sides * arc_length_per_corner\n\n# Perimeter of the outer boundary\nperimeter_outer_boundary = num_sides * side_length + total_arc_length\n\n# Total perimeter of S\ntotal_perimeter_S = perimeter_nonagon + perimeter_outer_boundary\nprint(total_perimeter_S)\n```\n```output\n42.283185307179586\n```\nThe total perimeter of $S$ is $36 + 2\\pi$ units. When evaluated numerically, this is approximately $42.283$ units. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{36 + 2\\pi}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to understand the shape of $S$. The set $S$ consists of all points inside a regular nonagon with side length 2 units and all points less than 1 unit away from a point on the perimeter of the nonagon. This means that $S$ is the region enclosed by a nonagon with side length 2 units and an additional 1 unit buffer around it. Essentially, $S$ is a nonagon with a side length of 2 + 2*1 = 4 units (since the buffer adds 1 unit on both sides of each edge).\n\nThe perimeter of a regular nonagon is simply the number of sides times the length of each side. Since a nonagon has 9 sides, the perimeter of $S$ is $9 \\times 4 = 36$ units.\n\nLet's confirm this with a simple Python code to ensure the calculation is accurate.\n```python\n# Number of sides of the nonagon\nn_sides = 9\n\n# Side length of the nonagon with the buffer\nside_length = 4\n\n# 