can be expressed as the square of an integer. The perfect squares less than 53 are 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, and 49. Since 53 is not in this list, it is not a perfect square.\n\nNext, we need to check if 53 can be factored into a product of a perfect square and another number. We do this by checking the prime factorization of 53. The number 53 is a prime number, which means its only factors are 1 and 53. Since 53 cannot be factored into a product of a perfect square and another number (other than 1 and 53 itself), the square root of 53 cannot be simplified further.\n\nTherefore, the square root of 53 in simplest radical form is \\(\\sqrt{53}\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{53}}\\).", "To find the simplest radical form of $\\sqrt{53}$, we need to determine if 53 can be factored into a perfect square and another integer. If it can, we can simplify the radical. Otherwise, $\\sqrt{53}$ is already in its simplest form.\n\nLet's check the prime factorization of 53. If 53 is a prime number, then it cannot be factored into a perfect square and another integer, and thus $\\sqrt{53}$ is in its simplest form.\n\nWe can use Python to check if 53 is a prime number and thus determine the simplest radical form of $\\sqrt{53}$.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Function to check if a number is prime\ndef is_prime(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return False\n    if n <= 3:\n        return True\n    if n % 2 == 0 or n % 3 == 0:\n        return False\n    i = 5\n    while i * i <= n:\n        if n % i == 0 or n % (i + 2) == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 6\n    return True\n\n# Check if 53 is prime\nis_53_prime = is_prime(53)\nprint(is_53_prime)\n```\n```output\nTrue\n```\nSince 53 is a prime number, it cannot be factored into a perfect square and another integer. Therefore, $\\sqrt{53}$ is already in its simplest radical form.\n\nThe simplest radical form of $\\sqrt{53}$ is $\\boxed{\\sqrt{53}}$.", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the simplest radical form of