sors other than 1 and itself. Therefore, it cannot be factored into a perfect square and another integer. This means that $\\sqrt{53}$ is already in its simplest radical form.\n\nWe can confirm this by checking if 53 has any perfect square factors other than 1. Since 53 is a prime number, it does not have any perfect square factors other than 1. Therefore, $\\sqrt{53}$ is already in its simplest radical form.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{\\sqrt{53}}$.\n\nLet\'s write the Python code to confirm this.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Function to check if a number is a perfect square\ndef is_perfect_square(n):\n    return int(math.isqrt(n))**2 == n\n\n# Check if 53 has any perfect square factors other than 1\ndef simplest_radical_form(n):\n    for i in range(2, int(math.isqrt(n)) + 1):\n        if n % (i**2) == 0:\n            return f"sqrt({i**2} * {n // (i**2)})"\n    return f"sqrt({n})"\n\n# Find the simplest radical form of sqrt(53)\nresult = simplest_radical_form(53)\nprint(result)\n```', 'What is the simplest radical form of $\\sqrt{53}$? \\boxed{\\sqrt{53}}', 'What is $\\sqrt{53}$ in simplest radical form? \\boxed{} \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\boxed{}. \\box