ight)^{55} = \\left(\\dfrac{37}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55}. \\]\n\n3. Combine the fractions raised to the same power:\n\\[ \\left(\\dfrac{37}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55} = \\left(\\dfrac{37}{8} \\cdot \\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55} = \\left(\\dfrac{37 \\cdot 8}{8 \\cdot 37}\\right)^{55} = \\left(\\dfrac{296}{296}\\right)^{55} = 1^{55} = 1. \\]\n\nSo the final answer is \\(1\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the fractions\nfraction1 = 37 / 8\nfraction2 = 8 / 37\n\n# Raise to the 55th power and multiply\nresult = (fraction1 ** 55) * (fraction2 ** 55)\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n0.9999999999999942\n```\nThe result from the Python code is approximately \\(1\\), confirming our analytical solution. The minor discrepancy is due to floating-point precision limitations in numerical computations.\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).", "To solve the problem \\(\\left(4\\dfrac{5}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55}\\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Convert the mixed number \\(4\\dfrac{5}{8}\\) to an improper fraction.\n2. Simplify the expression using the properties of exponents.\n3. Calculate the final value.\n\nLet's break it down step-by-step.\n\n1. Convert the mixed number \\(4\\dfrac{5}{8}\\) to an improper fraction:\n\\[ 4\\dfrac{5}{8} = 4 + \\dfrac{5}{8} = \\dfrac{32}{8} + \\dfrac{5}{8} = \\dfrac{37}{8}. \\]\n\n2. Substitute this into the expression:\n\\[ \\left(4\\dfrac{5}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55} = \\left(\\dfrac{37}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55}. \\]\n\n3. Use the property of exponents \\((a/b)^n \\cdot (b/a)^n = 1\\):\n\\[ \\left(\\dfrac{37}{8}\\right)^{55} \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55} = \\left(\\dfrac{37}{8} \\cdot \\dfrac{8}{37}\\right)^{55} = 1^{55} = 1. \\]\n\nSo, the final answer is \\(1\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# 