}{3}\\), we need to use some trigonometric identities and properties of inverse trigonometric functions.\n\nFirst, we know that \\(\\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = \\tan^{-1} (x)\\). This is because the cotangent and tangent functions are reciprocals of each other, and their inverse functions reflect this relationship.\n\nSo the equation simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( \\tan^{-1} (x) + \\tan^{-1} (x) \\right) = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nThis further simplifies to:\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( 2 \\tan^{-1} (x) \\right) = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nUsing the double-angle identity for sine, we know that:\n\\[\n\\sin (2 \\theta) = \\frac{2 \\tan \\theta}{1 + \\tan^2 \\theta}\n\\]\nIn this case, \\(\\theta = \\tan^{-1} (x)\\), so \\(\\tan \\theta = x\\). Therefore:\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( 2 \\tan^{-1} (x) \\right) = \\frac{2x}{1 + x^2}\n\\]\nSo the equation becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{2x}{1 + x^2} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nMultiplying both sides by \\(3(1 + x^2)\\) gives:\n\\[\n6x = 1 + x^2\n\\]\nRearranging terms, we get a quadratic equation:\n\\[\nx^2 - 6x + 1 = 0\n\\]\nWe can solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -6\\), and \\(c = 1\\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{32}}{2} = \\frac{6 \\pm 4\\sqrt{2}}{2} = 3 \\pm 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\nSo the solutions are:\n\\[\nx = 3 + 2\\sqrt{2}, \\quad x = 3 - 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify these solutions using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport math\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Define the equation\nequation = sp.sin(2 * sp.atan(x)) - 1/3\n\n# Solve the equation\nsolutions = sp.solve(equation, x)\nprint(solutions)\n```\n```output\n[0.171572875253810, 5.82842712474619]\n```\nThe solutions obtained from solving the equation \\(\\sin \\left( \\tan^{-1} (x) + \\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\right) = \\frac{1}{3}\\) are \\(x = 3 + 2\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(x = 3 - 2\\sqrt{2}\\), which approximately equal \\(5.828\\) and \\(0.172\\) respectively.\n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3 + 2\\sqrt{2}, 3 - 2\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]", "To solve the equation \\(\\sin \\left( \\tan^{-1} (x) + \\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\right) = \\frac{1}{3}\\), we need to understand the trigonometric identities involved. We know that \\(\\cot^{-1} \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right) = \\tan^{-1} (x)\\). Therefore, the equation simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( \\tan^{-1} (x) + \\tan^{-1} (x) \\right) = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nThis further simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( 2 \\tan^{-1} (x) \\right) = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nUsing the double-angle identity for sine, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sin (2 \\theta) = 2 \\sin (\\theta) \\cos (\\theta)\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(\\theta = \\tan^{-1} (x)\\), so \\(\\sin (\\theta) = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1 + x^2}}\\) and \\(\\cos (\\theta) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + x^2}}\\). Therefore:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\left( 2 \\tan^{-1} (x) \\right) = 2 \\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1 + x^2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 + x^2}} = \\frac{2x}{1 + x^2}\n\\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2x}{1 + x^2} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying gives:\n\n\\[\n6x = 1 + x^2\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 6x + 1 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -6\\), and \\(c = 1\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{36 - 4}}{2} = \\frac{6 \\pm \\sqrt{32}}{2} = \\frac{6 \\pm 4\\sqrt{2}}{2} = 3 \\pm 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nSo the solutions are \\(x = 3 + 2\\sqrt{2}\\) and \\(x = 3 - 2\\sqrt{2}\\). Let's verify these solutions using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport math\r\n\r\n# Define the solutions\r\nx1 = 3 + 2 * math.sqrt(2)\r\nx2 = 3 - 2 * math.sqrt(2)\r\n\r\n# Verify the solutions\r\ndef verify_solution(x):\r\n    return math.sin(2 * math.atan(x)) == 1/3\r\n\r\n# Check if the solutions