 = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\\[ x \\ln(1 + 2r) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\\[ x \\ln(1 + \\sqrt{r}) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\\[ x \\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right) = \\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right) \\]\n\nSo, the value of \\(x\\) for each equation is:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(1 + r)} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\right)} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(1 + 2r)} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln(1 + \\sqrt{r})} \\]\n\\[ x = \\frac{\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\ln\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\right)} \\]\n\nSince \\(\\ln\\left(\\frac{7}{3}\\right)\\) is a constant, the value of \\(x\\) will be largest when the denominator \\(\\ln(1 + a)\\) is smallest, where \\(a\\) is the argument of the logarithm. Since \\(0 < r < 3\\), we can compare the values of \\(1 + r\\), \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\), \\(1 + 2r\\), \\(1 + \\sqrt{r}\\), and \\(1 + \\frac{1}{r}\\).\n\nLet's evaluate these expressions for a typical value of \\(r\\) (e.g., \\(r = 1\\)):\n\n\\[ 1 + r = 2 \\]\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{r}{10} = 1.1 \\]\n\\[ 1 + 2r = 3 \\]\n\\[ 1 + \\sqrt{r} = 2 \\]\n\\[ 1 + \\frac{1}{r} = 2 \\]\n\nThe smallest value among these is \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10} = 1.1\\). Therefore, the largest value of \\(x\\) will be given by the equation where the base of the logarithm in the denominator is \\(1 + \\frac{r}{10}\\).\n\nThus, the equation with the largest solution \\(x\\) is:\n\n\\(\\textbf{(B)}\\ 3(1 + r/10)^x = 7\\)\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Define the value of r\nr = 1\n\n# Calculate the values of the bases\nbase1 = 1 + r\nbase2 = 1 + r / 10\nbase3 = 1 + 2 * r\nbase4 = 1 + math.sqrt(r)\nbase5 = 1 + 1 / r\n\n# Calculate the natural logarithms of the bases\nln_base1 = math.log(base1)\nln_base2 = math.log(base2)\nln_base3 = math.log(base3)\nln_base4 = math.log(base4)\nln_base5 = math.log(base5)