uation in terms of \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n(1 + r)^x &= \\frac{7}{3} \\\\\n(1 + r/10)^x &= \\frac{7}{3} \\\\\n(1 + 2r)^x &= \\frac{7}{3} \\\\\n(1 + \\sqrt{r})^x &= \\frac{7}{3} \\\\\n(1 + 1/r)^x &= \\frac{7}{3}\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nSince the right-hand side is the same for all equations, the value of \\( x \\) will be determined by the base of the exponent. The larger the base, the smaller the value of \\( x \\). So, we need to compare the bases:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n1 + r & \\quad \\text{(A)} \\\\\n1 + r/10 & \\quad \\text{(B)} \\\\\n1 + 2r & \\quad \\text{(C)} \\\\\n1 + \\sqrt{r} & \\quad \\text{(D)} \\\\\n1 + 1/r & \\quad \\text{(E)}\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( 0 < r < 3 \\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n1 + r & < 1 + 2r & \\text{(since } r < 2r \\text{)} \\\\\n1 + r/10 & < 1 + r & \\text{(since } r/10 < r \\text{)} \\\\\n1 + \\sqrt{r} & < 1 + 2r & \\text{(since } \\sqrt{r} < 2r \\text{ for } 0 < r < 3 \\text{)} \\\\\n1 + 1/r & > 1 + r & \\text{(since } 1/r > r \\text{ for } 0 < r < 1 \\text{ and } 1/r < r \\text{ for } 1 < r < 3 \\text{)}\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the largest base is \\( 1 + 1/r \\), so the equation with the smallest \\( x \\) is \\(\\text{(E)}\\). Consequently, the equation with the largest \\( x \\) is \\(\\text{(A)}\\).\n\n### Problem 2: Largest Possible Value of \\(\\min\\left\\{\\frac{a}{b + c}, \\frac{b}{a + c}, \\frac{c}{a + b}\\right\\}\\)\n\nLet's denote \\( x = \\min\\left\\{\\frac{a}{b + c}, \\frac{b}{a + c}, \\frac{c}{a + b}\\right\\} \\). We need to find the maximum possible value of \\( x \\).\n\nIf \\( x \\) is the minimum, then:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nx & \\leq \\frac{a}{b + c} \\\\\nx & \\leq \\frac{b}{a + c} \\\\\nx & \\leq \\frac{c}{a + b}\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nSumming these inequalities, we get:\n\n\\[\n3x \\leq \\frac", "We need to find the equation that has the largest solution for \\(x\\) when \\(0 < r < 3\\). To do this, we can solve each equation for \\(x\\) and compare the solution