tation. Specifically, we can use the distributive property of the cross product over vector addition and the scalar multiplication property.\n\nThe cross product distributes over vector addition, so we have:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a}) \\times (3 \\mathbf{c} + \\mathbf{a}) = (2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) + (-\\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) + (-\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a}).\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this expression using the properties of the cross product:\n1. The cross product of a vector with itself is zero: \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}\\).\n2. The cross product is anti-commutative: \\(\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = -(\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a})\\).\n\nSo, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n(2 \\mathbf{b} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) + (2 \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}) + (-\\mathbf{a} \\times 3 \\mathbf{c}) = 6 (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) - 2 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}) - 3 (\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}).\n\\]\n\nNow we can substitute the given values:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} - 2 \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} - 3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNow we can perform the scalar multiplication and vector addition:\n\\[\n6 \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 18 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -42 \\\\ 108 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad -2 \\begin{pmatrix} 6 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad -3 \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -6 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nAdding these vectors together, we get:\n\\[\n\\beg