qrt(3) * a**2\n\n# Side length of the smaller tetrahedron\nb = a / 2\n\n# Surface area of the smaller tetrahedron\nsmaller_surface_area = math.sqrt(3) * b**2\n\n# Surface area of the larger piece\nlarger_surface_area = original_surface_area - smaller_surface_area + smaller_surface_area\n\n# Surface area of the smaller piece\nsmaller_piece_surface_area = smaller_surface_area\n\nprint(((larger_surface_area, smaller_piece_surface_area)))\n```\n```output\n(6.928203230275509, 1.7320508075688772)\n```\nThe surface area of one of the pieces (the larger piece) is $4\\sqrt{3} \\approx 6.928$ and the surface area of the smaller piece is $\\sqrt{3} \\approx 1.732$. Therefore, the surface area of one of these pieces is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]", "To solve this problem, we need to understand the geometry of the tetrahedron and the plane that cuts it. The plane parallel to edges \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\) and lying halfway between them will cut the tetrahedron into two pieces. One of these pieces will be a smaller tetrahedron and the other will be a frustum-like shape.\n\nThe plane cuts the tetrahedron into two pieces, one of which is a smaller tetrahedron with half the height of the original tetrahedron. Since the original tetrahedron has a side length of 2, the smaller tetrahedron will have a side length of 1 (since the plane is halfway between the edges).\n\nThe surface area of a regular tetrahedron with side length \\(a\\) is given by the formula:\n\\[ \\text{Surface Area} = \\sqrt{3} a^2 \\]\n\nFor the smaller tetrahedron with side length 1, the surface area is:\n\\[ \\text{Surface Area}_{\\text{small}} = \\sqrt{3} \\cdot 1^2 = \\sqrt{3} \\]\n\nHowever, the problem asks for the surface area of one of the pieces, which includes the area of the new face created by the cut. The new face is a parallelogram (actually a rectangle in this case because the plane is parallel to the edges and the tetrahedron is regular), and its area can be calculated as follows:\n\nThe height of 