nFor the fractions $\\frac{7}{30}$ and $\\frac{10}{21}$, we have $m = 7$, $n = 30$, $p = 10$, and $q = 21$. Substituting these values into the operation, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21} = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{21}{30}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFirst, we simplify the fraction $\\frac{21}{30}$ to its simplest form. The greatest common divisor (gcd) of 21 and 30 is 3, so:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{21}{30} = \\frac{21 \\div 3}{30 \\div 3} = \\frac{7}{10}\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute this back into the operation:\n\n\\[\n(7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = (7)(7) = 49\n\\]\n\nSo the simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}$ is 49. Let's verify this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\n\n# Define the fractions\nm, n = 7, 30\np, q = 10, 21\n\n# Calculate the simplified value of the operation\nresult = (m * p * (q // gcd(q, n))) // (n // gcd(q, n))\n\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n49\n```\nThe simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}$ is \\(\\boxed{49}\\).", 'To solve the problem, we need to use the given operation definition and apply it to the fractions $\\frac{7}{30}$ and $\\frac{10}{21}$. The operation is defined as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{m}{n} @ \\frac{p}{q} = (m)(p)\\left(\\frac{q}{n}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFor the fractions $\\frac{7}{30}$ and $\\frac{10}{21}$, we can identify the values of $m$, $n$, $p$, and $q$ as follows:\n- $m = 7$\n- $n = 30$\n- $p = 10$\n- $q = 21$\n\nSubstituting these values into the operation definition, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21} = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{21}{30}\\right)\n\\]\n\nNow we need to compute the value of the expression:\n\n\\[\n(7)(10)\\left(\\frac{21}{30}\\right) = 70 \\cdot \\frac{21}{30} = \\frac{70 \\cdot 21}{30} = \\frac{1470}{30}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the fraction $\\frac{1470}{30}$. We can do this by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by their greatest common divisor, which is 30:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1470 \\div 30}{30 \\div 30} = \\frac{49}{1} = 49\n\\]\n\nSo the simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}$ is 49. Let\'s confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\n\n# Given fractions\nm, n = 7, 30\np, q = 10, 21\n\n# Applying the operation definition\nresult = (m * p * q) // n\n\n# Simplifying the fraction if necessary\nnumerator = m * p * q\ndenominator = n\ncommon_divisor = gcd(numerator, denominator)\nsimplified_numerator = numerator // common_divisor\nsimplified_denominator = denominator // common_divisor\n\n# Since the denominator is 1, the result is just the numerator\nsimplified_result = simplified_numerator if simplified_denominator == 1 else f"{simplified_numerator}/{simplified_denominator}"\n\nprint(simplified_result)\n```\n```output\n49\n```\nThe simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}$ is \\(\\boxed{49}\\).', "To solve the problem, we need to follow the definition of the operation @. The operation is defined as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{m}{n} @ \\frac{p}{q} = (m)(p)\\left(\\frac{q}{n}\\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven the fractions $\\frac{7}{30}$ and $\\frac{10}{21}$, we can substitute $m = 7$, $n = 30$, $p = 10$, and $q = 21$ into the formula. This gives us:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21} = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{21}{30}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFirst, we simplify $\\frac{21}{30}$ to its simplest form. The greatest common divisor (GCD) of 21 and 30 is 3, so we divide both the numerator and the denominator by 3:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{21}{30} = \\frac{21 \\div 3}{30 \\div 3} = \\frac{7}{10}\n\\]\n\nNow we can substitute this simplified fraction back into the formula:\n\n\\[\n(7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = 7 \\cdot 10 \\cdot \\frac{7}{10} = 7 \\cdot 7 = 49\n\\]\n\nSo the simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}$ is $49$. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\n\n# Define the fractions\nm, n = 7, 30\np, q = 10, 21\n\n# Simplify q/n\ngcd_qn = gcd(q, n)\nsimplified_qn = (q // gcd_qn, n // gcd_qn)\n\n# Calculate the result of the operation\nresult = m * p * (simplified_qn[0] / simplified_qn[1])\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n49.0\n```\nThe simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}$ is \\(\\boxed{49}\\).", 'The operation @ is defined as $\\frac{m}{n}@\\frac{p}{q} = (m)(p)\\left(\\frac{q}{n}\\right)$ for simplified fractions $\\frac{p}{q}$.  What is the simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30}@\\frac{10}{21}$? \\boxed{\\text{Your answer}}', "To solve the problem, we need to follow the definition of the operation @. According to the definition, for fractions $\\frac{m}{n}$ and $\\frac{p}{q}$, the operation is defined as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{m}{n} @ \\frac{p}{q} = (m)(p)\\left(\\frac{q}{n}\\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven the fractions $\\frac{7}{30}$ and $\\frac{10}{21}$, we can substitute $m = 7$, $n = 30$, $p = 10$, and $q = 21$ into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21} = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{21}{30}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFirst, we simplify $\\frac{21}{30}$ to its simplest form. The greatest common divisor (GCD) of 21 and 30 is 3, so:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{21}{30} = \\frac{21 \\div 3}{30 \\div 3} = \\frac{7}{10}\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute this back into the formula:\n\n\\[\n(7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = 7 \\cdot 7 = 49\n\\]\n\nSo the simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}$ is 49. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\n\n# Define the fractions\nm, n = 7, 30\np, q = 10, 21\n\n# Simplify q/n\ngcd_q_n = gcd(q, n)\nsimplified_q_n = (q // gcd_q_n) / (n // gcd_q_n)\n\n# Calculate the result of the operation @\nresult = m * p * simplified_q_n\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n49.0\n```\nThe simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}$ is \\(\\boxed{49}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to use the given operation definition and apply it to the fractions $\\frac{7}{30}$ and $\\frac{10}{21}$. The operation is defined as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{m}{n} @ \\frac{p}{q} = (m)(p)\\left(\\frac{q}{n}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFor the fractions $\\frac{7}{30}$ and $\\frac{10}{21}$, we have $m = 7$, $n = 30$, $p = 10$, and $q = 21$. Substituting these values into the operation definition, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21} = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{21}{30}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFirst, we simplify $\\frac{21}{30}$. The greatest common divisor (GCD) of 21 and 30 is 3, so:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{21}{30} = \\frac{7}{10}\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute this back into the expression:\n\n\\[\n(7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = 7 \\cdot 10 \\cdot \\frac{7}{10} = 7 \\cdot 7 = 49\n\\]\n\nSo the simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}$ is 49. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\n\n# Function to perform the custom operation\ndef custom_operation(m, n, p, q):\n    # Simplify q/n\n    common_divisor = gcd(q, n)\n    simplified_q = q // common_divisor\n    simplified_n = n // common_divisor\n    # Perform the operation\n    result = m * p * (simplified_q / simplified_n)\n    return result\n\n# Values for the fractions 7/30 and 10/21\nm, n = 7, 30\np, q = 10, 21\n\n# Calculate the result\nresult = custom_operation(m, n, p, q)\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n49.0\n```\nThe simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}$ is \\(\\boxed{49}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to use the given operation definition and apply it to the fractions $\\frac{7}{30}$ and $\\frac{10}{21}$. The operation is defined as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{m}{n} @ \\frac{p}{q} = (m)(p)\\left(\\frac{q}{n}\\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven fractions are $\\frac{7}{30}$ and $\\frac{10}{21}$. So, we have $m = 7$, $n = 30$, $p = 10$, and $q = 21$. Substituting these values into the operation definition, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21} = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{21}{30}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFirst, we simplify $\\frac{21}{30}$ to its simplest form. The greatest common divisor (GCD) of 21 and 30 is 3, so:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{21}{30} = \\frac{21 \\div 3}{30 \\div 3} = \\frac{7}{10}\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute this back into the expression:\n\n\\[\n(7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = 7 \\cdot 7 = 49\n\\]\n\nSo, the simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}$ is $49$. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\n\n# Given fractions\nm, n = 7, 30\np, q = 10, 21\n\n# Simplify q/n\ngcd_qn = gcd(q, n)\nsimplified_qn = q // gcd_qn\n\n# Calculate the result of the operation\nresult = (m * p * simplified_qn) // (n // gcd_qn)\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n49\n```\nThe simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21}$ is \\(\\boxed{49}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to apply the given operation @ to the fractions $\\frac{7}{30}$ and $\\frac{10}{21}$. The operation is defined as $\\frac{m}{n}@\\frac{p}{q} = (m)(p)\\left(\\frac{q}{n}\\right)$. Let's break down the steps:\n\n1. Identify the values of $m$, $n$, $p$, and $q$ from the fractions $\\frac{7}{30}$ and $\\frac{10}{21}$.\n   - For $\\frac{7}{30}$, $m = 7$ and $n = 30$.\n   - For $\\frac{10}{21}$, $p = 10$ and $q = 21$.\n2. Substitute these values into the operation formula:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{7}{30}@\\frac{10}{21} = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{21}{30}\\right).\n   \\]\n3. Simplify the expression:\n   \\[\n   (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{21}{30}\\right) = 70 \\cdot \\frac{21}{30} = \\frac{70 \\cdot 21}{30} = \\frac{1470}{30}.\n   \\]\n4. Simplify the fraction $\\frac{1470}{30}$ by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1470 and 30, which is 30. Divide both the numerator and the denominator by 30:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1470 \\div 30}{30 \\div 30} = \\frac{49}{1} = 49.\n   \\]\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\nfrom math import gcd\n\n# Values from the fractions\nm, n = 7, 30\np, q = 10, 21\n\n# Applying the operation\nresult = (m * p * q) // n\n\n# Simplifying the fraction (although it should be an integer in this case)\nnumerator = m * p * q\ndenominator = n\ncommon_divisor = gcd(numerator, denominator)\nsimplified_result = numerator // common_divisor\n\nprint(simplified_result)\n```\n```output\n49\n```\nThe simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30}@\\frac{10}{21}$ is \\(\\boxed{49}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to apply the given operation @ to the fractions $\\frac{7}{30}$ and $\\frac{10}{21}$. The operation is defined as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{m}{n}@\\frac{p}{q} = (m)(p)\\left(\\frac{q}{n}\\right)\n\\]\n\nGiven the fractions $\\frac{7}{30}$ and $\\frac{10}{21}$, we can substitute $m = 7$, $n = 30$, $p = 10$, and $q = 21$ into the operation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{30}@\\frac{10}{21} = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{21}{30}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFirst, we simplify the fraction $\\frac{21}{30}$:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{21}{30} = \\frac{7}{10}\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute this back into the expression:\n\n\\[\n(7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{7}{10}\\right) = (7)(7) = 49\n\\]\n\nSo the simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30}@\\frac{10}{21}$ is $49$. Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Define the operation @\ndef operation_at(m, n, p, q):\n    return (m * p * (q / n))\n\n# Given fractions\nm, n = 7, 30\np, q = 10, 21\n\n# Calculate the result of the operation\nresult = operation_at(m, n, p, q)\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n49.0\n```\nThe simplified value of $\\frac{7}{30}@\\frac{10}{21}$ is \\(\\boxed{49}\\).", "To solve the problem, we need to follow the given operation definition step by step. The operation @ is defined as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{m}{n} @ \\frac{p}{q} = (m)(p)\\left(\\frac{q}{n}\\right)\n\\]\n\nWe are given the fractions $\\frac{7}{30}$ and $\\frac{10}{21}$. Let's substitute $m = 7$, $n = 30$, $p = 10$, and $q = 21$ into the operation definition:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7}{30} @ \\frac{10}{21} = (7)(10)\\left(\\frac{21}{30}\\right)\n\\]\n\nFirst, we simplify the fraction $\\frac{21}{30}$. The greatest common divisor (GCD) of 21 and 30 is 3, so we can simplify it to $\\frac{7}{10}$:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{21}{30} = \\frac{7}{10}\n\\]\n\nNow we substitute this simplified fraction 