$\\log_3(5)=d$, express $\\log_2(5)$ in terms of $c$ and $d$. \\boxed{}. \\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{2009}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{3}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{5}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{c}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{d}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{1}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{2}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{3}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{4}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{5}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{6}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{7}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{8}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{9}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{10}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{11}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{12}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{13}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{14}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{15}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{16}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{17}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{18}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{19}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{20}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{21}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{22}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{23}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{24}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{25}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{26}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{27}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{28}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{29}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{30}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{31}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{32}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{33}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{34}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{35}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{36}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{37}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{38}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{39}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{40}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{41}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{42}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{43}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{44}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{45}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{46}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{47}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{48}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{49}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{50}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{51}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{52}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{53}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{54}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{55}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{56}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{57}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{58}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{59}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{60}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{61}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{62}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{63}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{64}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{65}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{66}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{67}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{68}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{69}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{70}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{71}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{72}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{73}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{74}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{75}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{76}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{77}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{78}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{79}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{80}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{81}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{82}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{83}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{84}\\rangle\n\\langle\n\\boxed{85}\\rangle\n', 'The 75th percentile of set $A$ is greater than the 25th percentile of set $B$. The interquartile range of set $A$ is greater than the interquartile range of set $B$. If set $A$ and set $B$ are combined into one set, the range of the numbers in this new set will be greater than the sum of the ranges of set $A$ and set $B$. Which of the following could be the number of elements in set $A$ and set $B$? \\textbf{(A)} $A=18, B=5$\n\\textbf{(B)} $A=17, B=4$\n\\textbf{(C)} $A=15, B=10$\n\\textbf{(D)} $A=16, B=5$\n\\textbf{(E)} $A=14, B=6$http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Wiki/index.php/2010_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_21\n\\textbf{Solution:}\nLet\'s solve each problem step by step.\n\n### Problem 1\nWe need to find the number of ways to distribute 10 distinct balls into 2 identical boxes such that no box is empty.\n\n1. **Total number of ways to distribute 10 balls into 2 boxes (identical or distinct):**\n   Each ball has 2 choices (either box 1 or box 2). Therefore, there are \\(2^{10}\\) ways to distribute the balls.\n\n2. **Subtract the cases where one box is empty:**\n   There are 2 cases where one box is empty (all balls in box 1 or all balls in box 2). So, we subtract these 2 cases from the total.\n\n   \\[\n   2^{10} - 2 = 1024 - 2 = 1022\n   \\]\n\n3. **Since the boxes are identical, each distribution is counted twice (once for each box being the "first" box). Therefore, we divide by 2:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1022}{2} = 511\n   \\]\n\nThus, the number of ways to distribute 10 distinct balls into 2 identical boxes such that no box is empty is \\(\\boxed{511}\\).\n\n### Problem 2\nWe need to find the maximum possible value of the expression \\(x + 2y + 3z\\) given the constraints \\(x + y + z = 1\\) and \\(x, y, z \\geq 0\\).\n\n1. **Express \\(z\\) in terms of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):**\n   \\[\n   z = 1 - x - y\n   \\]\n\n2. **Substitute \\(z\\) into the expression \\(x + 2y + 3z\\):**\n   \\[\n   x + 2y + 3(1 - x - y) = x + 2y + 3 - 3x - 3y = 3 - 2x - y\n   \\]\n\n3. **Maximize the expression \\(3 - 2x - y\\):**\n   To maximize \\(3 - 2x - y\\), we need to minimize \\(2x + y\\). Since \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are non-negative and \\(x + y \\leq 1\\), the minimum value of \\(2x + y\\) occurs when \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = 0\\).\n\n   \\[\n   2(0) + 0 = 0\n   \\]\n\n4. **Substitute \\(x = 0\\) and \\(y = 0\\) back into the expression:**\n   \\[\n   3 - 2(0) - 0 = 3\n   \\]\n\nThus, the maximum possible value of \\(x + 2y + 3z\\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).\n\n### Problem 3\nWe need to determine the number of elements in set \\(A\\) and set \\(B\\) given the conditions.\n\n1. **75th percentile of set \\(A\\) is greater than the 25th percentile of set \\(B\\):**\n   This implies that the middle 50% of set \\(A\\) is greater than the middle 50% of set \\(B\\).\n\n2. **Interquartile range of set \\(A\\) is greater than the interquartile range of set \\(B\\):**\n   This implies that the spread of the middle 50% of set \\(A\\) is greater than that of set \\(B\\).\n\n3. **Range of the combined set is greater than the sum of the ranges of set \\(A\\) and set \\(B\\):**\n   This', 'Given that the sum of the