at the polynomial \\( p(x) - 1 \\) has roots at \\( x = a \\), \\( x = b \\), and \\( x = c \\). Therefore, \\( p(x) - 1 \\) is a polynomial of degree 3 or higher, and since \\( p(x) \\) itself is a polynomial of degree 2, the only way this can happen is if \\( p(x) \\) is a constant polynomial. Since \\( p(x) \\) is a polynomial of degree 2 and it takes the value 1 at three distinct points, it must be the constant polynomial \\( p(x) = 1 \\).\n\nThus, the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is 0. Let's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, simplify\n\n# Define the variables\nx, a, b, c = symbols('x a b c')\n\n# Define the polynomial p(x)\np_x = ((x - b)*(x - c))/((a - b)*(a - c)) + ((x - a)*(x - c))/((b - a)*(b - c)) + ((x - a)*(x - b))/((c - a)*(c - b))\n\n# Simplify the polynomial\np_x_simplified = simplify(p_x)\nprint(p_x_simplified)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nThe simplified form of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is \\( 1 \\), which is a constant polynomial. Therefore, the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is \\( 0 \\).\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]", "To find the degree of the polynomial\n\\[p(x) = \\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)},\\]\nwe can analyze the expression step by step.\n\n1. Each term in the polynomial is a quadratic polynomial divided by a constant.\n2. The degree of each term is 2 because the highest power of \\(x\\) in each term is 2.\n3. When we add these terms together, the degree of the resulting polynomial is the maximum degree of the individual terms, which is 2.\n\nTo confirm this, let's write a Python script to verify the degree of the polynomial. We'll use symbolic computation with the sympy library to handle the algebraic manipulations.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variables\nx, a, b, c = sp.symbols('x a b c')\n\n# Define the polynomial p(x)\np = ( (x - b)*(x - c) / ((a - b)*(a 