(x) \\) for three distinct points \\( a, b, \\) and \\( c \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ L(x) = y_1 \\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + y_2 \\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + y_3 \\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)}. \\]\n\nIn our case, the \\( y_i \\) values are all 1, so the polynomial simplifies to:\n\n\\[ p(x) = \\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)}. \\]\n\nTo confirm that this is indeed a polynomial of degree 2, we can check the highest power of \\( x \\) in each term. Each term is a product of two linear factors in \\( x \\), divided by a constant. Therefore, each term is a quadratic polynomial in \\( x \\), and the sum of these terms is also a quadratic polynomial.\n\nLet's verify this by simplifying the expression using Python. We'll define the polynomial and check its degree.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, simplify\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\nx, a, b, c = symbols('x a b c')\r\n\r\n# Define the polynomial p(x)\r\np_x = ((x - b)*(x - c))/((a - b)*(a - c)) + ((x - a)*(x - c))/((b - a)*(b - c)) + ((x - a)*(x - b))/((c - a)*(c - b))\r\n\r\n# Simplify the polynomial\r\np_x_simplified = simplify(p_x)\r\nprint(p_x_simplified)\n```\n```output\n1\n```\nAfter simplifying the polynomial \\( p(x) \\), we find that it reduces to the constant polynomial 1. This means that the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is actually a constant function, and its degree is 0.\n\nTherefore, the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the degree of the polynomial \\( p(x) \\). The polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\np(x) = \\frac{(x - b)(x - c)}{(a - b)(a - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - c)}{(b - a)(b - c)} + \\frac{(x - a)(x - b)}{(c - a)(c - b)}\n\\]\n\nLet's break down the problem step-by-step:\n\n1. **Understand the Polynomial Structure:**\n   Each term in the polynomial \\( p(x) \\) is a fraction where the numerator is a qua