uare \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\square \\', "To determine the probability that the segments \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\) intersect, we can use geometric reasoning and combinatorial analysis.\n\nFirst, let's understand the problem. The segments \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\) will intersect if and only if the points \\(A, B, C,\\) and \\(D\\) are arranged such that one pair of points (either \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) or \\(C\\) and \\(D\\)) is separated by the other pair. This means that the points must be arranged in an alternating order around the circle, i.e., the order could be \\(ACBD\\) or \\(ADBC\\).\n\nGiven that the points are chosen randomly and independently, the total number of possible arrangements of the points \\(A, B, C,\\) and \\(D\\) is \\(4!\\) (4 factorial), which is 24. Out of these 24 arrangements, there are exactly 2 arrangements that result in intersecting segments: \\(ACBD\\) and \\(ADBC\\).\n\nTherefore, the probability that segments \\(AB\\) and \\(CD\\) intersect is the number of 