\[\n   P(2) = (2-2)(2-3)Q'(2) + 2R(2) = 2R(2) = 2(2a + b) = 4a + 2b\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P(3) = (3-2)(3-3)Q'(3) + 2R(3) = 2R(3) = 2(3a + b) = 6a + 2b\n   \\]\n\n6. **Use the fact that \\(P(x)\\) is the same polynomial in both expressions:**\n   Since \\(P(x)\\) is the same polynomial, we can equate the expressions for \\(P(1)\\) and \\(P(4)\\) from both forms:\n   \\[\n   a + b = 4a + b \\implies 3a = 0 \\implies a = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   4a + b = 6a + 2b \\implies 4(0) + b = 6(0) + 2b \\implies b = 0\n   \\]\n   But we already have \\(b = 5 - 4k\\), so \\(k = 1\\) and \\(b = 1\\).\n\n7. **Form the polynomial \\(P(x)\\):**\n   Now we know \\(R(x) = x + 1\\), so:\n   \\[\n   P(x) = (x-1)(x-4)Q(x) + x + 1\n   \\]\n   Since \\(P(x)\\) is monic, \\(Q(x)\\) must be of the form \\(x + c\\). Let's assume \\(Q(x) = x + c\\), then:\n   \\[\n   P(x) = (x-1)(x-4)(x+c) + x + 1\n   \\]\n   Expanding this, we get:\n   \\[\n   P(x) = (x^2 - 5x + 4)(x + c) + x + 1 = x^3 + cx^2 - 5x^2 - 5cx + 4x + 4c + x + 1 = x^3 + (c-5)x^2 +", "Let's start by defining the polynomial \\(P(x)\\) as a monic polynomial of degree 3. So, we can write:\n\\[ P(x) = x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c \\]\nWe are given that \\(P(x)\\) has remainder \\(R(x)\\) when it is divided by \\((x-1)(x-4)\\), and remainder \\(2R(x)\\) when it is divided by \\((x-2)(x-3)\\). This means that:\n\\[ P(x) = (x-1)(x-4)Q_1(x) + R(x) \\]\n\\[ P(x) = (x-2)(x-3)Q_2(x) + 2R(x) \\]\nwhere \\(Q_1(x)\\) and \\(Q_2(x)\\) are the quotient polynomials. Since \\(R(x)\\) is the remainder when dividing by a quadratic polynomial, \\(R(x)\\) must be a linear polynomial. Let's write \\(R(x) = mx + n\\). So, we have:\n\\[ P(1) = R(1) = m + n \\]\n\\[ P(4) = R(4) = 4m + n \\]\n\\[ P(2) = 2R(2) = 2(2m + n) = 4m + 2n \\]\n\\[ P(3) = 2R(3) = 2(3m + n) = 6m + 2n \\]\nWe also know that \\(P(0) = 5\\), so:\n\\[ P(0) = c = 5 \\]\nNow we can use these equations to solve for \\(a\\), \\(b\\), \\(m\\), and \\(n\\). Let's write the equations for \\(P(1)\\), \\(P(4)\\),