 negative sides. The floor function will map these values to 0 for sufficiently large \\( x \\).\n\n3. For \\( x \\) between 0 and 1:\n   \\[\n   \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^x\n   \\]\n   When \\( x \\) is between 0 and 1, \\( \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^x \\) will be a negative fraction between -1 and 0, so the floor function will map these values to -1.\n\n4. For \\( x \\) between 1 and 2:\n   \\[\n   \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^x\n   \\]\n   When \\( x \\) is between 1 and 2, \\( \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^x \\) will be a positive fraction between 0 and \\( \\frac{5}{8} \\), so the floor function will map these values to 0.\n\n5. For \\( x \\) between 2 and 3:\n   \\[\n   \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^x\n   \\]\n   When \\( x \\) is between 2 and 3, \\( \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^x \\) will be a negative fraction between -1 and 0, so the floor function will map these values to -1.\n\nFrom the above analysis, we can see that the distinct values in the range of \\( f(x) \\) are \\( 1, 0, \\) and \\( -1 \\). Therefore, there are 3 distinct values in the range of \\( f(x) \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with a Python code implementation to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\n# Define the function\ndef f(x):\n    return np.floor((-5/8)**x)\n\n# Generate a range of x values from 0 to a large number to observe the behavior\nx_values = np.linspace(0, 10, 1000)\nf_values = f(x_values)\n\n# Get the unique values in the range of f(x)\nunique_f_values = np.unique(f_values)\nprint((unique_f_values, len(unique_f_values)))\n```", "To determine the number of distinct values in the range of the function \\( f(x) = \\left\\lfloor \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^x \\right\\rfloor \\) for \\( x \\in [0, \\infty) \\), we need to analyze the behavior of the expression \\( \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^x \\).\n\n1. **Behavior of \\( \\left( -\\frac{5}{8} \\right)^x \\) for \\( x \\geq 0 \\):**\n   - For \\( x = 0