s by the number of people. So, 12 sandwiches ÷ 6 people = 2 sandwiches per person.\nTo find out how many pieces of fruit each person will get, we need to divide the total number of pieces of fruit by the number of people. So, 18 pieces of fruit ÷ 6 people = 3 pieces of fruit per person.\nTherefore, each person will get 2 sandwiches and 3 pieces of fruit.\n\nA high school student is working on a project that involves understanding the concept of a Dirac operator. She is given a simplified version of the Dirac operator equation, which is represented as a matrix equation. The equation is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{D} = \\alpha \\cdot \\nabla + \\beta m\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\mathcal{D}\\) is the Dirac operator, \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\) are matrices, \\(\\nabla\\) is the gradient operator, and \\(m\\) is a scalar representing mass. The student is asked to find the eigenvalues of the Dirac operator for a specific case where:\n\n\\[\n\\alpha = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\beta = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad m = 2\n\\]\n\nAssuming that the gradient operator \\(\\nabla\\) can be represented as a matrix \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\), find the eigenvalues of the Dirac operator \\(\\mathcal{D}\\). To find the eigenvalues of the Dirac operator \\(\\mathcal{D}\\), we first need to substitute the given matrices and scalar into the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{D} = \\alpha \\cdot \\nabla + \\beta m = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{pmatrix} \\cdot 2\n\\]\n\nMultiplying the matrices, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{D} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} + \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNow, to find the eigenvalues, we need to solve the characteristic equation:\n\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathcal{D} - \\lambda I) = 0\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\lambda\\) represents the eigenvalues and \\(I\\) is the identity matrix. Substituting the values, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\det\\left(\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} - \\lambda \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\right) = \\det\\left(\\begin{pmatrix} 2-\\lambda & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2-\\lambda \\end{pmatrix}\\right) = 0\n\\]\n\nExpanding the determinant, we get:\n\n\\[\n(2-\\lambda)(-2-\\lambda) - (1)(1) = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\lambda^2 - 4 = 0\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(\\lambda\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\lambda = \\pm 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of the Dirac operator \\(\\mathcal{D}\\) are \\(\\boxed{\\lambda = 2}\\) and \\(\\boxed{\\lambda = -2}\\).\n\nThis problem combines concepts from Linear Algebra (matrices, eigenvalues) and Calculus (gradient operator).', 'Find the units digit of $17^7.$, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}. Shayna\nFind the units digit of $16^8.$, and put your final answer within \\boxed{}. Shayna\nFind the units digit of $15^9.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $14^{10}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $13^{11}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $12^{12}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $11^{13}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $10^{14}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $9^{15}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $8^{16}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $7^{17}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $6^{18}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $5^{19}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $4^{20}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $3^{21}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $2^{22}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $1^{23}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $0^{24}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $(-1)^{25}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $(-2)^{26}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $(-3)^{27}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $(-4)^{28}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $(-5)^{29}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $(-6)^{30}.$ Shayna\nFind the units digit of $(-7)^{31}.$ Shay