\(8^4 = 4096\\) (units digit is 6)\n- \\(8^5 = 32768\\) (units digit is 8)\n- \\(8^6 = 262144\\) (units digit is 4)\n\nWe can see that the units digits repeat every 4 numbers: 8, 4, 2, 6. Since the exponent 6 is 2 more than a multiple of 4 (i.e., \\(6 \\mod 4 = 2\\)), the units digit of \\(8^6\\) will be the same as the units digit of \\(8^2\\), which is 4.\n\nSo, the units digit of \\(18^6\\) is 4.\n\n2. **Find the units digit of \\(7^{12}\\).**\n\nSimilarly, to find the units digit of \\(7^{12}\\), we can focus on the units digit of the base, which is 7 in this case. We need to determine the units digit of \\(7^{12}\\).\n\nWe can observe the pattern in the units digits of the powers of 7:\n- \\(7^1 = 7\\) (units digit is 7)\n- \\(7^2 = 49\\) (units digit is 9)\n- \\(7^3 = 343\\) (units digit is 3)\n- \\(7^4 = 2401\\) (units digit is 1)\n- \\(7^5 = 16807\\) (units digit is 7)\n- \\(7^6 = 117649\\) (units digit is 9)\n\nWe can see that the units digits repeat every 4 numbers: 7, 9, 3, 1. Since the exponent 12 is a multiple of 4 (i.e., \\(12 \\mod 4 = 0\\)), the units digit of \\(7^{12}\\) will be the same as the units digit of \\(7^4\\), which is 1.\n\nSo, the units digit of \\(7^{12}\\) is 1.\n\nLet's confirm these results using Python code.\n```python\n# Function to find the units digit of a^b\ndef units_digit(a, b):\n    # Extract the units digit of the base\n    units_digit_base = a % 10\n    # Find the units digit of the power\n    units_digit_power = pow(units_digit_base, b, 10)\n    return units_digit_power\n\n# Find the units digit of 18^6\nunits_digit_18_6 = units_digit(18, 6)\n# Find the units digit of 7^12\nunits_digit_7_12 = units_digit(7, 12)\n\nprint((units_digit_18_6, units_digit_7_12))\n```\n```output\n(4, 1)\n```\nThe units digit of \\(18^6\\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\), and the units digit of \\(7^{12}\\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).\n\nIf the equation $x^2 + ky^2 = 2$ represents an ellipse with foci on the y-axis, then the range of real numbers for \\( k \\) is 