n_wins * prob_freddie_wins\n\n# Expected value calculation for green marbles drawn in 100 draws\ntotal_marbles = 60\ngreen_marbles = 27\nprob_green_marble = green_marbles / total_marbles\nexpected_green_marbles = 100 * prob_green_marble\n\nprint(((prob_both_win, expected_green_marbles)))\n```\n```output\n(0.25, 45.0)\n```\nThe probability that both Dan and Freddie win their respective elections is \\( \\frac{1}{4} \\).\n\nThe expected number of green marbles drawn in 100 draws is 45.\n\nSo, the final answers are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\boxed{45}\n\\]", 'A 10 by 10 grid is composed of 100 unit squares.  How many unit squares are contained within the region inside the circle?  (Note: the unit squares contained on the border of the region are not counted.)  (Note: the circle has a radius of 5 and its center is the center of the grid.)  (Note: for this problem, $\\pi = 3.14$)  (Note: the answer is an integer.)  (Note: the diagram is not drawn to scale.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Note: this is the last note in this problem.)  (Not