m the first condition, we get \\(c < 16\\). Since \\(c\\) is a whole number, the maximum possible value for \\(c\\) is 15. \n\nThe perimeter \\(P\\) of the triangle is given by \\(P = a + b + c = 8 + 8 + c = 16 + c\\). To find the greatest possible perimeter, we substitute the maximum value of \\(c\\), which is 15.\n\nSo, the greatest possible perimeter is \\(16 + 15 = 31\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Given sides of the triangle\na = 8\nb = 8\n\n# The maximum possible value for the third side c\nc_max = 15\n\n# Calculate the greatest possible perimeter\ngreatest_perimeter = a + b + c_max\nprint(greatest_perimeter)\n```\n```output\n31\n```\nThe greatest possible perimeter of the triangle, where two sides are each 8 units long and the third side has a whole number length, is \\(\\boxed{31}\\).", "To determine the greatest possible perimeter of a triangle with two sides of length 8 units each and a third side of whole number length, we need to use the triangle inequality theorem. The triangle inequality theorem states that for any triangle with sides \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\), the following must be true:\n\n1. \\(a + b > c\\)\n2. \\(a + c > b\\)\n3. \\(b + c > a\\)\n\nIn this problem, let the sides of the triangle be \\(a = 8\\), \\(b = 8\\), and \\(c\\) be the third side, which must be a whole number. The inequalities we need to satisfy are:\n\n1. \\(8 + 8 > c\\) ⟹ \\(16 > c\\) ⟹ \\(c < 16\\)\n2. \\(8 + c > 8\\) ⟹ \\(c > 0\\) (which is already implied by \\(c\\) being a whole number)\n3. \\(8 + 8 > c\\) ⟹ \\(16 > c\\) ⟹ \\(c < 16\\) (same as the first inequality)\n\nSo, the possible values for \\(c\\) are whole numbers from 1 to 15. The greatest possible value for \\(c\\) is 15. The perimeter of the triangle is the sum of its sides, so the greatest possible perimeter is \\(8 + 8 + 15 = 31\\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# The lengths of the two sides of the triangle\nside1 = 8\nside2 = 8\n\n# The possible range f