irc \\), we have \\( y = 40^\\circ \\). Substituting \\( y \\) into the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n40^\\circ + 40^\\circ + 2x = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\n80^\\circ + 2x = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\n2x = 100^\\circ\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = 50^\\circ\n\\]\n\nSo, the measure of \\( \\angle BAC \\) is \\( 50^\\circ \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the calculation is accurate.\n```python\n# Calculation to confirm the angle BAC\n# Given angle BCA = 40 degrees\nangle_BCA = 40\n\n# Since D is the circumcenter, angle BDC is twice angle BAC\n# Let angle BAC = x, then angle BDC = 2x\n# In triangle BDC, the sum of angles is 180 degrees\n# angle DBC + angle DCB + angle BDC = 180 degrees\n# Since BD = CD, angle DBC = angle DCB = 40 degrees\n# So, 40 + 40 + 2x = 180\n# 80 + 2x = 180\n# 2x = 100\n# x = 50\nangle_BAC = (180 - 2 * angle_BCA) / 2\nprint(angle_BAC)\n```\n```output\n50.0\n```\nThe measure of \\( \\angle BAC \\) is \\(\\boxed{50^\\circ}\\).", 'In the diagram, \\(AD = BD = CD\\) and \\(\\angle BCA = 40^\\circ\\). We need to find the measure of \\(\\angle BAC\\).\n\nFirst, since \\(AD = BD = CD\\), point \\(D\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ABC\\). This means that \\(D\\) is equidistant from all three vertices \\(A\\), \\(B\\), and \\(C\\). Therefore, \\(AD = BD = CD\\) implies that \\(\\triangle ABD\\) and \\(\\triangle BCD\\) are isosceles triangles.\n\nSince \\(D\\) is the circumcenter, \\(\\angle BAC\\) is an inscribed angle that subtends the same arc as \\(\\angle BDC\\). The measure of an inscribed angle is half the measure of the central angle that subtends the same arc. Therefore, \\(\\angle BAC = \\frac{1}{2} \\angle BDC\\).\n\nNext, we need to find \\(\\angle BDC\\). Since \\(D\\) is the circumcenter, \\(\\angle BDC\\) is a central angle. The measure of a central angle is twice the measure of the inscribed angle that subtends the same arc. Therefore, \\(\\angle BDC = 2 \\angle BAC\\).\n\nHowever, we