n of the baseball, \\( \\frac{a_{\\text{softball}}}{a_{\\text{baseball}}} \\).\n\nFrom the equation above, we can solve for \\( \\frac{a_{\\text{softball}}}{a_{\\text{baseball}}} \\):\n\n\\[ \\frac{a_{\\text{softball}}}{a_{\\text{baseball}}} = \\frac{m_{\\text{baseball}}}{m_{\\text{softball}}} = \\frac{150}{200} = \\frac{3}{4} \\]\n\nSo, the ratio of the acceleration of the softball to the acceleration of the baseball is \\( \\frac{3}{4} \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Given masses\nmass_softball = 200  # in grams\nmass_baseball = 150  # in grams\n\n# Calculate the ratio of accelerations\nratio_accelerations = mass_baseball / mass_softball\n\n# Simplify the fraction\nfrom fractions import Fraction\nratio_fraction = Fraction(mass_baseball, mass_softball)\n\nprint(ratio_fraction)\n```\n```output\n3/4\n```\nThe ratio of the acceleration of the softball to the acceleration of the baseball is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{4}}\\).", "To solve this problem, we'll use the formula for force, which is given by \\( F = m \\cdot a \\), where \\( F \\) is the force, \\( m \\) is the mass, and \\( a \\) is the acceleration.\n\nGiven that the force exerted by Jen and Jack is the same, we can set up the following equations:\n\n\\[ F = m_{\\text{softball}} \\cdot a_{\\text{softball}} \\]\n\\[ F = m_{\\text{baseball}} \\cdot a_{\\text{baseball}} \\]\n\nSince the force \\( F \\) is the same in both cases, we can equate the two expressions:\n\n\\[ m_{\\text{softball}} \\cdot a_{\\text{softball}} = m_{\\text{baseball}} \\cdot a_{\\text{baseball}} \\]\n\nWe can rearrange this equation to solve for the ratio of the accelerations:\n\n\\[ \\frac{a_{\\text{softball}}}{a_{\\text{baseball}}} = \\frac{m_{\\text{baseball}}}{m_{\\text{softball}}} \\]\n\nGiven that the mass of the softball \\( m_{\\text{softball}} = 200 \\) g and the mass of the baseball \\( m_{\\text{baseball}} = 150 \\) g, we can substitute these values into the equation:\n\n\\[