p the equation for the same force\nequation = sp.Eq(m_s * a_s, m_b * a_b)\n\n# Solve for the ratio a_s / a_b\nratio = sp.solve(equation, a_s)[0] / a_b\n\n# Simplify the ratio\nsimplified_ratio = sp.simplify(ratio)\nprint(simplified_ratio)\n```\n```output\n3/4\n```\nThe ratio of the acceleration of Jen's ball to Jack's ball is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{4}}\\).\n\nGiven the function $f(x)=\\sin 2x+2\\cos ^{2}x-1$, the horizontal coordinates of each point on the graph of $f(x)$ are shortened to $\\frac{1}{2}$ of the original length, while the vertical coordinates remain unchanged. Then, the resulting graph is shifted to the right by $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ units, forming the graph of the function $y=g(x)$. Determine the analytical expression for $y=g(x)$.\n\nA) $g(x)=\\sqrt{2}\\sin x$\nB) $g(x)=\\sqrt{2}\\cos x$\nC) $g(x)=\\sqrt{2}\\sin (4x-\\frac{3\\pi}{4})$\nD) $g(x)=\\sqrt{2}\\cos 4x$\nTo determine the analytical expression for the function \\( y = g(x) \\) after the transformations, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Simplify the Original Function:**\n   The original function is \\( f(x) = \\sin 2x + 2 \\cos^2 x - 1 \\). We can use the double-angle identity for cosine, \\( \\cos^2 x = \\frac{1 + \\cos 2x}{2} \\), to simplify it.\n   \\[\n   f(x) = \\sin 2x + 2 \\left( \\frac{1 + \\cos 2x}{2} \\right) - 1 = \\sin 2x + \\cos 2x\n   \\]\n   Using the angle addition formula, we can rewrite this as:\n   \\[\n   f(x) = \\sqrt{2} \\left( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\sin 2x + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\cos 2x \\right) = \\sqrt{2} \\sin \\left( 2x + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Horizontal Compression:**\n   The horizontal coordinates are shortened to \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) of the original length. This means we replace \\( x \\) with \\( 2x \\) in the function \\( f(x) \\):\n   \\[\n   f(2x) = \\sqrt{2} \\sin \\left( 2(2x) + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) = \\sqrt{2} \\sin \\left( 4x + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)\n   \\]\n\n3.", "To solve this problem, we need to use the formula for force, which 