3)!} = \\frac{5!}{3!2!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4 \\times 3!}{3! \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n\\]\n\nStep 2: Assign the 3 people to the 3 chosen offices.\nThe number of ways to assign 3 people to 3 offices is given by the permutation formula \\( P(3,3) \\) or \\( 3! \\):\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nStep 3: Multiply the number of ways to choose the offices by the number of ways to assign the people to the offices.\n\\[\n10 \\times 6 = 60\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to assign 3 people to 5 different offices such that each person gets exactly one office and no office is left unoccupied is \\(\\boxed{60}\\).', "How many different ways can a team of 3 people be chosen from a group of 12 people? \\boxed{}. Shay\nTo solve this problem, we need to use the concept of combinations. A combination is a way of selecting items from a collection, such that the order of selection does not matter.\n\nThe formula for combinations is given by:\n\n$$\n\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\n$$\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to choose from, \\( k \\) is the number of items to choose, and \\( ! \\) denotes the factorial, which is the product of all positive integers up to that number.\n\nIn this problem, we have 12 people, and we want to choose 3 of them. So, \\( n = 12 \\) and \\( k = 3 \\).\n\nUsing the formula, we get:\n\n$$\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3! \\cdot 9!}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's calculate the factorials:\n\n$$\n12! = 12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!\n$$\n\n$$\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n$$\n\nNow, substitute these values back into the combination formula:\n\n$$\n\\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10 \\times 9!}{6 \\times 9!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{6}\n$$\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{6} = \\frac{1320}{6} = 220\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to choose a team of 3 people from a group of 12 is:\n\n$$\n\\