 $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ to? 1. **Understanding the Reflection**:\n   - We need to find the reflection of the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$ given that the reflection of $\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$ is $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n   - The reflection matrix $R$ can be derived from the given vectors. If $R$ is the reflection matrix, then $R \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\n2. **Finding the Reflection Matrix**:\n   - Let $R = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}$. Then we have:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}\n     \\]\n     This gives us the equations:\n     \\[\n     5a = 4 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 5c = 3\n     \\]\n     So, $a = \\frac{4}{5}$ and $c = \\frac{3}{5}$.\n   - Since $R$ is a reflection matrix, it must be orthogonal and have a determinant of -1. The general form of a reflection matrix is:\n     \\[\n     R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos(2\\theta) & \\sin(2\\theta) \\\\ \\sin(2\\theta) & -\\cos(2\\theta) \\end{pmatrix}\n     \\]\n     From the above equations, we can determine that $\\cos(2\\theta) = \\frac{4}{5}$ and $\\sin(2\\theta) = \\frac{3}{5}$.\n   - Therefore, the reflection matrix $R$ is:\n     \\[\n     R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Applying the Reflection Matrix**:\n   - Now we apply this reflection matrix to the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix}$:\n     \\[\n     R \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{5} \\cdot (-2) + \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot (-2) + \\left(-\\frac{4}{5}\\right) \\cdot 3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\be