h 2 and 3.\n\n   - **Divisibility by 2:** The units digit \\( N \\) must be even. So, \\( N \\) can be one of \\( \\{2, 4, 6, 8\\} \\).\n   - **Divisibility by 3:** The sum of the digits of the number must be divisible by 3. The sum of the digits of \\( 21420N \\) is \\( 2 + 1 + 4 + 2 + 0 + N = 9 + N \\). So, \\( 9 + N \\) must be divisible by 3. Since 9 is already divisible by 3, \\( N \\) must also be divisible by 3. So, \\( N \\) can be one of \\( \\{3, 6, 9\\} \\).\n\n   The only nonzero value of \\( N \\) that satisfies both conditions is \\( N = 6 \\).\n\n2. **Problem 2:**\n   We need to find the largest possible value of \\( N \\) such that the number \\( 100N + 10 + 1 = 100N + 11 \\) is divisible by 3. A number is divisible by 3 if the sum of its digits is divisible by 3.\n\n   - The sum of the digits of \\( 100N + 11 \\) is \\( N + 1 + 1 = N + 2 \\). So, \\( N + 2 \\) must be divisible by 3. The largest possible value of \\( N \\) that satisfies this condition is \\( N = 7 \\) (since \\( 7 + 2 = 9 \\), which is divisible by 3).\n\nLet's verify these solutions using Python code.\n```python\n# Problem 1\n# Find the nonzero value of N such that 21420N is divisible by 6\ndef find_N_for_divisibility_by_6():\n    for N in range(1, 10):  # N must be a nonzero digit (1 to 9)\n        if (214200 + N) % 6 == 0:\n            return N\n\n# Problem 2\n# Find the largest possible value of N such that 100N + 11 is divisible by 3\ndef find_largest_N_for_divisibility_by_3():\n    for N in range(9, 0, -1):  # N must be a digit (9 to 1)\n        if (100 * N + 11) % 3 == 0:\n            return N\n\n# Get the results\nN_for_divisibility_by_6 = find_N_for_divisibility_by_6()\nlargest_N_for_divisibility_by_3 = find_largest_N_for_divisibility_by_3()\n\nprint((N_for_divisibility_by_6, largest_N_for_divisibility_by_3))\n```\n```output\n(6, 7)\n```\nThe nonzero value of \\( N \\) that makes the number \\( 21420N \\) divisible by 6 is \\( \\boxed{6} \\).\n\nThe largest possible value 