):\n\n\\[a = 2 - \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{9}{5}\\]\n\\[b = 2\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right) - 4 = \\frac{2}{5} - 4 = \\frac{2}{5} - \\frac{20}{5} = -\\frac{18}{5}\\]\n\\[c = 3 - \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{15}{5} - \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{14}{5}\\]\n\nSo, the polynomial \\(P(x)\\) is:\n\n\\[P(x) = \\frac{9}{5}x^2 - \\frac{18}{5}x + \\frac{14}{5}\\]\n\nNow we need to find \\(P(16)\\):\n\n\\[P(16) = \\frac{9}{5}(16^2) - \\frac{18}{5}(16) + \\frac{14}{5}\\]\n\\[P(16) = \\frac{9}{5}(256) - \\frac{18}{5}(16) + \\frac{14}{5}\\]\n\\[P(16) = \\frac{2304}{5} - \\frac{288}{5} + \\frac{14}{5}\\]\n\\[P(16) = \\frac{2304 - 288 + 14}{5} = \\frac{2030}{5} = 406\\]\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{406}\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the polynomial function P(x) with the determined coefficients\ndef P(x):\n    return (9/5)*x**2 - (18/5)*x + (14/5)\n\n# Calculate P(16)\nP_16 = P(16)\nprint(P_16)\n```", 'Let $P(x)$ be a polynomial with integer coefficients satisfying $P(1) = 2$ and $P(2) = 3$. Find the maximum possible number of integer solutions $k$ to the equation $P(k) = k^3$. \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed{.} \\boxed