\end{pmatrix}$ and has the smallest magnitude, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Let $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\\\ v_2 \\\\ v_3 \\end{pmatrix}$. The cross product equation can be written as:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\\\ v_2 \\\\ v_3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2v_3 - (-5v_2) \\\\ -5v_1 - 1v_3 \\\\ 1v_2 - 2v_1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2v_3 + 5v_2 \\\\ -5v_1 - v_3 \\\\ v_2 - 2v_1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}.\n   \\]\n   This gives us the following system of equations:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{cases}\n   2v_3 + 5v_2 = 90 \\\\\n   -5v_1 - v_3 = 30 \\\\\n   v_2 - 2v_1 = 30\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n\n2. We can solve this system of equations to express $v_1$, $v_2$, and $v_3$ in terms of each other. Let's solve the system step-by-step.\n\nFrom the third equation, we can express $v_2$ in terms of $v_1$:\n\\[\nv_2 = 2v_1 + 30.\n\\]\n\nSubstitute $v_2$ into the first equation:\n\\[\n2v_3 + 5(2v_1 + 30) = 90 \\implies 2v_3 + 10v_1 + 150 = 90 \\implies 2v_3 + 10v_1 = -60 \\implies v_3 + 5v_1 = -30 \\implies v_3 = -5v_1 - 30.\n\\]\n\nNow substitute $v_3$ into the second equation:\n\\[\n-5v_1 - (-5v_1 - 30) = 30 \\implies -5v_1 + 5v_1 + 30 = 30 \\implies 30 = 30.\n\\]\n\nThis is an identity, meaning the equations are consistent and we have a free variable. We can choose $v_1$ to be any value, and then determine $v_2$ and $v_3$. To find the vector with the smallest magnitude, we need to minimize $\\|\\mathbf{v}\\| = \\sqrt{v_1^2 + v_2^2 + v_3^2}$. Let's express the magnitude in terms of $v_1$:\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf{v}\\| = \\sqrt{v_1^2 + (2v_1 + 30)^2 + (-5v_1 - 30)^2} = \\sqrt{v_1^2 + 4v_1^2 + 120v_1 + 900 + 25v_1^2 + 300v_1 + 900} = \\sqrt{30v_1^2 + 420v_1 + 1800}.\n\\]\n\nTo minimize this, we can take the derivative with respect to $v_1$ and set it to zero:\n\\[\n\\frac{d}{dv_1} \\left( 30v_1^2 + 420v_1 + 1800 \\right) = 60v_1 + 420 = 0 \\implies v_1 = -7.\n\\]\n\nNow we can find $v_2$ and $v_3$:\n\\[\nv_2 = 2(-7) + 30 = -14 + 30 = 16,\n\\]\n\\[\nv_3 = -5(-7) - 30 = 35 - 30 = 5.\n\\]\n\nSo the vector $\\", "Let's denote the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ as $\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\\\ v_2 \\\\ v_3 \\end{pmatrix}$. The cross product of the vectors $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by:\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\end{pmatrix} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2v_3 - (-5v_2) \\\\ -5v_1 - 1v_3 \\\\ 1v_2 - 2v_1 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2v_3 + 5v_2 \\\\ -5v_1 - v_3 \\\\ v_2 - 2v_1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nWe are given that this cross product is equal to $\\begin{pmatrix} 90 \\\\ 30 \\\\ 30 \\end{pmatrix}$. Therefore, we can set up the following system of equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n2v_3 + 5v_2 = 90 \\\\\n-5v_1 - v_3 = 30 \\\\\nv_2 - 2v_1 = 30\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nWe can solve this system of equations step by step. First, we can solve the third equation for $v_2$:\n\\[\nv_2 = 30 + 2v_1.\n\\]\nNext, we substitute $v_2$ into the first equation:\n\\[\n2v_3 + 5(30 + 2v_1) = 90 \\implies 2v_3 + 150 + 10v_1 = 90 \\implies 2v_3 + 10v_1 = -60 \\implies v_3 = -30 - 5v_1.\n\\]\nFinally, we substitute $v_3$ into the second equation:\n\\[\n-5v_1 - (-30 - 5v_1) = 30 \\implies -5v_1 + 30 + 5v_1 = 30 \\implies 30 = 30.\n\\]\nThis equation is always true, so it doesn't give us any new information. Instead, we can use the expressions we found for $v_2$ and $v_3$ in terms of $v_1$ to express the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ as:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\\\ 30 + 2v_1 \\\\ -30 - 5v_1 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\nThe magnitude of $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by:\n\\[\n\\|\\mathbf{v}\\| = \\sqrt{v_1^2 + (30 + 2v_1)^2 + (-30 - 5v_1)^2}.\n\\]\nTo find the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ with the smallest magnitude, we need to minimize this expression. Let's define the magnitude squared as a function of $v_1$ and find its minimum:\n\\[\nf(v_1) = v_1^2 + (30 + 2v_1)^2 + (-30 - 5v_1)^2.\n\\]