ation\nequation = sp.acos(x) + sp.acos(2*x) + sp.acos(3*x) - sp.pi\n\n# Solve the equation\nsolutions = sp.solve(equation, x)\n\n# Filter the solutions to find the valid one in the range [-1/3, 1/3]\nvalid_solutions = [sol.evalf() for sol in solutions if sol.is_real and -1/3 <= sol <= 1/3]\n\n# Find the cubic polynomial\nif valid_solutions:\n    x_val = valid_solutions[0]\n    # Use sympy\'s minimal polynomial function to find the polynomial\n    poly = sp.minimal_polynomial(x_val, x)\n    a, b, c, d = poly.all_coeffs()\n    # Calculate the sum of absolute values of coefficients\n    result = sum(abs(coeff) for coeff in [a, b, c, d])\n    print(result)\nelse:\n    print("No valid solution found")\n```\n```output\nNo algorithms are implemented to solve equation acos(x) + acos(2*x) + acos(3*x) - pi\n```\nIt seems that the direct symbolic solution for the equation \\(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x = \\pi\\) is not straightforward using sympy\'s `solve` function. Instead, we can take a different approach by leveraging the properties of the inverse cosine function and the range of \\(x\\).\n\nGiven the equation \\(\\arccos x + \\arccos 2x + \\arccos 3x = \\pi\\), we can use the fact that \\(\\arccos x\\) is a decreasing function and that the sum of the angles must equal \\(\\pi\\). This implies that \\(x\\), \\(2x\\), and \\(3x\\) must be such that their cosines sum up in a way that their inverse cosines add up to \\(\\pi\\).\n\nLet\'s consider the range of \\(x\\). Since \\(x \\in [-\\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{1}{3}]\\), we can test the values within this range to find the solution. We can use numerical methods to find the root of the equation.\n\nLet\'s use sympy\'s `nsolve` function to find the numerical solution to the equation.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nx = sp.symbols(\'x\')\n\n# Define the equation\nequation = sp.acos(x) + sp.acos(2*x) + sp.acos(3*x) - sp.pi\n\n# Use nsolve to find the numerical solution\n# We need to provide an in