3\\alpha_4 = 0$.\n- The sum of the product of the roots taken three at a time is $-c$. In this case, $\\alpha_1\\alpha_2\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_1\\alpha_2\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_1\\alpha_3\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_3\\alpha_4 = 0$.\n- The product of the roots is $d$. In this case, $\\alpha_1\\alpha_2\\alpha_3\\alpha_4 = 2$.\n\nWe need to find the values of $\\alpha_1\\alpha_2 + \\alpha_3\\alpha_4$, $\\alpha_1\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_4$, and $\\alpha_1\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_3$. Let's denote these by $S_1, S_2,$ and $S_3$ respectively. We know that:\n\\[\nS_1 + S_2 + S_3 = \\alpha_1\\alpha_2 + \\alpha_1\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_1\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_3 + \\alpha_2\\alpha_4 + \\alpha_3\\alpha_4 = 0.\n\\]\nSince the roots are distinct, we can use the fact that the polynomial can be factored into two quadratics. Let's assume:\n\\[\nx^4 + 2x^3 + 2 = (x^2 + ax + b)(x^2 + cx + d).\n\\]\nBy comparing coefficients, we get:\n\\[\na + c = 2, \\quad ac + b + d = 0, \\quad ad + bc = 0, \\quad bd = 2.\n\\]\nSolving these equations, we can find the values of $a, b, c,$ and $d$. Let's use Python to solve this system of equations and find the roots of the polynomial.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\na, b, c, d = sp.symbols('a b c d')\r\n\r\n# Define the equations based on the polynomial coefficients\r\neq1 = sp.Eq(a + c, 2)\r\neq2 = sp.Eq(a*c + b + d, 0)\r\neq3 = sp.Eq(a*d + b*c, 0)\r\neq4 = sp.Eq(b*d, 2)\r\n\r\n# Solve the system of equations\r\nsolutions = sp.solve((eq1, eq2, eq3, eq4), (a, b, c, d))\r\nprint(solutions)\n```\n```output\n[(1 - I, 1 + I, 1 + I, 1 - I), (1 + I, 1 - I, 1 - I, 1 + I), (1 - sqrt(I), -sqrt(I)*(1 + sqrt(I)), 1 + sqrt(I), -sqrt(I)*(1 - sqr...(I), 1 - sqrt(I), -sqrt(I)*(1 - sqrt(I))), ((1 + I)**2/2, 1 - I, (1 - I)**2/2, 1 + I), ((1 - I)**2/2, 1 + I, (1 + I)**2/2, 1 - I)]\n```\nThe solutions for the coefficients $(a, b, c, d)$ are complex and involve imaginary numbers. Let's focus on the real solutions,", "To solve this pro