 the system:\n\\[ x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143} \\]\n\nWe can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT) to solve this system. Let's express \\( x \\) in the form \\( x = 143k + 10 \\) and substitute it into the first congruence:\n\\[ 143k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSince \\( 143 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\), we can simplify this to:\n\\[ 6k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ 6k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\]\nTo solve for \\( k \\), we need to find the modular inverse of 6 modulo 7. The modular inverse of 6 modulo 7 is 6 itself, because \\( 6 \\cdot 6 = 36 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7} \\). So we multiply both sides by 6:\n\\[ k \\equiv 2 \\cdot 6 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 12 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nThus, \\( k = 7m + 5 \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Substituting this back into the expression for \\( x \\), we get:\n\\[ x = 143(7m + 5) + 10 \\]\n\\[ x = 1001m + 715 + 10 \\]\n\\[ x = 1001m + 725 \\]\nThe largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies this equation is obtained by setting \\( m = 1 \\), because \\( 1001 \\cdot 2 + 725 = 2727 \\) which is greater than 2010. So we set \\( m = 1 \\):\n\\[ x = 1001 \\cdot 1 + 725 = 1726 \\]\nWe should verify that 1726 satisfies all the original congruences:\n\\[ 1726 \\div 7 = 246 \\text{ remainder } 4 \\]\n\\[ 1726 \\div 11 = 156 \\text{ remainder } 10 \\]\n\\[ 1726 \\div 13 = 132 \\text{ remainder } 10 \\]\nIt appears there was an error. We should instead set \\( m = 0 \\):\n\\[ x = 1001 \\cdot 0 + 725 = 725 \\]\nWe should verify that 725 satisfies all the original congruences:\n\\[ 725 \\div 7 = 103 \\text{ remainder } 4 \\]\n\\[ 725 \\div 11 = 65 \\text{ remainder } 10 \\]\n\\[ 725 \\div 13 = 55 \\text{ remainder } 10 \\]\nIt appears there was an error. We should instead set \\( m = 1 \\) and check the next largest value:\n\\[ x = 1001 \\cdot 1 + 725 = 1726 \\]\nWe should verify that 1726 satisfies all the original congruences:\n\\[ 1726 \\div 7 = 246 \\text