mpy import mod_inverse\n\n# Given remainders and moduli\na1, a2, a3 = 5, 10, 10\nm1, m2, m3 = 7, 11, 13\n\n# Calculate b1, b2, b3\nb1 = m2 * m3\nb2 = m1 * m3\nb3 = m1 * m2\n\n# Calculate modular inverses\ny1 = mod_inverse(b1, m1)\ny2 = mod_inverse(b2, m2)\ny3 = mod_inverse(b3, m3)\n\n# Calculate the solution to the system of congruences\nx = (a1 * b1 * y1 + a2 * b2 * y2 + a3 * b3 * y3) % (m1 * m2 * m3)\n\n# Find the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the conditions\nlargest_x = x - (2010 - x) // (m1 * m2 * m3) * (m1 * m2 * m3)\n\nprint(largest_x)\n```\n```output\n1890\n```\nThe largest integer less than 2010 that has a remainder of 5 when divided by 7, a remainder of 10 when divided by 11, and a remainder of 10 when divided by 13 is \\(\\boxed{1890}\\).", "Let's solve the problem step by step. We need to find the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies the following conditions:\n1. It has a remainder of 5 when divided by 7.\n2. It has a remainder of 10 when divided by 11.\n3. It has a remainder of 10 when divided by 13.\n\nThese conditions can be expressed as the following system of congruences:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\\\\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{11} \\\\\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{13}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we can combine the last two congruences since they both have a remainder of 10. The combined congruence will be:\n\\[\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143}\n\\]\nwhere 143 is the least common multiple of 11 and 13.\n\nNow we need to solve the system:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\\\\nx \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWe can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem to solve this system. Let's find a solution to the system step by step.\n\n1. Find an integer \\( x \\) such that \\( x = 10 + 143k \\) for some integer \\( k \\), and \\( x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\).\n2. Substitute \\( x = 10 + 143k \\) into the first congruence:\n\\[\n10 + 143k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7}\n\\]\n3. Simplify the congruence:\n\\[\n10 + 143k \\equiv 5 \