. This gives us:\n\\[ x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143} \\]\nsince 143 is the least common multiple (LCM) of 11 and 13.\n\nNow we need to find a number that satisfies both \\( x \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\) and \\( x \\equiv 10 \\pmod{143} \\). We can use the Chinese Remainder Theorem (CRT) to solve this system of congruences. Let's start by finding a solution to these congruences.\n\nWe can write \\( x = 143k + 10 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). We need to find \\( k \\) such that:\n\\[ 143k + 10 \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[ 143k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSince \\( 143 \\equiv 6 \\pmod{7} \\), we can rewrite this as:\n\\[ 6k \\equiv -5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSince \\(-5 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7}\\), we get:\n\\[ 6k \\equiv 2 \\pmod{7} \\]\nMultiplying both sides by the modular inverse of 6 modulo 7 (which is 6, since \\(6 \\cdot 6 \\equiv 36 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{7}\\)), we get:\n\\[ k \\equiv 2 \\cdot 6 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 12 \\pmod{7} \\]\n\\[ k \\equiv 5 \\pmod{7} \\]\nSo, \\( k = 7m + 5 \\) for some integer \\( m \\). Substituting this back into the equation \\( x = 143k + 10 \\), we get:\n\\[ x = 143(7m + 5) + 10 \\]\n\\[ x = 1001m + 715 + 10 \\]\n\\[ x = 1001m + 725 \\]\nWe need the largest \\( x \\) less than 2010, so we set:\n\\[ 1001m + 725 < 2010 \\]\n\\[ 1001m < 1285 \\]\n\\[ m < \\frac{1285}{1001} \\approx 1.28 \\]\nThe largest integer \\( m \\) that satisfies this inequality is \\( m = 1 \\). So, substituting \\( m = 1 \\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[ x = 1001 \\cdot 1 + 725 = 1726 \\]\nSo the largest integer less than 2010 that satisfies all the conditions is 1726. Let's verify this using Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\n# Function to check if a number satisfies all the given conditions\r\ndef satisfies_conditions(x):\r\n    return (x % 7 == 5) and (x % 11 == 10) and (x % 13 == 10)\r\n\r\n# Find the largest number less than 2010 that satisfies the conditions\r\nfor x in range(2009, 0, -1):\r\n    if satisfies_conditions(x):\r