erstand the recursive function \\( f(n) \\) and then find the sum \\( f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1985) \\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ f(n + 1) = (-1)^{n + 1} n - 2f(n) \\]\nwith the initial condition \\( f(1) = f(1986) \\).\n\nLet's break down the problem step-by-step:\n\n1. **Determine the pattern of \\( f(n) \\):**\n   We can start by calculating the first few terms of the sequence to identify any patterns.\n\n2. **Calculate \\( f(n) \\) for small values of \\( n \\):**\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   f(2) &= (-1)^2 \\cdot 1 - 2f(1) = 1 - 2f(1) \\\\\n   f(3) &= (-1)^3 \\cdot 2 - 2f(2) = -2 - 2(1 - 2f(1)) = -2 - 2 + 4f(1) = -4 + 4f(1) \\\\\n   f(4) &= (-1)^4 \\cdot 3 - 2f(3) = 3 - 2(-4 + 4f(1)) = 3 + 8 - 8f(1) = 11 - 8f(1) \\\\\n   f(5) &= (-1)^5 \\cdot 4 - 2f(4) = -4 - 2(11 - 8f(1)) = -4 - 22 + 16f(1) = -26 + 16f(1) \\\\\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Identify the pattern:**\n   From the calculations, we can see that the terms alternate between expressions involving \\( f(1) \\) and constants. We need to find a general form for \\( f(n) \\).\n\n4. **Use the condition \\( f(1) = f(1986) \\):**\n   We can use the recursive relation to express \\( f(1986) \\) in terms of \\( f(1) \\) and then solve for \\( f(1) \\).\n\n5. **Sum the series:**\n   Once we have the general form of \\( f(n) \\), we can sum the series \\( f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + \\dots + f(1985) \\).\n\nLet's implement this in Python using sympy to find the solution.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the initial condition and the recursive function\nf = sp.Function('f')\nn = sp.symbols('n', integer=True)\n\n# Define the recursive relation\nrecurrence = sp.Eq(f(n + 1), (-1)**(n + 1) * n - 2 * f(n))\n\n# Solve the recurrence relation for f(n)\ngeneral_solution = sp.rsolve(recurrence, f(n), {f(1): sp.symbols('C')})\n\n# Find the value of C using the condition f(1) = f(1986)\nC = sp.symbols('C')\nf_1986 = general_solution.subs(n, 1986)\nC_value = sp.solve(sp.Eq(C, f_1986), C)[0]\n\n# Substitute the value