wer in the simplest form. Do not round your answer.-dialog\nA certain type of bacteria doubles its population every hour. At midnight, there are $5000$ bacteria. The population of the bacteria is $128000$ at $3$ p.m. of the same day. Find the population of the bacteria at noon of the same day.-dialog\nA rectangle has a length of $10$ and a width of $8$. The rectangle is cut into $10$ congruent rectangles. Find the sum of all possible perimeters of the congruent rectangles.-dialog\nThe numbers $1$ to $10$ are written on a blackboard. You can choose any two numbers on the blackboard, erase them, and write down their difference instead. For example, if you choose $4$ and $5$, you erase them and write down $1$ on the blackboard. After this process is repeated $9$ times, there will be only one number left on the blackboard. Find the sum of all possible numbers that can be left on the blackboard.-dialog\nA fair coin is tossed $10$ times. Find the probability that there are at least two consecutive heads. (Express your answer in the simplest form.-dialog\nA circle is inscribed in a square. A point $P$ is chosen at random inside the square. Find the probability that $P$ is inside the circle. (Express your answer in terms of $\\pi$.-dialog\nA fair six-sided die is rolled repeatedly until a six is rolled. Find the expected number of rolls.-dialog\nA fair coin is flipped $10$ times. Find the probability that there are exactly $3$ heads. (Express your answer as a common fraction.-dialog\nA fair six-sided die is rolled. Find the probability that the number rolled is a prime number. (Express your answer as a common fraction.-dialog\nA fair six-sided die is rolled twice. Find the probability that the sum of the numbers rolled is $7$. (Express your answer as a common fraction.-dialog\nA fair six-sided die is rolled twice. Find the probability that the product of the numbers rolled is $12$. (Express your answer as a common fraction.-dialog\nA fair six-sided die is rolled twice. Find