o dice showing a number other than 1 and the rest showing 1 can be calculated using the binomial probability formula:\n\\[ P(X = k) = \\binom{n}{k} p^k (1-p)^{n-k} \\]\nwhere $n$ is the number of trials (dice), $k$ is the number of successes (dice showing a number other than 1), $p$ is the probability of success on a single trial, and $\\binom{n}{k}$ is the binomial coefficient.\n\nIn this case, $k = 2$, $p = \\frac{5}{6}$, and the probability of exactly two dice showing a number other than 1 is:\n\\[ \\binom{n}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} = \\frac{25}{216} \\]\nThis can be simplified to:\n\\[ \\binom{n}{2} \\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)^2 \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} = \\frac{25}{216} \\]\n\\[ \\binom{n}{2} \\left(\\frac{25}{36}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{n-2} = \\frac{25}{216} \\]\n\\[ \\binom{n}{2} \\left(\\frac{25}{36}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{6^{n-2}}\\right) = \\frac{25}{216} \\]\n\\[ \\binom{n}{2} \\left(\\frac{25}{36}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{6^{n-2}}\\right) = \\frac{25}{216} \\]\n\\[ \\binom{n}{2} \\left(\\frac{1}{36 \\cdot 6^{n-2}}\\right) = \\frac{1}{216} \\]\n\\[ \\binom{n}{2} = \\frac{36 \\cdot 6^{n-2}}{216} \\]\n\\[ \\binom{n}{2} = \\frac{6^{n-2}}{6} \\]\n\\[ \\binom{n}{2} = 6^{n-3} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} = 6^{n-3} \\]\nWe can now test values of $n$ to find which one satisfies this equation. Let's start with $n = 3$ and go upwards.\n\nIf $n = 3$:\n\\[ \\frac{3 \\cdot 2}{2} = 6^{3-3} \\]\n\\[ 3 = 1 \\]\nThis is not true.\n\nIf $n = 4$:\n\\[ \\frac{4 \\cdot 3}{2} = 6^{4-3} \\]\n\\[ 6 = 6 \\]\nThis is true. So, the value of $n$ is 4. Let's confirm this using Python code.\n```python\nfrom math import comb\n\n# Function to check the probability\ndef check_probability(n):\n    p = 5 / 6\n    q = 1 / 6\n    prob = comb(n, 2) * (p ** 2) * (q ** (n - 2))\n    return prob\n\n# Iterate to find the correct n\nfor n in range(3, 10):  # We start from 3 and go up to 10 for safety\n    if abs(check_probability(n)