of \\(17 \\pmod{83}\\), we need to find an integer \\(x\\) such that \\(17x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{83}\\). This is equivalent to finding \\(x\\) such that \\(17x - 83k = 1\\) for some integer \\(k\\). This is a linear Diophantine equation, and we can solve it using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nThe steps are as follows:\n\n1. Use the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 17 and 83.\n2. Use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express the gcd as a linear combination of 17 and 83.\n3. If the gcd is 1, the coefficient of 17 in that linear combination will be the modular inverse of 17 modulo 83.\n\nLet's start with the Euclidean Algorithm to find the gcd:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n83 &= 17 \\cdot 4 + 15, \\\\\n17 &= 15 \\cdot 1 + 2, \\\\\n15 &= 2 \\cdot 7 + 1, \\\\\n2 &= 1 \\cdot 2 + 0.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nThe gcd of 17 and 83 is 1, so the modular inverse exists. Now we use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a linear combination of 17 and 83:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n1 &= 15 - 2 \\cdot 7, \\\\\n2 &= 17 - 15 \\cdot 1, \\\\\n15 &= 83 - 17 \\cdot 4.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting back, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n1 &= 15 - 2 \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= 15 - (17 - 15 \\cdot 1) \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= 15 - 17 \\cdot 7 + 15 \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= 15 \\cdot 8 - 17 \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= (83 - 17 \\cdot 4) \\cdot 8 - 17 \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= 83 \\cdot 8 - 17 \\cdot 32 - 17 \\cdot 7 \\\\\n&= 83 \\cdot 8 - 17 \\cdot 39.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nSo, we have \\(1 = 83 \\cdot 8 - 17 \\cdot 39\\), which means \\(-39 \\cdot 17 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{83}\\). Since we need a positive residue, we add 83 to \\(-39\\) to get \\(44\\). Therefore, the modular inverse of 17 modulo 83 is 44.\n\nLet's verify this using Python code.\n```python\ndef extended_gcd(a, b):\n    if a == 0:\n        return b, 0, 1\n    gcd, x1, y1 = extended_gcd(b % a, a)\n    x = y1 - (b // a) * x1\n    y = x1\n    return gcd, x, y\n\na = 17\nb = 83\ngcd, x, y = extended_gcd(a, b)\nmod_inverse = x % b  # Ensure the result is in the range from 0 to 82\nprint(mod_inverse)\n```\n```output\n44\n```\nThe modular inverse of \\(17 \\pmod{83}\\) is \\(\\boxed{44}\\).", "To find the modular inverse of 17 modulo 83, we need to find an integer \\( x \\) such that \\( 17x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{83} \\). This is equivalent to finding \\( x \\) such that \\( 17x = 83k + 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). This can be solved using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm, which finds solutions to equations of the form \\( ax + by = \\gcd(a, b) \\).\n\nHere, \\( a = 17 \\) and \\( b = 83 \\). Since 17 and 83 are coprime (i.e., \\(\\gcd(17, 83) = 1\\)), there exist integers \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) such that \\( 17x + 83y = 1 \\). The value of \\( x \\) will be the modular inverse of 17 modulo 83.\n\nLet's use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to find \\( x \\):\n\n1. Compute the gcd of 17 and 83 using the Euclidean Algorithm.\n2. Use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express the gcd as a linear combination of 17 and 83.\n\nWe start with the division steps of the Euclidean Algorithm:\n\\[ 83 = 17 \\cdot 4 + 15 \\]\n\\[ 17 = 15 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\]\n\\[ 15 = 2 \\cdot 7 + 1 \\]\n\\[ 2 = 1 \\cdot 2 + 0 \\]\n\nThe gcd is 1, as expected. Now we use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a linear combination of 17 and 83:\n\\[ 1 = 15 - 2 \\cdot 7 \\]\n\\[ 1 = 15 - (17 - 15 \\cdot 1) \\cdot 7 = 15 \\cdot 8 - 17 \\cdot 7 \\]\n\\[ 1 = (83 - 17 \\cdot 4) \\cdot 8 - 17 \\cdot 7 = 83 \\cdot 8 - 17 \\cdot 39 \\]\n\nSo, we have \\( 17 \\cdot (-39) + 83 \\cdot 8 = 1 \\). This means that the modular inverse of 17 modulo 83 is \\( -39 \\). Since we need a residue from 0 to 82, we add 83 to \\(-39\\) to get a positive value:\n\\[ -39 + 83 = 44 \\]\n\nThus, the modular inverse of 17 modulo 83 is \\( 44 \\). Let's verify this using Python code to ensure the result is accurate.\n```python\ndef extended_gcd(a, b):\n    if a == 0:\n        return b, 0, 1\n    gcd, x1, y1 = extended_gcd(b % a, a)\n    x = y1 - (b 