olve for \\(u\\):\n\n\\[u \\equiv 23 \\cdot 43 \\pmod{50}.\\]\n\nLet's calculate \\(23 \\cdot 43 \\mod 50\\):\n\n\\[23 \\cdot 43 = 989,\\]\n\\[989 \\mod 50 = 39.\\]\n\nSo, one solution to the congruence \\(7u \\equiv 23 \\pmod{50}\\) is \\(u \\equiv 39 \\pmod{50}\\). This means that the general solution to the original congruence \\(14u \\equiv 46 \\pmod{100}\\) is:\n\n\\[u = 39 + 50k,\\]\nwhere \\(k\\) is an integer.\n\nThe two smallest positive integer solutions are obtained by setting \\(k = 0\\) and \\(k = 1\\):\n\n\\[u_1 = 39,\\]\n\\[u_2 = 39 + 50 = 89.\\]\n\nThe average of these two solutions is:\n\n\\[\\frac{39 + 89}{2} = \\frac{128}{2} = 64.\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Function to find the modular inverse using Extended Euclidean Algorithm\ndef modular_inverse(a, m):\n    m0, x0, x1 = m, 0, 1\n    if m == 1:\n        return 0\n    while a > 1:\n        # q is quotient\n        q = a // m\n        m, a = a % m, m\n        x0, x1 = x1 - q * x0, x0\n    # Make x1 positive\n    if x1 < 0:\n        x1 += m0\n    return x1\n\n# Given values\na = 14\nb = 46\nm = 100\n\n# Simplify the congruence by dividing by gcd(14, 100) = 2\na_simplified = a // 2\nb_simplified = b // 2\nm_simplified = m // 2\n\n# Find the modular inverse of a_simplified modulo m_simplified\ninverse = modular_inverse(a_simplified, m_simplified)\n\n# Solve for u\nu = (b_simplified * inverse) % m_simplified\n\n# The two smallest positive integer solutions\nu1 = u\nu2 = u + m_simplified\n\n# Calculate the average\naverage = (u1 + u2) // 2\nprint(average)\n```\n```output\n64\n```\nThe average of the two smallest positive integer solutions to the congruence \\(14u \\equiv 46 \\pmod{100}\\) is \\(\\boxed{64}\\).", "\\emph{Two} positive integer solutions mean that the solutions are both positive integers. To solve the congruence \\(14u \\equiv 46 \\pmod{100}\\), we need to find the integer solutions for \\(u\\). This can be done by finding the modular inverse of 14 modulo 100