\n```\n```output\n15.975\n```\nThe map distance corresponding to a real-life distance of 53.25 miles is 15.975 inches. To express this as a fraction, we need to convert 15.975 to a fraction.\n\nFirst, we can express 15.975 as a fraction by recognizing that $15.975 = \\frac{15975}{1000}$. We can then simplify this fraction by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 15975 and 1000.\n\nLet's use Python to simplify the fraction.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Numerator and denominator\nnumerator = 15975\ndenominator = 1000\n\n# Find the greatest common divisor (GCD)\ngcd = math.gcd(numerator, denominator)\n\n# Simplify the fraction\nsimplified_numerator = numerator // gcd\nsimplified_denominator = denominator // gcd\n\nsimplified_fraction = (simplified_numerator, simplified_denominator)\nprint(simplified_fraction)\n```\n```output\n(639, 40)\n```\nThe simplified fraction for 15.975 is $\\frac{639}{40}$.\n\nTherefore, the distance between the buildings on the map is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{639}{40}}\\) inches.", "To solve this problem, we need to determine the scale of the map and then use that scale to find the distance on the map that corresponds to a real-life distance of 53.25 miles.\n\nThe scale of the map is given as 3 inches on the map equating to 10 miles in real life. This means that for every 10 miles in real life, the distance on the map is 3 inches. We can express this as a ratio:\n\n\\[ \\text{Scale} = \\frac{3 \\text{ inches}}{10 \\text{ miles}} \\]\n\nTo find the distance on the map that corresponds to 53.25 miles in real life, we can set up a proportion:\n\n\\[ \\frac{3 \\text{ inches}}{10 \\text{ miles}} = \\frac{x \\text{ inches}}{53.25 \\text{ miles}} \\]\n\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{3 \\text{ inches} \\times 53.25 \\text{ miles}}{10 \\text{ miles}} \\]\n\nLet's calculate this using Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Given values\nreal_distance = 53.25  # miles\nmap_scale = 3  # inches per 10 miles\n\n# Calculate the distance on the map\nmap_distance = (map_scale * real_distance) / 10\nprint(map_distance)\n```\n```output\n15.975\n```\nThe distance between the buildings on the map is \\( 15.975 \\) inches. To express this as a fraction, we recognize that \\( 15.975 \\) is the same as \\( \\frac{15975}{1000} \\). We can simplify this fraction by finding the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 15975 and 1000.\n\nLet's simplify the fraction \\(\\frac{15975}{1000}\\) using Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Numerator and denominator\nnumerator = 15975\ndenominator = 1000\n\n# Find the greatest common divisor\ngcd = math.gcd(numerator, denominator)\n\n# Simplify the fraction\nsimplified_numerator = numerator // gcd\nsimplified_denominator = denominator // gcd\n\nsimplified_fraction = (simplified_numerator, simplified_denominator)\nprint(simplified_fraction)\n```\n```output\n(639, 40)\n```\nThe simplified fraction for \\( 15.975 \\) is \\( \\frac{639}{40} \\).\n\nTherefore, the distance between the buildings on the map is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{639}{40}}\\) inches.", 'On a particular map, $3$ inches on the map equates to $10$ miles in real life. If you know that the real life distance between two buildings on the map is $53.25$ miles, what would the distance between the buildings be (in inches) on the map, expressed as a fraction?  \\boxed{}.  \\boxed{}Assistant\nOn a particular map, $3$ inches on the map equates to $10$ miles in real life. If you know that the real life distance between two buildings on the map is $53.25$ miles, what would the distance between the buildings be (in inches) on the map, expressed as a fraction?  \\boxed{}Assistant\nOn a particular map, $3$ inches on the map equates to $10$ miles in real life. If you know that the real life distance between two buildings on the map is $53.25$ miles, what would the distance between the buildings be (in inches) on the map, expressed as a fraction?  \\boxed{}Assistant\nOn a particular map, $3$ inches on the map equates to $10$ miles in real life. I