o we can write \\( 3 = f(-2) + 9 \\).\n   - Solving for \\( f(-2) \\), we get \\( f(-2) = 3 - 9 = -6 \\).\n\n3. **Use the property of odd functions to find \\( f(2) \\):**\n   - Since \\( f(x) \\) is an odd function, \\( f(-2) = -f(2) \\).\n   - We found that \\( f(-2) = -6 \\), so \\( -6 = -f(2) \\).\n   - Solving for \\( f(2) \\), we get \\( f(2) = 6 \\).\n\nLet's implement this in Python using sympy to verify our steps.\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the function f(x) as an odd function\nf = sp.Function('f')\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Given that f(x) is an odd function, so f(-x) = -f(x)\n# We know g(x) = f(x) + 9 and g(-2) = 3\ng = f(x) + 9\n\n# Substitute x = -2 in g(x)\ng_at_neg2 = g.subs(x, -2)\n# We know g(-2) = 3, so we can solve for f(-2)\nf_neg2 = sp.solve(g_at_neg2 - 3, f(-2))[0]\n\n# Since f(x) is an odd function, f(-2) = -f(2)\nf_2 = -f_neg2\n\nprint(f_2)\n```\n```output\n6\n```\nThe value of \\( f(2) \\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).\n\nThe solution set of the inequality $2x - x^2 > 0$ is (\u3000\u3000)\n\nA: $(-\\infty, 2)$  \nB: $(-\\infty, 0) \\cup (2, +\\infty)$  \nC: $(2, +\\infty)$  \nD: $(0, 2)$\nTo solve the inequality \\(2x - x^2 > 0\\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Factor the quadratic expression on the left-hand side of the inequality.\n2. Determine the roots of the equation \\(2x - x^2 = 0\\).\n3. Use the roots to test the intervals and determine where the inequality \\(2x - x^2 > 0\\) holds true.\n\nLet's start by factoring the quadratic expression:\n\n\\[2x - x^2 = x(2 - x)\\]\n\nThe roots of the equation \\(x(2 - x) = 0\\) are \\(x = 0\\) and \\(x = 2\\). These roots divide the real number line into three intervals: \\((-\\infty, 0)\\), \\((0, 2)\\), and \\((2, +\\infty)\\). We need to test each interval to determine", "First, we need to determine the value of $\\lceil{\\sqrt{20}}\\rceil$. The ceiling function, denoted by $\\lceil x \\rceil$, returns the smallest integer greater than or equal to $x$. The square root of 20 is appr