: Volume of a Cylinder\n\n3. **Calculate the volume of the cylinder:**\n   The formula for the volume \\( V \\) of a cylinder is:\n   \\[\n   V = \\pi r^2 h\n   \\]\n   Given the radius \\( r = 4 \\) inches and the height \\( h = 10 \\) inches, we substitute:\n   \\[\n   V = \\pi (4)^2 (10) = \\pi (16) (10) = 160\\pi\n   \\]\n   Using \\( \\pi \\approx 3.14 \\):\n   \\[\n   V \\approx 160 \\times 3.14 = 502.4\n   \\]\n   So, the volume of the cylinder is approximately \\(\\boxed{502.4}\\) cubic inches.\n\n### Problem 4: Volume of a Pyramid\n\n4. **Calculate the volume of the pyramid:**\n   The formula for the volume \\( V \\) of a pyramid is:\n   \\[\n   V = \\frac{1}{3} \\text{Base Area} \\times \\text{Height}\n   \\]\n   Given the base is a square with side length \\( s = 6 \\) inches and the height \\( h = 8 \\) inches, we first find the base area:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Base Area} = s^2 = 6^2 = 36 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n   Now, substitute into the volume formula:\n   \\[\n   V = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 36 \\times 8 = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 288 = 96\n   \\]\n   So, the volume of the pyramid is \\(\\boxed{96}\\) cubic inches.\n\n### Problem 5: Volume of a Cube\n\n5. **Determine the side length of the cube:**\n   The sphere is inscribed in the cube, meaning the diameter of the sphere is equal to the side length of the cube. The diameter of the sphere is twice the radius:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Diameter} = 2 \\times 3 = 6 \\text{ inches}\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the side length of the cube is 6 inches.\n\n6. **Calculate the volume of the cube:**\n   The formula for the volume \\( V \\) of a cube is:\n   \\[\n   V = s^3\n   \\]\n   Given the side length \\( s = 6 \\) inches, we substitute:\n   \\[\n   V = 6^3 = 216\n   \\]\n   So, the volume of the cube is \\(\\boxed{216}\\) cubic inches.\n\nI\'ve come across a research paper titled "On the number of representations of', 'A steel sphere with a 3-inch radius is made by removing metal from the corners of a cube that has the 