inder:**\n   The problem states that the sphere is inscribed in a cylinder whose height and radius both equal the diameter of the sphere. Therefore, the height \\(h\\) and the radius \\(r_{\\text{cylinder}}\\) of the cylinder are both 6 inches.\n   \n   The volume \\(V\\) of a cylinder with radius \\(r\\) and height \\(h\\) is given by the formula:\n   \\[\n   V = \\pi r^2 h\n   \\]\n   Given the radius \\(r_{\\text{cylinder}} = 6\\) inches and height \\(h = 6\\) inches, the volume of the cylinder is:\n   \\[\n   V_{\\text{cylinder}} = \\pi (6)^2 \\cdot 6 = \\pi \\cdot 36 \\cdot 6 = 216\\pi \\text{ cubic inches}\n   \\]\n   \n   The ratio of the volume of the sphere to the volume of the cylinder is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Ratio} = \\frac{V_{\\text{sphere}}}{V_{\\text{cylinder}}} = \\frac{36\\pi}{216\\pi} = \\frac{36}{216} = \\frac{1}{6}\n   \\]\n\nLet's confirm these calculations with Python code.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Volume of the sphere\nradius_sphere = 3\nvolume_sphere = (4/3) * math.pi * (radius_sphere**3)\n\n# Side length of the cube\nside_length_cube = 2 * radius_sphere\nvolume_cube = side_length_cube**3\n\n# Volume of the cylinder\nradius_cylinder = side_length_cube\nheight_cylinder = side_length_cube\nvolume_cylinder = math.pi * (radius_cylinder**2) * height_cylinder\n\n# Ratio of the volume of the sphere to the volume of the cylinder\nratio = volume_sphere / volume_cylinder\n\nprint(((volume_sphere, volume_cube, ratio)))\n```\n```output\n(113.09733552923254, 216, 0.16666666666666666)\n```\nThe calculations confirm our reasoning:\n\n1. The volume of the sphere is \\(36\\pi \\approx 113.10\\) cubic inches.\n2. The volume of the cube is \\(216\\) cubic inches.\n3. The ratio of the volume of the sphere to the volume of the cylinder is \\(\\frac{1}{6} \\approx 0.1667\\).\n\nThus, the final answers are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{216}\n\\]\nfor the volume of the cube, and\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\n\\]\nfor the ratio of the volume of the sphere to the volume of the cyli