t itertools\nimport math\n\n# Factors of 66\nfactors = [1, -1, 2, -2, 3, -3, 6, -6, 11, -11, 22, -22, 33, -33, 66, -66]\n\n# Function to calculate |a_{n-1}|\ndef calculate_a_n_minus_1(roots):\n    return abs(sum(roots) * 2)\n\n# Find all possible sets of distinct factors of 66\npossible_sets = []\nfor r in range(1, len(factors) + 1):\n    for combination in itertools.combinations(factors, r):\n        if math.prod(combination) == 66:\n            possible_sets.append(combination)\n\n# Calculate |a_{n-1}| for each set and find the minimum value\nmin_value = float('inf')\nfor roots in possible_sets:\n    a_n_minus_1 = calculate_a_n_minus_1(roots)\n    if a_n_minus_1 < min_value:\n        min_value = a_n_minus_1\n\nprint(min_value)\n```\n```output\n22\n```\nThe least possible value of \\( |a_{n-1}| \\) for the polynomial \\( f(x) = 2x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + 66 \\) with integer coefficients and distinct integer roots is \\(\\boxed{22}\\).", "Given the polynomial \\( f(x) = 2x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + 66 \\) with integer coefficients and distinct integer roots, we need to find the least possible value of \\( |a_{n-1}| \\).\n\nFirst, let's denote the roots of the polynomial by \\( r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n \\). Since the polynomial has integer coefficients and its roots are distinct integers, we can express the polynomial as:\n\\[ f(x) = 2(x - r_1)(x - r_2) \\cdots (x - r_n) \\]\n\nThe constant term \\( a_0 \\) of the polynomial is given by:\n\\[ a_0 = 2(-r_1)(-r_2) \\cdots (-r_n) = 2r_1r_2 \\cdots r_n = 66 \\]\n\nSo, we need to find distinct integers \\( r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n \\) such that their product is \\( \\frac{66}{2} = 33 \\). The integer factorizations of 33 are:\n\\[ 33 = 1 \\cdot 33 \\]\n\\[ 33 = -1 \\cdot -33 \\]\n\\[ 33 = 3 \\cdot 11 \\]\n\\[ 33 = -3 \\cdot -11 \\]\n\nSince we need distinct roots, the possible sets of roots are \\( \\{1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11\\} \\) or any subset of these that multiply to 33. We need to find the combination that minimizes \\( |a_{n-1}| \\).\n\nThe coefficient \\( a_{n-1} \\) is given by:\n\\[ a_{n-1} = -2 \\sum_{i=1}^n r_i \\]\n\nLet's check the possible combinations of roots and calculate \\( |a_{n-1}| \\):\n\n1. If the roots are \\( \\{1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11\\} \\):\n\\[ a_{n-1} = -2(1 - 1 + 3 - 3 + 11 - 11) = -2 \\cdot 0 = 0 \\]\nSo, \\( |a_{n-1}| = 0 \\).\n\n2. If the roots are \\( \\{1, 3, 11\\} \\):\n\\[ a_{n-1} = -2(1 + 3 + 11) = -2 \\cdot 15 = -30 \\]\nSo, \\( |a_{n-1}| = 30 \\).\n\n3. If the roots are \\( \\{-1, -3, -11\\} \\):\n\\[ a_{n-1} = -2(-1 - 3 - 11) = -2 \\cdot -15 = 30 \\]\nSo, \\( |a_{n-1}| = 30 \\).\n\n4. If the roots are \\( \\{1, -3, -11\\} \\):\n\\[ a_{n-1} = -2(1 - 3 - 11) = -2 \\cdot -13 = 26 \\]\nSo, \\( |a_{n-1}| = 26 \\).\n\n5. If the roots are \\( \\{-1, 3, 11\\} \\):\n\\[ a_{n-1} = -2(-1 + 3 + 11) = -2 \\cdot 13 = -26 \\]\nSo, \\( |a_{n-1}| = 26 \\).\n\nThe least possible value of \\( |a_{n-1}| \\) is \\( 0 \\). Let's confirm this with Python code.\n```python\n# Function to calculate |a_{n-1}| given the roots\r\ndef calculate_a_n_minus_1(roots):\r\n    return abs(-2 * sum(roots))\r\n\r\n# Possible sets of roots that multiply to 33\r\npossible_roots = [\r\n    [1, -1, 3, -3, 11, -11],\r\n    [1, 3, 11],\r\n    [-1, -3, -11],\r\n    [1, -3, -11],\r\n    [-1, 3, 11]\r\n]\r\n\r\n# Calculate |a_{n-1}| for each set of roots\r\na_n_minus_1_values = [calculate_a_n_minus_1(roots) for roots in possible_roots]\r\n\r\n# Find the minimum value of |a_{n-1}|\r\n", "To solve this problem, we need to consider the properties of the polynomial and its roots. Given that the polynomial $f(x) = 2x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_2x^2 + a_1x + 66$ has integer coefficients and its roots are distinct integers, we can use Vieta's formulas to relate the coefficients of the polynomial to the sums and products of its roots.\n\nLet the roots of the polynomial be $r_1, r_2, \\ldots, r_n$. By Vieta's formulas, we know that:\n- The sum of the roots taken one at a time (with the polynomial's leading coefficient factored out) is $-\\frac{a_{n-1}}{a_n} = -\\frac{a_{n-1}}{2}$. So, $a_{n-1} = -2(r_1 + r_2 + \\cdots + r_n)$.\n- The product of the roots (with the polynomial's leading coefficient factored out) is $\\frac{a_0}{a_n} = \\frac{66}{2} = 33$. So, $r_1 r_2 \\cdots r_n = 33$.\n\nWe need to find the distinct integer roots whose product is 33. The possible factorizations of 33 are:\n- $33 = 33 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-1) \\cdot (-1)$\n- $33 = 11 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-1)$\n- $33 = 33 \\cdot (-1)$\n- $33 = 11 \\cdot 3$\n- $33 = 33 \\cdot 1$\n\nSince we need distinct integer roots, the possible sets of roots are $\\{33, 1, -1\\}$, $\\{11, 3, 1, -1\\}$, and $\\{33, -1\\}$, $\\{11, 3\\}$. We need to find the configuration that gives the smallest possible value for $|a_{n-1}|$.\n\nLet's calculate $|a_{n-1}|$ for each configuration:\n1. For roots $\\{33, 1, -1\\}$, we have $a_{n-1} = -2(33 + 1 - 1) = -2 \\cdot 33 = -66$, so $|a_{n-1}| = 66$.\n2. For roots $\\{11, 3, 1, -1\\}$, we have $a_{n-1} = -2(11 + 3 + 1 - 1) = -2 \\cdot 14 = -28$, so $|a_{n-1}| = 28$.\n3. For roots $\\{33, -1\\}$, we have $a_{n-1} = -2(33 - 1) = -2 \\cdot 32 = -64$, so $|a_{n-1}| = 64$.\n4. For roots $\\{11, 3\\}$, we have $a_{n-1} = -2(11 + 3) = -2 \\cdot 14 = -28$, so $|a_{n-1}| = 28$.\n\nThe smallest possible value of $|a_{n-1}|$ is 28. Therefore, the answer is $\\boxed{28}$.\n\nLet's verify this with Python code to ensure the calculations are correct.\n```python\n# Define the possible sets of roots and calculate |a_{n-1}| for each set\nroots_sets = [\n    [33, 1, -1],\n    [11, 3, 1, -1],\n    [33, -1],\n    [11, 3]\n]\n\n# Calculate |a_{n-1}| for each set of roots\na_n_minus_1_abs_values = []\nfor roots in roots_sets:\n    a_n_minus_1 = -2 * sum(roots)\n    a_n_minus_1_abs_values.append(abs(a_n_minus_1))\n\n# Find the minimum value of |a_{n-1}|\nmin_a_n_minus_1_abs_value = min(a_n_minus_1_abs_values)\nprint(min_a_n_minus_1_abs_value)\n```", 